(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["brahmavidya"]={"slug":"brahmavidya","name":"Brahmavidyā","deva":"ब्रह्मविद्या","note":"","intro":["A minor Upaniṣad (110 verses in the longer recension) of the <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</strong>, classed as the <strong>40th</strong> of the 108 Upaniṣads in the Muktikā canon and counted among the twenty <strong>Yoga Upaniṣads</strong>. Its core subject is the <strong>Praṇava-Haṃsa-vidyā</strong> — the path of liberation through meditation on <em>Oṃ</em> (in its three <em>mātrā</em>-s and a fourth <em>ardha-mātrā</em>) coupled with the <em>Haṃsaḥ / So'haṃ</em> breath-mantra (the <em>Ajapā-gāyatrī</em>).","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (`brahmavidya.itx`, encoded and proofread by Sunder Hattangadi, last update 16 Feb 2000). Cross-checked against T.R.S. Ayyangar &amp; Pandit S. Subrahmanya Śāstrī, <em>The Yoga Upaniṣads — Translated into English on the basis of the Commentary of Śrī Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</em> (Adyar Library, 1938; rev. G. Srinivasa Murti 1952)."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Brahma-Vidyā Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<strong>ब्रह्मविद्योपनिषत्</strong> — <em>\"The Upaniṣad of the Knowledge of Brahman\"</em>","A minor Upaniṣad (110 verses in the longer recension) of the <strong>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</strong>, classed as the <strong>40th</strong> of the 108 Upaniṣads in the Muktikā canon and counted among the twenty <strong>Yoga Upaniṣads</strong>. Its core subject is the <strong>Praṇava-Haṃsa-vidyā</strong> — the path of liberation through meditation on <em>Oṃ</em> (in its three <em>mātrā</em>-s and a fourth <em>ardha-mātrā</em>) coupled with the <em>Haṃsaḥ / So'haṃ</em> breath-mantra (the <em>Ajapā-gāyatrī</em>).","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (`brahmavidya.itx`, encoded and proofread by Sunder Hattangadi, last update 16 Feb 2000). Cross-checked against T.R.S. Ayyangar &amp; Pandit S. Subrahmanya Śāstrī, <em>The Yoga Upaniṣads — Translated into English on the basis of the Commentary of Śrī Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</em> (Adyar Library, 1938; rev. G. Srinivasa Murti 1952)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Manuscript variations at a glance","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Edition / source</td><td>Verses</td><td>Veda</td><td>Notes</td></tr><tr><td>Sanskritdocuments.org (Hattangadi)</td><td>110</td><td>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</td><td>The longer recension; standard for the Yoga-Upaniṣad corpus. Used as the basis here.</td></tr><tr><td>T.R.S. Ayyangar — Adyar 1938</td><td>110 (groups numbered 1–111; some verses split)</td><td>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</td><td>Follows Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin's Sanskrit <em>bhāṣya</em>. Verse-groups are arranged under nine thematic sub-headings.</td></tr><tr><td>Telugu recension (cited in Muktikā printings)</td><td>110</td><td>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</td><td>Substantively identical to Ayyangar; minor orthographic differences.</td></tr><tr><td>Paul Deussen, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em> (1897)</td><td>14</td><td>Atharvaveda (so titled)</td><td>An abbreviated \"Brahmavidyā\" text covering only the OM-section (≈ vv. 2–13 here); the 96 verses on Haṃsa-vidyā, Guru-bhakti and the <em>aham-graha</em> string are absent.</td></tr><tr><td>Muktikā index (Rāma-Hanumat dialogue)</td><td>—</td><td>Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</td><td>Listed as #40 of 108.</td></tr></table>","<strong>Date.</strong> Gavin Flood (<em>An Introduction to Hinduism</em>, 1996) places the Yoga-Upaniṣads, including Brahma-Vidyā, between <strong>c. 100 BCE – 300 CE</strong>. Mircea Eliade (<em>Yoga: Immortality and Freedom</em>, 1958) treats it as post-Maitrāyaṇī, contemporary with the early didactic <em>Mahābhārata</em> and other Bindu-/Yoga-Upaniṣads."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. <strong>Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</strong> (Rāmacandrendra Sarasvatī, Kāñcī, early 18th c.) — the <em>only</em> surviving classical Sanskrit <em>bhāṣya</em> on the minor Upaniṣads (covers all 108). Pure Advaita Vedānta in the Śaṅkara line. He reads the <em>Praṇava-Haṃsa-vidyā</em> not as a competing yogic technique but as a <em>upāsanā</em> that delivers the same <em>jīvanmukti</em> the major Upaniṣads teach; the closing <em>aham</em>-string (vv. 81–110) is treated as a fifty-one-fold <em>aham-graha-upāsanā</em> (the matching <em>mātṛkā</em> alphabet of fifty-one letters being its outer form).","2. <strong>T.R.S. Ayyangar &amp; Pandit S. Subrahmanya Śāstrī</strong> (Adyar Library, 1938; rev. Murti 1952) — the standard English translation. Closely follows Brahma-Yogin; uses square brackets for Brahma-Yogin's explanatory expansions, round brackets for translator's clarifications. Groups the 110 verses under nine thematic headings (listed below). Conservative, literal, and the working basis for almost all later English versions.","3. <strong>Paul Deussen</strong> (<em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em>, Leipzig 1897; Eng. trans. Bedekar &amp; Palsule, Motilal 1980) — Indological reading. Knew only the 14-verse Atharvaveda recension; treats the text as a pure <em>Oṃ-vidyā</em> parallel to Māṇḍūkya. Does not address vv. 14–110.","4. <strong>Swami Sivananda</strong> (Divine Life Society; <em>Mind: Its Mysteries and Control</em>; <em>Practical Lessons in Yoga</em>; <em>Japa Yoga</em>) — practical <em>sādhanā</em>-oriented. No verse-by-verse <em>bhāṣya</em> on this text, but his Haṃsa-vidyā chapters in <em>Japa Yoga</em> (1947) and <em>Practical Lessons in Yoga</em> (1938) are the clearest modern handbook for vv. 19–25, vv. 53–57 and vv. 65–80. Frames the <em>Ajapā-gāyatrī</em> of v. 79 (21,600 breaths/day) as the practical heart of the Upaniṣad.","5. <strong>Swami Krishnananda</strong> (Divine Life Society lectures, <em>The Mandukya Upanishad</em> corpus, <em>Studies in the Upanishads</em>) — philosophical-existential Advaita. No standalone commentary on this Upaniṣad, but his Pranava-vidyā exegesis in the <em>Māṇḍūkya</em> lectures, and his <em>avasthā-traya / sākṣī</em> analysis, illuminate vv. 1–13, vv. 38–43, and the <em>aham</em>-string vv. 81–110 (which he reads as a <em>Nṛsiṃha-tāpinī</em>-style <em>aham-graha</em>).","<em>On usage below:</em> under each verse, the <strong>Brahma-Yogin</strong> line gives the classical Advaita <em>bhāṣya</em> lens (extracted from Ayyangar's bracketed expansions, which paraphrase Brahma-Yogin); <strong>Ayyangar</strong> captures the translator's literal English and any footnoted variants; <strong>Deussen</strong> is given only where the verse falls within his 14-verse Atharvaveda parallel (otherwise marked \"<em>not in Deussen's recension</em>\"); <strong>Sivananda</strong> is <em>sādhanā</em>-practical and <strong>Krishnananda</strong> is philosophical-Advaita — both are thematic rather than verse-strict, so for verses outside their direct treatment I quote the closest thematic statement they make on the topic."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Ayyangar's nine thematic sections (a structural roadmap)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>§</td><td>Verses</td><td>Theme</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>1–3</td><td>Praṇava as the indicator of Brahman; its three <em>mātrā</em>-s and the <em>ardha-mātrā</em></td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>4–9</td><td>Amplification of the four <em>mātrā</em>-s (A, U, M, ardha): three Vedas, three fires, three worlds, three gods, three lights</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>10–11</td><td>The Suṣumnā <em>nāḍī</em> bursting through the Sun and the 72,000 <em>nāḍī</em>-s</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>12–13</td><td>Liberation by the laya (dissolution) of the OM-sound</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>14–21</td><td>The <em>jīva</em> analysed; the cause of bondage and liberation; the inner <em>Haṃsa</em> with <em>anāhata</em>-dhvani</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>22–26</td><td>Practical Haṃsa-yoga (kumbhaka + <em>nābhi-kanda</em> meditation), siddhis, the centrality of Haṃsa-vidyā</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>27–35</td><td>Guru-bhakti; the renunciation of varṇa-āśrama identifications; the Guru as Hari incarnate</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>36–43</td><td>Finite (<em>sakala</em>) vs infinite (<em>niṣkala</em>) Ātman; the five <em>ātma-sthāna</em>-s; the <em>dehātīta</em> twelve-finger station</td></tr><tr><td>IX</td><td>44–48</td><td>Yoga's secrecy and adhikārī (the qualified disciple)</td></tr><tr><td>X</td><td>49–53</td><td>Stage-independence (gṛhastha, brahmacārī, vānaprastha, bhikṣu); the three Gurus (prompter, awakener, liberator)</td></tr><tr><td>XI</td><td>54–57</td><td>The inner <em>yajña</em> (pratyakṣa-yajana): bindu, the two <em>ayana</em>-s and equinoxes</td></tr><tr><td>XII</td><td>58–80</td><td>The full Haṃsa-yoga: pañca-vāyu, pañca-upa-vāyu, three <em>granthi</em>-s, Kuṇḍalinī, Khecarī, Jālandhara, the <em>Ajapā-gāyatrī</em> of 21,600</td></tr><tr><td>XIII</td><td>81–110</td><td>The <em>aham-graha-upāsanā</em>: fifty-plus self-identifications culminating in \"<em>sa vai puruṣa ucyate</em>\"</td></tr></table>","<em>(Ayyangar's printed sub-headings give twelve such divisions; the table above conflates two short ones.)</em>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (Peace invocation)","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्विनावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["Oṃ saha nāv-avatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai | tejasvi nāv adhītam astu | mā vidviṣāvahai || Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"May He protect us both (teacher and pupil); may He nourish us both; may we both work together with vigour; may our study be illumined; may we not hate. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>(Standard Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda</em> śānti-mantra<em>, identical to the Taittirīya/Kaṭha opening — appropriate to the Upaniṣad's Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda affiliation.)</em>"]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"Maṅgala-śloka (the editor's invocation, before v. 1)","title":"","sa":["स्वाविद्यातत्कार्यजातं यद्विद्यापह्नवं गतम् ।","तद्धंसविद्यानिष्पन्नं रामचन्द्रपदं भजे ॥"],"iast":["sva-avidyā-tat-kārya-jātaṃ yad-vidyāpahnavaṃ gatam |","tad-haṃsa-vidyā-niṣpannaṃ rāma-candra-padaṃ bhaje ||"],"words":[],"lit":"I worship the feet of Rāmacandra (the Guru = Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin's lineage-name <em>Rāmacandrendra</em>) — those feet from which, ripened in the Haṃsa-vidyā, the cluster of effects of self-ignorance has been dispelled.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<em>(This is the Brahma-Yogin / Ayyangar editorial verse, not part of the Upaniṣad proper; it identifies the Haṃsa-vidyā as the engine of self-knowledge before the text begins.)</em>"]}]},{"t":"The 110 verses","u":[{"ref":"The 110 verses","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each verse below is given as: <strong>Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal translation → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest.</strong>","<em>Grammar abbreviations:</em> m./f./n. = masc./fem./neut.; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./pf./pot./impv./ger./inf./caus. = tense/mood/non-finite; tatpur./bahuvr./karmadh./dvandva = compound types.","<em>Verse numbering</em> follows the sanskritdocuments.org / Hattangadi text (1–110). Where Ayyangar's edition groups two verses under a single number, both are given together with a note."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"§ I. Praṇava as the indicator of Brahman (vv. 1–3)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 1","title":"The esoteric truth of Brahma-vidyā","sa":["प्रसादाद्ब्रह्मणस्तस्य विष्णोरद्भुतकर्मणः ।","रहस्यं ब्रह्मविद्याया ध्रुवाग्निं सम्प्रचक्षते ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["prasādād brahmaṇas tasya viṣṇor adbhuta-karmaṇaḥ |","rahasyaṃ brahma-vidyāyā dhruvāgniṃ sampracakṣate || 1 ||"],"words":[["prasādāt","m. abl. sg.","by the grace"],["brahmaṇaḥ","m. gen. sg.","of (the god) Brahmā"],["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","of that"],["viṣṇoḥ","m. gen. sg.","of Viṣṇu"],["adbhuta-karmaṇaḥ","m. gen. sg. (bahuvr.)","of (him) whose deeds are marvellous"],["rahasyam","n. acc. sg.","the secret / esoteric (truth)"],["brahma-vidyāyāḥ","f. gen. sg. (tatpur.)","of Brahma-knowledge"],["dhruva-agnim","m. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","the steady fire"],["sampracakṣate","3 pl. pres. ātm. of sam-pra-cakṣ","they (the seers) declare"]],"lit":"\"By the grace of Brahmā and of Viṣṇu whose deeds are marvellous, they declare the steady fire — the secret (truth) of Brahma-vidyā.\"","para":"Invocation. The teaching's source is <em>prasāda</em> (grace), not unaided effort; its content is a <em>dhruva-agni</em> — the unwavering inner fire that reduces the cotton-mountain of <em>avidyā</em> to ash.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>dhruvāgni</em> = the unmoving <em>jñānāgni</em> in the <em>daharākāśa</em> of the heart; it \"burns up the cotton-pile of saṃsāra\" (Ayyangar's bracketed gloss preserves this image verbatim). The grace of Brahmā/Viṣṇu is the doctrinal authority of the Vedas, not theistic favour."],["Ayyangar","\"By the grace of Brahman and that Viṣṇu who commands the marvellous … the esoteric Truth underlying Brahma-vidyā, they say, is the Brahman.\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his 14-verse recension</em> (his text begins with what is here v. 2)."],["Sivananda","the verse opens his treatment of <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em> by establishing it as a <em>para-vidyā</em> requiring <em>guru-prasāda</em>; not a mere technique."],["Krishnananda","compares to Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.22 — <em>Brahma-vidyā</em> is <em>para-vidyā</em>, \"by which the imperishable is known\"; the <em>dhruvāgni</em> is the <em>sākṣi-caitanya</em> that never flickers."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 2","title":"OM is Brahman; its body, seat and three durations","sa":["ओमित्येकाक्षरं ब्रह्म यदुक्तं ब्रह्मवादिभिः ।","शरीरं तस्य वक्ष्यामि स्थानं कालत्रयं तथा ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ ity ekākṣaraṃ brahma yad uktaṃ brahma-vādibhiḥ |","śarīraṃ tasya vakṣyāmi sthānaṃ kāla-trayaṃ tathā || 2 ||"],"words":[["oṃ","indecl.","Oṃ"],["iti","indecl.","thus"],["eka-akṣaram","n. nom. sg.","the one-syllabled / imperishable"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","(is) Brahman"],["yat","rel. pron. n. nom. sg.","which"],["uktam","n. nom. sg. pp.","(has been) declared"],["brahma-vādibhiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by the expounders-of-Brahman"],["śarīram","n. acc. sg.","the body"],["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","of it (Oṃ)"],["vakṣyāmi","1 sg. fut. of vac","I shall declare"],["sthānam","n. acc. sg.","the seat"],["kāla-trayam","n. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","the three-fold duration"],["tathā","indecl.","also"]],"lit":"\"'Oṃ' — the one imperishable syllable, which has been declared as Brahman by the expounders of Brahman — its body, its seat, and so too its three durations, I shall declare.\"","para":"Programmatic verse. The Upaniṣad's subject is the <em>praṇava</em> analysed under three rubrics: <em>śarīra</em> (its constituents A, U, M, ardha-mātrā), <em>sthāna</em> (where each <em>mātrā</em> \"sits\" in the body / cosmos), and <em>kāla-traya</em> (its three temporal phases — beginning, middle, <em>laya</em>).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","picks up the Māṇḍūkya thread directly — <em>oṃ ity etad akṣaram idaṃ sarvam</em> (Māṇ. 1). The \"three durations\" are the three time-units a <em>mātrā</em> occupies in chanted <em>praṇava</em> (each <em>mātrā</em> = one <em>nimeṣa</em>, with the ardha overshooting all three)."],["Ayyangar","notes the Adyar manuscript's reading <em>bramha</em> (m.c.) at line-end; renders <em>kāla-trayam</em> as \"its three durations\" rather than \"three times\"."],["Deussen","opens his 14-verse text here. Calls the verse the <em>Programmsatz</em> of the <em>Brahma-vidyā</em> in the strict sense — an Upaniṣad of Oṃ."],["Sivananda","the verse is the doctrinal warrant for his teaching that <em>praṇava-japa</em> without <em>praṇava-vicāra</em> (its body/seat/duration) is mechanical and produces no fruit."],["Krishnananda","the <em>ekākṣara</em> designation is itself the <em>mahā-vākya</em> of the Atharvaśiras tradition; \"imperishable\" doubles as \"non-decomposable\" — Oṃ is the irreducible Name of the irreducible Real."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 3","title":"The triadic correspondences of the three mātrās","sa":["तत्र देवास्त्रयः प्रोक्ता लोका वेदास्त्रयोऽग्नयः ।","तिस्रो मात्रार्धमात्रा च त्र्यक्षरस्य शिवस्य तु ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["tatra devās trayaḥ proktā lokā vedās trayo'gnayaḥ |","tisro mātrārdha-mātrā ca tryakṣarasya śivasya tu || 3 ||"],"words":[["tatra","indecl.","therein (in Oṃ)"],["devāḥ","m. nom. pl.","gods"],["trayaḥ","m. nom. pl. num.","three"],["proktāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","(are) declared"],["lokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","worlds (three)"],["vedāḥ","m. nom. pl.","Vedas (three)"],["trayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","three"],["agnayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","fires (three)"],["tisraḥ","f. nom. pl. num.","three (f.)"],["mātrāḥ","f. nom. pl.","mātrās"],["ardha-mātrā","f. nom. sg. (karmadh.)","the half-mātrā"],["ca","conj.","and"],["try-akṣarasya","n. gen. sg. (karmadh.)","of the three-syllabled"],["śivasya","m. gen. sg.","of (that) auspicious one"],["tu","indecl. (emph.)","indeed"]],"lit":"\"Therein (in Oṃ) are declared the three gods, the three worlds, the three Vedas, the three fires, the three <em>mātrā</em>-s and the half-<em>mātrā</em> — (all) of that three-syllabled auspicious (Oṃ).\"","para":"Oṃ contains four triads (gods, worlds, Vedas, fires) keyed to its three sound-units, plus a transcending fourth (the <em>ardha-mātrā</em>). The next six verses unpack each triad slot by slot.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>catur-mātraka</em> analysis is older than the Upaniṣad; the <em>śiva</em> epithet here is not Rudra but the generic \"auspicious\", to avoid premature sectarian collapse — the gods of A/U/M are Brahmā/Viṣṇu/Īśvara (vv. 4–6), and Rudra only enters as the \"fourth\" later (v. 41)."],["Ayyangar","groups vv. 1–3 as a single \"Indication of Brahman by the Praṇava\" sub-section."],["Deussen","parallels the Praśna 5.5–7 <em>eka-mātra/dvi-mātra/tri-mātra</em> meditation; treats v. 3 as the master-table for vv. 4–9."],["Sivananda","uses the triadic structure as the standing template for <em>praṇava-upāsanā</em> — one identifies with each correspondence-set in turn until they merge in the ardha-mātrā."],["Krishnananda","the four-fold mātrā = Māṇḍūkya's <em>jāgrat / svapna / suṣupti / turīya</em>, with Oṃ as their common phoneme; vv. 3–9 are the Brahma-Vidyā's compressed Māṇḍūkya."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ II. The four mātrās expounded (vv. 4–9)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 4","title":"A-kāra: Ṛgveda, Gārhapatya, Earth, Brahmā","sa":["ऋग्वेदो गार्हपत्यं च पृथिवी ब्रह्म एव च ।","आकारस्य शरीरं तु व्याख्यातं ब्रह्मवादिभिः ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["ṛg-vedo gārhapatyaṃ ca pṛthivī brahma eva ca |","ākārasya śarīraṃ tu vyākhyātaṃ brahma-vādibhiḥ || 4 ||"],"words":[["ṛg-vedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Ṛg-veda"],["gārhapatyam","n. nom. sg.","the Gārhapatya (householder's) fire"],["ca","conj.","and"],["pṛthivī","f. nom. sg.","the earth"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","(the god) Brahmā"],["eva ca","indecl. + conj.","and indeed"],["ā-kārasya","m. gen. sg.","of the letter A"],["śarīram","n. nom. sg.","(is) the body"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["vyākhyātam","n. nom. sg. pp.","(it has been) expounded"],["brahma-vādibhiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by the expounders-of-Brahman"]],"lit":"\"The Ṛg-veda and the Gārhapatya fire, the Earth, and Brahmā — (these together) are expounded by the expounders-of-Brahman as the body of the letter A.\"","para":"<em>A</em> = Ṛgveda (speech) + Gārhapatya (the householder's continuously-burning fire) + Pṛthivī (the lowest of the three worlds) + Brahmā (the creator). Four parallel \"lowest / first / outgoing\" slots collapsed into one phoneme.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","A is the <em>udaya</em>-mātrā — the rising phase; hence Earth, Gārhapatya (the fire never lit afresh, always kept burning = beginning), Ṛgveda (the first Veda), and Brahmā (the first deity in the <em>sṛṣṭi-krama</em>)."],["Ayyangar","literal — \"The body of 'A' (of the Praṇava) has been explained by the expounders of the Vedānta as the Ṛg-veda, the Gārhapatya (Fire), the Earth and (God) Brahman.\""],["Deussen","his § 3; he prints a table in <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's</em> identical in substance."],["Sivananda","the practical instruction: while pronouncing <em>A</em>, the <em>bhāvanā</em> travels Earth → Gārhapatya → Ṛgveda → Brahmā, fixing the <em>mātrā</em> in <em>creation</em> aspect."],["Krishnananda","A = <em>jāgrat</em> / Viśva (Māṇḍūkya 3); the four correspondences are <em>adhyātma</em> (Brahmā), <em>adhibhūta</em> (Earth), <em>adhidaiva</em> (Gārhapatya), <em>ādhiśrauta</em> (Ṛgveda)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 5","title":"U-kāra: Yajurveda, Antarikṣa, Dakṣiṇa, Viṣṇu","sa":["यजुर्वेदोऽन्तरिक्षं च दक्षिणाग्निस्तथैव च ।","विष्णुश्च भगवान्देव उकारः परिकीर्तितः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["yajur-vedo'ntarikṣaṃ ca dakṣiṇāgnis tathaiva ca |","viṣṇuś ca bhagavān deva ukāraḥ parikīrtitaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["yajur-vedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Yajur-veda"],["antarikṣam","n. nom. sg.","the mid-region (sky)"],["ca","conj.","and"],["dakṣiṇa-agniḥ","m. nom. sg. (karmadh.)","the southern (sacrificial) fire"],["tathā eva ca","indecl.","likewise indeed"],["viṣṇuḥ","m. nom. sg.","Viṣṇu"],["ca","conj.","and"],["bhagavān","m. nom. sg.","the Lord"],["devaḥ","m. nom. sg.","god"],["u-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the letter U"],["parikīrtitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","is celebrated as"]],"lit":"\"The Yajur-veda, the mid-region, and so the southern fire — and Viṣṇu the Lord-god — are celebrated as the letter U.\"","para":"<em>U</em> = Yajurveda (action) + Antarikṣa (the middle world) + Dakṣiṇāgni (the southern fire, faced during ancestral rites) + Viṣṇu (the <em>sthiti</em>-deity of the trimūrti).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","U is the <em>sthiti</em>-mātrā — the sustained phase; its triad-slots are all \"middle / sustaining\"."],["Ayyangar","literal."],["Deussen","his § 4. He notes the Dakṣiṇāgni is the <em>paitṛka</em> (ancestral) fire — Viṣṇu as preserver of lineage."],["Sivananda","the <em>U</em>-mātrā is the longest in pronounced <em>praṇava</em> — the <em>bhāvanā</em> dwells in Viṣṇu / mid-region throughout sustained breath."],["Krishnananda","U = <em>svapna</em> / Taijasa (Māṇḍūkya 4); the inner-region (antar-ikṣa) is the dream-world's metaphysical correlate."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 6","title":"M-kāra: Sāmaveda, Dyaus, Āhavanīya, Īśvara","sa":["सामवेदस्तथा द्यौश्चाहवनीयस्तथैव च ।","ईश्वरः परमो देवो मकारः परिकीर्तितः ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["sāma-vedas tathā dyauś cāhavanīyas tathaiva ca |","īśvaraḥ paramo devo makāraḥ parikīrtitaḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["sāma-vedaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Sāma-veda"],["tathā","indecl.","so also"],["dyauḥ","f. nom. sg. of div","the heaven"],["ca","conj.","and"],["āhavanīyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Āhavanīya fire (eastern)"],["tathā eva ca","indecl.","likewise indeed"],["īśvaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Lord"],["paramaḥ","m. nom. sg.","supreme"],["devaḥ","m. nom. sg.","god"],["ma-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the letter M"],["parikīrtitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","is celebrated as"]],"lit":"\"The Sāma-veda, likewise the heaven, and likewise the Āhavanīya — Īśvara the supreme god — is celebrated as the letter M.\"","para":"<em>M</em> = Sāma (chant, the highest Veda by mystical rank) + Dyaus (the highest world) + Āhavanīya (the fire of offering, into which the oblation disappears) + Īśvara (the <em>laya</em>-deity).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","M is the <em>laya</em>-mātrā — the dissolution phase, where speech, world, fire and deity all \"return\" upward (Sāma is sung upward; Āhavanīya consumes upward; Dyaus is overhead; Īśvara dissolves all)."],["Ayyangar","literal."],["Deussen","his § 5."],["Sivananda","the closing of the mouth on M is itself a <em>mudrā</em> — the <em>bhāvanā</em> must follow the sound into laya."],["Krishnananda","M = <em>suṣupti</em> / Prājña (Māṇḍūkya 5); causal dissolution, the seed-state in which everything is held undivided."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 7","title":"Seats of A and U in the body","sa":["सूर्यमण्डलमध्येऽथ ह्यकारः शङ्खमध्यगः ।","उकारश्चन्द्रसङ्काशस्तस्य मध्ये व्यवस्थितः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["sūrya-maṇḍala-madhye'tha hy a-kāraḥ śaṅkha-madhya-gaḥ |","u-kāraś candra-saṅkāśas tasya madhye vyavasthitaḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["sūrya-maṇḍala-madhye","n. loc. sg. (tatpur.)","in the middle of the sun's orb"],["atha","indecl.","now / next"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["a-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the letter A"],["śaṅkha-madhya-gaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","(it is) abiding in the middle of the conch (= forehead-region)"],["u-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the letter U"],["candra-saṅkāśaḥ","m. nom. sg.","resembling the moon"],["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","of that (sun's orb)"],["madhye","n. loc. sg.","in the middle"],["vyavasthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","is well-placed"]],"lit":"\"Now indeed <em>A</em> abides in the middle of the sun's orb, in the middle of the <em>śaṅkha</em> (the conch-shaped fore-skull); <em>U</em> — moon-like — is well-placed in the middle of that (orb).\"","para":"Where in the body to <em>visualise</em> each <em>mātrā</em>. <em>A</em> sits in the solar disc in the centre of the forehead/inner conch; <em>U</em>, moon-like, sits at the centre of that very disc — a smaller moon inside a larger sun.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>śaṅkha</em> = the cranial cavity bounded by the temporal bones (the head viewed as a conch). <em>Sūrya-maṇḍala</em> = the <em>ājñā-cakra</em> radiance; the <em>candra</em> of U = the <em>amṛta-bindu</em> shining within it."],["Ayyangar","renders <em>śaṅkha-madhya-ga</em> as \"well placed in the middle of the forehead, in the middle of the region of the Sun\"."],["Deussen","his § 6; reads the sun/moon imagery as inherited from Maitrāyaṇī 6.4."],["Sivananda","the practical visualisation — gaze at the <em>bhrū-madhya</em> with eyes closed, see the bright disc; then locate the smaller moon-bindu at its centre."],["Krishnananda","the sun-orb is <em>Hiraṇyagarbha</em>; the moon-bindu within is <em>jīva-caitanya</em>. The geometry teaches non-difference of <em>jīva</em> and <em>Īśvara</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 8","title":"Seat of M; the three mātrās as moon-sun-fire","sa":["मकारस्त्वग्निसङ्काशो विधूमो विद्युतोपमः ।","तिस्रो मात्रास्तथा ज्ञेया सोमसूर्याग्निरूपिणः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["makāras tv agni-saṅkāśo vidhūmo vidyutopamaḥ |","tisro mātrās tathā jñeyā soma-sūryāgni-rūpiṇaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["ma-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the letter M"],["tu","indecl.","but/and"],["agni-saṅkāśaḥ","m. nom. sg.","fire-like"],["vidhūmaḥ","m. nom. sg.","smokeless"],["vidyut-upamaḥ","m. nom. sg.","comparable to lightning"],["tisraḥ","f. nom. pl.","three"],["mātrāḥ","f. nom. pl.","mātrās"],["tathā","indecl.","thus"],["jñeyāḥ","f. nom. pl. gerundive","are to be known"],["soma-sūrya-agni-rūpiṇaḥ","m. nom. pl. (bahuvr.)","(as) of the form of moon-sun-fire"]],"lit":"\"<em>M</em>, however, is fire-like, smokeless, comparable to lightning. The three <em>mātrā</em>-s are thus to be known as of the form of moon, sun, and fire.\"","para":"Locking the triadic light-imagery: <em>A</em> = sun, <em>U</em> = moon, <em>M</em> = (smokeless) fire / lightning. The three lights of the <em>bhrū-madhya</em> meditation.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the \"smokeless fire\" qualifier is critical — gross fire smokes; this is the <em>ātma-tejas</em> (lightning is its closest visible analogue). Picks up Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.3.6 (<em>nīla-pīta-vidyut</em>)."],["Ayyangar","notes that the order <em>A-U-M</em> maps onto sun-moon-fire only in the cosmological reading; in the cranial reading (v. 7), <em>A</em> was already in the sun and <em>U</em> in the moon, so v. 8 simply completes the triple."],["Deussen","his § 7; calls <em>vidyutopama</em> the \"Blitz-Symbol\" of Brahman, parallel to Kena 4.4."],["Sivananda","the lightning image is the <em>jñāna-jyoti</em> — meditation is to flash, not to smoulder."],["Krishnananda","\"smokeless\" = <em>nirvāsana</em>; fire of pure knowledge is recognised because nothing is being burnt up <em>for</em> it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 9","title":"The ardha-mātrā as the flame-tip","sa":["शिखा तु दीपसङ्काशा तस्मिन्नुपरि वर्तते ।","अर्धमात्रा तथा ज्ञेया प्रणवस्योपरि स्थिता ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["śikhā tu dīpa-saṅkāśā tasminn upari vartate |","ardha-mātrā tathā jñeyā praṇavasyopari sthitā || 9 ||"],"words":[["śikhā","f. nom. sg.","flame / topknot"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["dīpa-saṅkāśā","f. nom. sg.","lamp-like"],["tasmin","pron. m. loc. sg.","upon that (M, the fire)"],["upari","indecl.","above"],["vartate","3 sg. pres. ātm. of vṛt","abides"],["ardha-mātrā","f. nom. sg.","the half-mātrā"],["tathā","indecl.","so also"],["jñeyā","f. nom. sg. gerundive","is to be known"],["praṇavasya","m. gen. sg.","of the Praṇava"],["upari","indecl.","above"],["sthitā","f. nom. sg. pp.","stationed"]],"lit":"\"A flame, lamp-like, abides upon that (fire) above; so also is the half-mātrā to be known, stationed above the Praṇava.\"","para":"The <em>ardha-mātrā</em> (the nasal resonance after M) is the flame-tip <em>over</em> the M-fire: it is <em>of</em> the lamp but <em>above</em> its body — the trans-phonemic residue that is the Upaniṣad's actual target.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this is the <em>turīya</em> of the Māṇḍūkya, transposed into yogic-acoustic form. The ardha is <em>amātra</em> in Māṇḍūkya 12; here it is <em>ardha-mātrā</em> (held in the body but bodiless)."],["Ayyangar","\"even as the flame of a lamp stands over it, so also should the <em>ardha-mātrā</em> be understood as standing over the Praṇava.\""],["Deussen","his § 8; aligns with Māṇḍūkya 12 directly."],["Sivananda","the practical mark — when the chanted <em>m</em> ends and the nasal hum lingers, <em>that</em> lingering is the <em>ardha-mātrā</em>; <em>laya</em> is its further dissolution (v. 12)."],["Krishnananda","the flame <em>belongs to</em> the lamp yet is not <em>the</em> lamp — image of <em>turīya</em>'s relation to the three states: of them, but not them."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ III. The Suṣumnā piercing the Sun and the 72,000 nāḍīs (vv. 10–11)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 10","title":"The subtle nāḍī of the flame-tip","sa":["पद्मसूत्रनिभा सूक्ष्मा शिखा सा दृश्यते परा ।","सा नाडी सूर्यसङ्काशा सूर्यं भित्त्वा तथापरा ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["padma-sūtra-nibhā sūkṣmā śikhā sā dṛśyate parā |","sā nāḍī sūrya-saṅkāśā sūryaṃ bhittvā tathāparā || 10 ||"],"words":[["padma-sūtra-nibhā","f. nom. sg. (karmadh.)","resembling the lotus-stalk's fibre"],["sūkṣmā","f. nom. sg.","subtle"],["śikhā","f. nom. sg.","the flame-tip"],["sā","pron. f. nom. sg.","she / that"],["dṛśyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is seen"],["parā","f. nom. sg.","transcendent"],["sā nāḍī","f. nom. sg.","that channel"],["sūrya-saṅkāśā","f. nom. sg.","sun-like"],["sūryam","m. acc. sg.","the sun"],["bhittvā","ger. of bhid","having pierced"],["tathā aparā","indecl. + f. nom. sg.","and (extending) further"]],"lit":"\"That subtle flame-tip is seen, transcendent, resembling the fibre of a lotus-stalk. That channel, sun-like, having pierced the sun, (extends) yet further.\"","para":"The <em>ardha-mātrā</em>'s \"flame-tip\" is not merely acoustic; it has a corresponding <em>nāḍī</em>-form — as fine as the lotus-fibre, sun-bright, that pierces the inner sun-disc and continues on.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this <em>nāḍī</em> is the <strong>Suṣumnā</strong> (named explicitly in his commentary). The \"sun\" pierced is the <em>sūrya-maṇḍala</em> of v. 7 — i.e., the <em>ājñā-cakra</em> radiance."],["Ayyangar","explicit gloss: \"That transcendent (Suṣumnā) flame is seen to be as subtle as the fibre of the lotus-stalk.\""],["Deussen","his § 9; the lotus-fibre image is Chāndogya 8.6.1."],["Sivananda","practical correlation — <em>praṇava-laya</em> (v. 9) opens the Suṣumnā automatically when the <em>vāyu</em> follows the sound into the <em>brahma-randhra</em>."],["Krishnananda","the <em>padma-sūtra</em> image quantifies subtlety — <em>nāḍī</em> is not anatomical but <em>prāṇa-vāhinī</em>; what flows is not blood but awareness-as-breath."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 11","title":"Bursting the 72,000 nāḍīs","sa":["द्विसप्ततिसहस्राणि नाडीं भित्त्वा च मूर्धनि ।","वरदः सर्वभूतानां सर्वं व्याप्यावतिष्ठति ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["dvi-saptati-sahasrāṇi nāḍīṃ bhittvā ca mūrdhani |","varadaḥ sarva-bhūtānāṃ sarvaṃ vyāpyāvatiṣṭhati || 11 ||"],"words":[["dvi-saptati-sahasrāṇi","n. acc. pl. (dvigu)","seventy-two thousand"],["nāḍīm","f. acc. sg. (collective sg.)","channels"],["bhittvā","ger. of bhid","having pierced"],["ca","conj.","and"],["mūrdhani","n. loc. sg. of mūrdhan","in the head/crown"],["varadaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(it is) the granter-of-boons"],["sarva-bhūtānām","n. gen. pl.","of all beings"],["sarvam","n. acc. sg.","everything"],["vyāpya","ger. of vi-āp","having pervaded"],["avatiṣṭhati","3 sg. pres. of ava-sthā","stands"]],"lit":"\"Having pierced the seventy-two thousand <em>nāḍī</em>-s, (the Suṣumnā) stands in the head, the granter-of-boons to all beings, pervading everything.\"","para":"The Suṣumnā breaches the entire 72,000-fold subtle network, anchoring at the <em>brahma-randhra</em> in the crown. From there it bestows <em>varas</em> (powers / liberation) on all beings and pervades all.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the 72,000 count appears earlier in Praśna 3.6, where each of 101 main nāḍī-s subdivides into 100, then each of those into 72 — totalling 72,72,10,201; here the <em>prathama-vibhāga</em> (first division) only is invoked. The \"boon-granting\" is <em>jīvanmukti</em> itself."],["Ayyangar","\"as if she is the giver of the boons to all beings\" — <em>as if</em>, because the giving is not an act but the standing."],["Deussen","his § 10; treats this as the last verse of the proper <em>Oṃ-vidyā</em> section before the <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em> enters."],["Sivananda","the 72,000 number is the <em>sthāna-kalpana</em> — what the Yogin actually experiences is the <em>daśa-prāṇa-vāha</em> (ten chief channels) and within them the Suṣumnā as central."],["Krishnananda","\"pervading all\" — the <em>nāḍī</em> is the conduit of consciousness; piercing the 72,000 is the dissolution of the <em>kośa</em>-bound jīva-perspective, leaving only the all-pervasive <em>sākṣin</em>."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ IV. Liberation by the laya of the OM-sound (vv. 12–13)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 12","title":"The bell-metal simile","sa":["कांस्यघण्टानिनादस्तु यथा लीयति शान्तये ।","ओङ्कारस्तु तथा योज्यः शान्तये सर्वमिच्छता ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["kāṃsya-ghaṇṭā-ninādas tu yathā līyati śāntaye |","oṅkāras tu tathā yojyaḥ śāntaye sarvam icchatā || 12 ||"],"words":[["kāṃsya-ghaṇṭā-ninādaḥ","m. nom. sg. (tatpur.)","the toll of a bell-metal bell"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["yathā","indecl.","just as"],["līyati","3 sg. pres. of lī","dissolves"],["śāntaye","f. dat. sg.","for (its own) peace / silence"],["oṅkāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Oṃkāra"],["tu tathā","indecl.","just so"],["yojyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive of yuj","is to be applied"],["śāntaye","f. dat. sg.","for peace (= mokṣa)"],["sarvam","n. acc. sg.","the All"],["icchatā","m. instr. sg. pres. part. of iṣ","by one desiring"]],"lit":"\"Just as the toll of a bell-metal bell dissolves into its own silence, just so is the <em>Oṃkāra</em> to be applied for peace by one desiring the All.\"","para":"<em>Praṇava-laya</em> is taught by analogy. A bronze bell's tone naturally decays into stillness; let the chanted Oṃ do the same — and let the chanter follow the sound into that stillness. The fruit is <em>sarvam</em> (all = <em>brahma-bhāva</em>).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the simile mandates <em>prolongation followed by attentive cessation</em> — neither cutting Oṃ off nor letting attention slacken in the decay. The whole <em>Bindu</em>-Upaniṣad corpus rests on this verse."],["Ayyangar","the bracketed expansion specifies <em>sixteen</em> mātrā-units (the <em>ṣoḍaśī</em>) for the long chant, identifying the Praṇava with the cosmic <em>Ṣoḍaśa-kalā-puruṣa</em>."],["Deussen","his § 11; calls this the <em>Schlüsselsatz</em> (key verse) of the <em>Brahma-vidyā</em>."],["Sivananda","the entire chapter on <em>Oṃkāra-japa</em> in <em>Japa Yoga</em> expands this verse; he prescribes 21 long Oṃ-s morning and evening with attention held in the decay."],["Krishnananda","the <em>laya</em> is not the bell going silent but the listener becoming silence; subject-object distinction dissolves with the sound."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 13","title":"The Brahman where sound dissolves","sa":["यस्मिन्विलीयते शब्दस्तत्परं ब्रह्म गीयते ।","धियं हि लीयते ब्रह्म सोऽमृतत्वाय कल्पते ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["yasmin vilīyate śabdas tat paraṃ brahma gīyate |","dhiyaṃ hi līyate brahma so'mṛtatvāya kalpate || 13 ||"],"words":[["yasmin","pron. n. loc. sg.","in which"],["vi-līyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of vi-lī","dissolves"],["śabdaḥ","m. nom. sg.","sound"],["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["param brahma","n. nom. sg.","(is) the supreme Brahman"],["gīyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of gai","is sung (proclaimed)"],["dhiyam","f. acc. sg. of dhī","the intellect"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["līyate","3 sg. pres. ātm.","dissolves (into)"],["brahma","n. nom./acc. sg.","(the) Brahman"],["saḥ","pron. m. nom. sg.","he"],["amṛtatvāya","n. dat. sg.","for immortality"],["kalpate","3 sg. pres. ātm.","is fit / becomes capable"]],"lit":"\"That in which the sound dissolves — <em>that</em> is sung as the supreme Brahman. The intellect, indeed, dissolves into the Brahman; he becomes fit for immortality.\"","para":"Restating v. 12 as ontology: the <em>silence into which sound dies</em> is itself the <em>paraṃ brahma</em>. The <em>dhī</em> that follows the sound into that silence is <em>itself</em> dissolved into Brahman; that dissolution is <em>amṛtatva</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","a strict Advaita <em>paryavasāna</em> — <em>dhī</em> (the intellect, the last residue of agency) is the final offering. The text reads not \"the dhī dissolves into Brahman\" but (in his interpretation) \"the dhī <em>is</em> Brahman the moment it dissolves\" — no two-step ontology."],["Ayyangar","renders <em>amṛtatvāya kalpate</em> as \"makes for that immortal state of becoming one with the Brahman\"."],["Deussen","his § 12; quotes Maitri 6.22 (<em>śabda-brahma-prāptyā paraṃ brahma adhigamyate</em>)."],["Sivananda","the practical formula — <em>Praṇava → Anāhata → Laya → Brahma-bhāva</em>. He marks this verse as the bridge from <em>japa</em> into <em>dhyāna</em> proper."],["Krishnananda","this is the Upaniṣad's <em>mahā-vākya</em>-equivalent: identity is established not by predicating \"tat tvam asi\" but by demonstrating that the <em>dhī</em> which would predicate has itself melted."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ V. The jīva, the cause of bondage and liberation, the inner Haṃsa (vv. 14–21)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 14","title":"The triple jīva","sa":["वायुः प्राणस्तथाकाशस्त्रिविधो जीवसञ्ज्ञकः ।","स जीवः प्राण इत्युक्तो वालाग्रशतकल्पितः ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["vāyuḥ prāṇas tathākāśas tri-vidho jīva-saṃjñakaḥ |","sa jīvaḥ prāṇa ity ukto vālāgra-śata-kalpitaḥ || 14 ||"],"words":[["vāyuḥ","m. nom. sg.","air"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","breath / vital-fire (radiance)"],["tathā ākāśaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","so also ether"],["tri-vidhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","three-fold"],["jīva-saṃjñakaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","named \"jīva\""],["sa jīvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","that jīva"],["prāṇa iti uktaḥ","m. nom. sg.","is said as \"prāṇa\""],["vāla-agra-śata-kalpitaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","imagined as a hundredth-of-the-tip-of-a-hair"]],"lit":"\"Air, breath-radiance, and so ether — three-fold is what is named <em>jīva</em>. That <em>jīva</em>, called <em>prāṇa</em>, is imagined to be (the size of) a hundredth of the tip of a hair.\"","para":"What is conventionally called <em>jīva</em> is in fact a three-fold subtle complex (air-element, prāṇa-radiance, and ether). Its subtle dimension is the Upaniṣadic <em>vālāgra-śata-bhāga</em> (Śvetāśvatara 5.9).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>jīva</em> is not a substance but a <em>trayī-saṃghāta</em> (triple aggregate) of <em>vāyu</em> + <em>teja</em> (prāṇa as fire-radiance) + <em>ākāśa</em> — a yogic restatement of the <em>liṅga-śarīra</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"That which is called the Jīva is of three kinds: the vital air, radiance and so also the Ether.\""],["Deussen","his § 13 (the last of his 14-verse text)."],["Sivananda","the <em>vālāgra-śata</em> size is a meditation-prop, not an anatomical claim — the <em>jīva</em> must be <em>located</em> before it can be <em>transcended</em>."],["Krishnananda","the citation is Śvetāśvatara 5.9 verbatim — the <em>jīva</em> is the <em>infinitesimal</em> of the <em>infinite</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 15","title":"The jīva at the navel as Sun-like Truth","sa":["नाभिस्थाने स्थितं विश्वं शुद्धतत्त्वं सुनिर्मलम् ।","आदित्यमिव दीप्यन्तं रश्मिभिश्चाखिलं शिवम् ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["nābhi-sthāne sthitaṃ viśvaṃ śuddha-tattvaṃ sunirmalam |","ādityam iva dīpyantaṃ raśmibhiś cākhilaṃ śivam || 15 ||"],"words":[["nābhi-sthāne","n. loc. sg. (tatpur.)","in the navel-place"],["sthitam","n. nom. sg. pp.","abiding"],["viśvam","n. nom. sg.","the All"],["śuddha-tattvam","n. nom. sg. (karmadh.)","the pure Truth"],["sunirmalam","n. nom. sg.","utterly stainless"],["ādityam iva","m. acc. sg. + indecl.","like the sun"],["dīpyantam","n. nom. sg. pres. part. of dīp","blazing"],["raśmibhiḥ","m. instr. pl.","with rays"],["ca","conj.","and"],["akhilam","n. nom. sg.","entire"],["śivam","n. nom. sg.","auspicious"]],"lit":"\"Abiding in the navel-place, the All — pure Truth, utterly stainless, blazing like the sun with (its) rays, entire and auspicious (one should know).\"","para":"The \"jīva\" of v. 14, traced to its seat, is found at the navel as a stainless sun-like radiance. This is the locus of the <em>Haṃsa</em> about to be introduced — and it <em>is</em> the All in seed.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>nābhi</em> is the <em>kanda</em> — meeting-point of all <em>nāḍī</em>-s and the seat of the <em>manipūra</em>-fire. The \"All\" abides here in <em>bīja</em>-form."],["Ayyangar","\"(They see the Jīva in the innermost Ātman) partaking of the nature of fire, taking his stand in the region of the navel, the pure Truth unalloyed.\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","the <em>nābhi-kanda</em> meditation is the first lokus for Haṃsa-japa (see vv. 22–23)."],["Krishnananda","the language \"<em>viśvaṃ ... śuddhatattvam</em>\" is the precise Advaita move — the All is not a sum of objects but the unsumable Real."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 16","title":"The unending Sa-Ha japa","sa":["सकारं च हकारं च जीवो जपति सर्वदा ।","नाभिरन्ध्राद्विनिष्क्रान्तं विषयव्याप्तिवर्जितम् ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["sa-kāraṃ ca ha-kāraṃ ca jīvo japati sarvadā |","nābhi-randhrād viniṣkrāntaṃ viṣaya-vyāpti-varjitam || 16 ||"],"words":[["sa-kāram","m. acc. sg.","the letter \"sa\""],["ca","conj.","and"],["ha-kāram","m. acc. sg.","the letter \"ha\""],["ca","conj.","and"],["jīvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the jīva"],["japati","3 sg. pres. of jap","mutters / chants"],["sarvadā","indecl.","always"],["nābhi-randhrāt","n. abl. sg.","from the navel-cavity"],["viniṣkrāntam","n. acc. sg. pp.","(the japa is) outgoing"],["viṣaya-vyāpti-varjitam","n. acc. sg. (karmadh.+bahuvr.)","devoid of contamination by objects"]],"lit":"\"The <em>jīva</em> always mutters 'sa' and 'ha' (i.e., <em>so'haṃ</em>), outgoing from the navel-cavity, devoid of contamination by objects.\"","para":"Every breath is already a japa — exhalation = <em>ha</em>, inhalation = <em>sa</em> — emerging from the navel-cavity. Because breath does not pick up objects, this <em>japa</em> is <em>suddha</em> (object-free) by nature.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this is the <strong>Ajapā-gāyatrī</strong> (declared by name at v. 78). The <em>jīva</em>'s ongoing breath is, unbeknownst to him, the supreme mantra; the entire <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em> is making him <em>aware</em> of what he already does."],["Ayyangar","\"the <em>jīva</em> is always muttering the letters 'Sa' and 'Ha' (in the form of <em>So'haṃ</em>; with the elision of the sibilants <em>S</em> and <em>Ha</em>, it assumes the form of the Praṇava).\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","\"Watch the breath in <em>hamsa-so'ham</em> — every soul does this 21,600 times a day without knowing it. The <em>japa</em> is to know it.\" (<em>Japa Yoga</em>, ch. on Ajapā)."],["Krishnananda","the <em>japa</em> is \"<em>viṣaya-vyāpti-varjita</em>\" — it is the one mental act not contaminated by intentionality; hence its <em>brahma-prāpaka</em> status."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 17","title":"Identity of the jīva and the niṣkala, via five prāṇāyāmas","sa":["तेनेदं निष्कलं विद्यात्क्षीरात्सर्पिर्यथा तथा ।","कारणेनात्मना युक्तः प्राणायामैश्च पञ्चभिः ॥ १७ ॥"],"iast":["tenedaṃ niṣkalaṃ vidyāt kṣīrāt sarpir yathā tathā |","kāraṇenātmanā yuktaḥ prāṇāyāmaiś ca pañcabhiḥ || 17 ||"],"words":[["tena","pron. m. instr. sg.","by that (Haṃsa-japa)"],["idam","pron. n. acc. sg.","this"],["niṣkalam","n. acc. sg.","the partless"],["vidyāt","3 sg. opt. of vid","one should know"],["kṣīrāt","n. abl. sg.","from milk"],["sarpiḥ","n. nom. sg.","ghee"],["yathā tathā","indecl.","just as ... so"],["kāraṇena ātmanā","m. instr. sg. (karmadh.)","with the causal Self"],["yuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","yoked"],["prāṇāyāmaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by the prāṇāyāmas"],["ca","conj.","and"],["pañcabhiḥ","m. instr. pl.","(by) five"]],"lit":"\"By that (Haṃsa-japa) one should know this partless (Brahman) — just as ghee (is known to be one) with milk; yoked with the causal Self by the five <em>prāṇāyāma</em>-s.\"","para":"The Haṃsa-japa makes the <em>niṣkala-brahma</em> recognisable as already-present-in-jīva, just as ghee, though latent, <em>is</em> milk's own substance. The yoking-instrument is the fivefold <em>prāṇāyāma</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the \"five <em>prāṇāyāma</em>-s\" are not five breath-counts but the five <em>aṅgas</em> of inner yoga: <em>prāṇāyāma, pratyāhāra, dhāraṇā, dhyāna, samādhi</em> (Ayyangar's bracketed gloss). This collapses Patañjali's last five aṅgas into a single ladder."],["Ayyangar","\"becomes one with its prime source, the Paramātman, through the five-fold Prāṇāyāma-s (viz., Prāṇāyāma, Pratyāhāra, Dhāraṇā, Dhyāna and Samādhi).\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","milk-ghee is a <em>standard</em> Vedāntic <em>upādāna</em> image; the five-fold reading is taken verbatim into his <em>Sādhanā Tattva</em> schema."],["Krishnananda","\"<em>kṣīrāt sarpir yathā</em>\" — the jīva is not <em>transformed into</em> Brahman but <em>revealed as</em> Brahman; the <em>yoga</em> is recognitional, not productive."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 18","title":"Four kalās + the heart-sphere","sa":["चतुष्कला समायुक्तो भ्राम्यते च हृदिस्थितः ।","गोलकस्तु यदा देहे क्षीरदण्डेन वा हतः ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["catuṣkalā samāyukto bhrāmyate ca hṛdi-sthitaḥ |","golakas tu yadā dehe kṣīra-daṇḍena vā hataḥ || 18 ||"],"words":[["catuṣ-kalā-samāyuktaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","conjoined with the four kalās"],["bhrāmyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of bhram","is whirled"],["ca","conj.","and"],["hṛdi-sthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","seated in the heart"],["golakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","sphere (Suṣumnā-orb)"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["yadā","indecl.","when"],["dehe","m. loc. sg.","in the body"],["kṣīra-daṇḍena","m. instr. sg.","by the milk-shaft"],["vā","conj.","or"],["hataḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of han","struck"]],"lit":"\"(The jīva), conjoined with the four <em>kalā</em>-s and seated in the heart, is whirled (about); and when, in the body, the (Suṣumnā-)sphere is struck by the milk-(white) shaft …\"","para":"While bound to the four <em>avasthā</em>-s (Viśva/Taijasa/Prājña/Turīya — Ayyangar's gloss), the jīva is \"whirled\" in the heart-sphere. Liberation begins the moment the <em>kṣīra-daṇḍa</em> of Suṣumnā-power <em>strikes</em> that sphere open.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the four <em>kalā</em>-s = <em>Viśva-Taijasa-Prājña-Turīya</em> (his bracketed list, preserved by Ayyangar). The <em>golaka</em> is the heart-sphere; <em>kṣīra-daṇḍa</em> is the Suṣumnā's milky-white core."],["Ayyangar","\"engages itself (in the investigation of bondage, liberation and their effects). When the sphere (of the Suṣumnā, shining at the top of the Mūlādhāra and the triangular Svādhiṣṭhāna plexuses) in the (physical) body is pierced through by the Kṣīra-daṇḍa…\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","the four kalās are the four states; <em>bhrāmyate</em> is the experience of waking-dream-deep sleep oscillation that meditation must arrest."],["Krishnananda","note the passive <em>bhrāmyate</em> — the jīva does not <em>whirl</em> but <em>is whirled</em>; agency is already not where it seems to be."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 19","title":"The great bird and the niṣkala state","sa":["एतस्मिन्वसते शीघ्रमविश्रान्तं महाखगः ।","यावन्निश्वसितो जीवस्तावन्निष्कलतां गतः ॥ १९ ॥"],"iast":["etasmin vasate śīghram aviśrāntaṃ mahā-khagaḥ |","yāvan niśvasito jīvas tāvan niṣkalatāṃ gataḥ || 19 ||"],"words":[["etasmin","pron. m. loc. sg.","in this (body)"],["vasate","3 sg. pres. ātm.","dwells"],["śīghram","adv.","swiftly"],["aviśrāntam","adv.","restlessly"],["mahā-khagaḥ","m. nom. sg. (karmadh.)","the great bird (= Haṃsa)"],["yāvat","indecl.","as long as"],["niśvasitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","breathed-out"],["jīvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the jīva"],["tāvat","indecl.","so long"],["niṣkalatām","f. acc. sg.","the partless state"],["gataḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of gam","(he is) gone to / attained"]],"lit":"\"In this (body) the great bird dwells swiftly, restlessly; as long as the jīva (continues to be) breathed-out (= until the final out-breath), so long is the partless state being approached.\"","para":"The <em>Haṃsa</em> (the <em>jīva</em> qua bird) inhabits the body restlessly; yet every out-breath is, simultaneously, a step toward <em>niṣkalatā</em> — the breath is both the prison and the parole.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","\"great bird\" = <em>Haṃsa</em> (<em>ha</em> + <em>sa</em> of v. 16, now personified). <em>Aviśrāntam</em> (restless) is <em>kāmika-saṃsāra</em> — bird-on-twig-of-desire. The compound verb <em>niṣkalatāṃ gataḥ</em> is in present-perfect aspect: <em>with each out-breath one has-just-gone</em> toward the partless."],["Ayyangar","\"the great bird (Haṃsa) takes its residence at once, (taking hold of the ignorance of the Ātman, and abides, moving restlessly therein, as long as the knowledge of the Ātman is attained). With the outgoing of breath (from the body), the jīva attains the state of cessation of diverse aspects.\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","the practical reading: <em>every exhalation is a micro-mokṣa-rehearsal</em>. Practitioners are told to \"die into\" each <em>ha</em>-breath."],["Krishnananda","the imagery is from Muṇḍaka 3.1.1–2 — <em>dvā suparṇā ... sakhāyā</em>; here, the second bird is being identified with the first by the very breath that seemed to keep them apart."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 20","title":"Meditation on the niṣkala in the heart's ether","sa":["नभस्थं निष्कलं ध्यात्वा मुच्यते भवबन्धनात् ।","अनाहतध्वनियुतं हंसं यो वेद हृद्गतम् ॥ २० ॥"],"iast":["nabha-sthaṃ niṣkalaṃ dhyātvā mucyate bhava-bandhanāt |","anāhata-dhvani-yutaṃ haṃsaṃ yo veda hṛd-gatam || 20 ||"],"words":[["nabha-stham","n. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","abiding in (the heart's) ether"],["niṣkalam","n. acc. sg.","the partless"],["dhyātvā","ger. of dhyai","having meditated upon"],["mucyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of muc","is released"],["bhava-bandhanāt","n. abl. sg.","from the bondage of becoming"],["anāhata-dhvani-yutam","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","conjoined with the unstruck sound"],["haṃsam","m. acc. sg.","the Haṃsa"],["yaḥ","rel. pron. m. nom. sg.","who(ever)"],["veda","3 sg. pf. of vid","knows"],["hṛd-gatam","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","gone-to / seated in the heart"]],"lit":"\"Having meditated on the partless (Brahman) abiding in the (heart-)ether, one is released from the bondage of becoming. Whoever knows the Haṃsa, seated in the heart, conjoined with the <em>anāhata</em>-sound … (cont. v. 21).\"","para":"Two parallel formulas of liberation: (a) meditation on <em>niṣkala</em> in <em>daharākāśa</em> releases from <em>saṃsāra</em>; (b) knowing the <em>Haṃsa-with-anāhata</em>-sound at the heart accomplishes the same. The latter is the Upaniṣad's signature path.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>anāhata-dhvani</em> is <em>para-nāda</em> — the soundless sound; recognising the Haṃsa as already <em>conjoined</em> with this <em>nāda</em> is liberation itself."],["Ayyangar","\"He is sung as the Haṃsa, who knows the Haṃsa seated in his heart, with the sound of the <em>anāhata</em> Ether, the self-luminous consciousness and Bliss.\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","the <em>anāhata-nāda</em> hierarchy (cini, ciñcini, ghaṇṭā, śaṅkha, tantrī, tāla, veṇu, mṛdaṅga, bherī, megha — from <em>Haṃsa-Upaniṣad</em> / <em>Nādabindu</em>) is mapped onto this verse."],["Krishnananda","<em>anāhata</em> = <em>aja</em> (unborn); the unstruck sound is unborn awareness recognising itself; \"knowing the Haṃsa with the <em>anāhata</em>-dhvani\" is therefore not two acts but one."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 21","title":"Self-luminous Bliss; the Yogin who gives up recaka/pūraka","sa":["स्वप्रकाशचिदानन्दं स हंस इति गीयते ।","रेचकं पूरकं मुक्त्वा कुम्भकेन स्थितः सुधीः ॥ २१ ॥"],"iast":["sva-prakāśa-cidānandaṃ sa haṃsa iti gīyate |","recakaṃ pūrakaṃ muktvā kumbhakena sthitaḥ sudhīḥ || 21 ||"],"words":[["sva-prakāśa-cidānandam","m. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","self-luminous Consciousness-Bliss"],["saḥ","pron. m. nom. sg.","he"],["haṃsaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) Haṃsa"],["iti gīyate","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","is so sung / celebrated"],["recakam","m. acc. sg.","exhalation"],["pūrakam","m. acc. sg.","inhalation"],["muktvā","ger. of muc","having abandoned"],["kumbhakena","m. instr. sg.","by retention"],["sthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","standing"],["sudhīḥ","m. nom. sg.","the wise one"]],"lit":"\"He, the self-luminous Consciousness-Bliss, is sung as Haṃsa. Having abandoned exhalation and inhalation, the wise one is established in <em>kumbhaka</em>.\"","para":"Closes the previous verse (the <em>Haṃsa-knower</em> = the <em>sva-prakāśa-cit-ānanda</em>), then introduces the <em>kumbhaka</em>-yogin's practical step: drop <em>recaka/pūraka</em> and rest in <em>kumbhaka</em> — the breathless state where the Haṃsa is recognised.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>sva-prakāśa</em> is the <em>jñāna-lakṣaṇa</em> (knowledge-mark) and <em>cidānanda</em> the <em>svarūpa-lakṣaṇa</em> (essence-mark) of the Haṃsa — the same Vedāntic two-fold definition as for Brahman."],["Ayyangar","\"After giving up Recaka and Pūraka, the Yogin with a sharp intellect, taking his stand on the Kumbhaka …\""],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","the technical instruction begins here — <em>kevala-kumbhaka</em> (effortless retention) as the natural fruit, not the imposed cause, of Haṃsa-recognition."],["Krishnananda","\"<em>muktvā</em>\" — <em>let go</em>, not <em>fight off</em>; the dropping is recognitional, the <em>kumbhaka</em> is what remains when grasping ceases."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ VI. Practical Haṃsa-yoga, siddhis, the centrality of Haṃsa-vidyā (vv. 22–26)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 22","title":"Prāṇa-Apāna equalised at the nābhi-kanda","sa":["नाभिकन्दे समौ कृत्वा प्राणापानौ समाहितः ।","मस्तकस्थामृतास्वादं पीत्वा ध्यानेन सादरम् ॥ २२ ॥"],"iast":["nābhi-kande samau kṛtvā prāṇāpānau samāhitaḥ |","mastaka-sthāmṛtāsvādaṃ pītvā dhyānena sādaram || 22 ||"],"words":[["nābhi-kande","m. loc. sg. (tatpur.)","at the navel-bulb (Suṣumnā-root)"],["samau","m. acc. du.","(the two) equal"],["kṛtvā","ger. of kṛ","having made"],["prāṇa-apānau","m. acc. du. (dvandva)","the prāṇa and apāna"],["samāhitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","well-collected"],["mastaka-stha-amṛta-āsvādam","m. acc. sg. (tatpur.)","the savour of the nectar stationed in the head"],["pītvā","ger. of pā","having drunk"],["dhyānena","n. instr. sg.","by meditation"],["sādaram","adv.","reverently"]],"lit":"\"Having made <em>prāṇa</em> and <em>apāna</em> equal at the navel-bulb, well-collected, having drunk by meditation, reverently, the savour of the nectar stationed in the head, …\"","para":"Practical posture-instruction: balance prāṇa and apāna at the <em>kanda</em> (mūlādhāra-svādhiṣṭhāna junction); then \"drink\" by meditation the <em>amṛta</em> falling from the <em>brahma-randhra</em>. Sentence continues in v. 23.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>samau kṛtvā</em> is the <em>prāṇāpāna-yoga</em> of Bhagavad-Gītā 4.29 — <em>apāne juhvati prāṇam …</em>. The \"nectar in the head\" is <em>candra-kalā</em> (the <em>bindu</em> of the <em>soma-cakra</em>)."],["Ayyangar","\"Eagerly drinking the savoury nectar stationed in the head, in a state of mental abstraction, and bathing the great god…\""],["Sivananda","the <em>amṛta-svāda</em> is <em>Khecarī-mudrā</em>'s reward — when the palate is \"drunk\", the tongue tastes the descending <em>bindu</em> literally as sweet."],["Krishnananda","the <em>samau</em> is the key — yoga is <em>equalisation</em>, not domination of one over the other."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 23","title":"Bathing the lamp-formed Mahādeva and chanting Haṃsa Haṃsa","sa":["दीपाकारं महादेवं ज्वलन्तं नाभिमध्यमे ।","अभिषिच्यामृतेनैव हंस हंसेति यो जपेत् ॥ २३ ॥"],"iast":["dīpākāraṃ mahādevaṃ jvalantaṃ nābhi-madhyame |","abhiṣicyāmṛtenaiva haṃsa haṃseti yo japet || 23 ||"],"words":[["dīpa-ākāram","m. acc. sg.","lamp-shaped"],["mahā-devam","m. acc. sg.","the great god"],["jvalantam","m. acc. sg. pres. part.","blazing"],["nābhi-madhyame","m. loc. sg.","in the middle of the navel"],["abhiṣicya","ger. of abhi-ṣic","having anointed"],["amṛtena","n. instr. sg.","with the nectar"],["eva","indecl.","indeed"],["haṃsa haṃsa iti","mantra","\"Haṃsa Haṃsa\""],["yaḥ","rel. m. nom. sg.","who(ever)"],["japet","3 sg. opt. of jap","should chant"]],"lit":"\"(He who) anoints the lamp-shaped Great God blazing in the middle of the navel with that very nectar, and chants 'Haṃsa Haṃsa' — …\"","para":"The downward-flowing <em>amṛta</em> of v. 22 is poured (by <em>bhāvanā</em>) over the lamp-flame in the navel — the <em>jaṭhara-Mahādeva</em>. While this anointing is held, the practitioner chants <em>Haṃsa Haṃsa</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>dīpākāra Mahādeva</em> is the <em>manipūra-jyoti</em> — Śiva-as-flame at the navel. <em>Abhiṣeka</em> of fire by nectar = union of <em>agni</em> and <em>soma</em> = inner <em>yajña</em>."],["Ayyangar","literal; explicates the <em>anointing</em> as a <em>bhāvanā</em> (mental ritual)."],["Sivananda","practical mark — when <em>Khecarī</em> + <em>Haṃsa-japa</em> are sustained, an audible \"buzz\" arises in the <em>bhrū-madhya</em> simultaneous with sweetness at palate; that is the verse's experiential signature."],["Krishnananda","\"Mahādeva blazing in the navel\" — divinity is not located in the head alone; the <em>jvalat</em> in the navel is no less Mahādeva than the <em>jyotis</em> in the brow."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 24","title":"Freedom from disease, death, decay; daily siddhi-practice","sa":["जरामरणरोगादि न तस्य भुवि विद्यते ।","एवं दिने दिने कुर्यादणिमादिविभूतये ॥ २४ ॥"],"iast":["jarā-maraṇa-rogādi na tasya bhuvi vidyate |","evaṃ dine dine kuryād aṇimādi-vibhūtaye || 24 ||"],"words":[["jarā-maraṇa-rogādi","n. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","dotage-death-disease etc."],["na","indecl.","not"],["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","of/for him"],["bhuvi","f. loc. sg. of bhū","on earth"],["vidyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","exists"],["evam","indecl.","thus"],["dine dine","n. loc. sg. (vīpsā)","day by day"],["kuryāt","3 sg. opt. of kṛ","one should do"],["aṇimādi-vibhūtaye","f. dat. sg.","for the powers beginning with aṇimā"]],"lit":"\"For him there is no dotage, death, disease, etc., on earth. Thus, day by day, one should practise — for the (siddhi)-powers beginning with aṇimā.\"","para":"Fruit + injunction. Fruit: jarā-maraṇa-roga cease. Injunction: practise <em>daily</em>, aiming at the <em>aṣṭa-siddhi</em>-s (aṇimā etc.) — though the next verse will subordinate these to <em>Īśvaratva</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the eight siddhi-s are <em>aṇimā, mahimā, laghimā, garimā, prāpti, prākāmya, vaśitva, īśitva</em> — but Brahma-Yogin reads <em>aṇimādi-vibhūtaye</em> as <em>Īśvaratva-vibhūti</em> (the very next verse's word), warning the disciple not to settle for lesser powers."],["Ayyangar","\"Every day should he practise thus, for the attainment of mystic powers, such as attenuation.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>jarā-maraṇa</em> claim is read symbolically — freedom from <em>psychological</em> aging and <em>spiritual</em> death; not a deathlessness-of-body promise. He cites this verse in <em>Practice of Yoga</em> as a corrective to siddhi-hunters."],["Krishnananda","the verse-pair v. 24–25 illustrates the <em>artha-vāda</em> / <em>vidhi</em> structure — the siddhi promise is <em>praśaṃsā</em> to motivate the <em>abhyāsa</em>, not the <em>phala</em> proper."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 25","title":"Īśvaratva by constant practice; many paths, one Haṃsa","sa":["ईश्वरत्वमवाप्नोति सदाभ्यासरतः पुमान् ।","बहवो नैकमार्गेण प्राप्ता नित्यत्वमागताः ॥ २५ ॥"],"iast":["īśvaratvam avāpnoti sadābhyāsa-rataḥ pumān |","bahavo naika-mārgeṇa prāptā nityatvam āgatāḥ || 25 ||"],"words":[["īśvaratvam","n. acc. sg.","Īśvara-hood"],["avāpnoti","3 sg. pres. of ava-āp","attains"],["sadā-abhyāsa-rataḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","(he) delighting in constant practice"],["pumān","m. nom. sg. of puṃs","the man"],["bahavaḥ","m. nom. pl.","many"],["na-eka-mārgeṇa","m. instr. sg. (karmadh.)","by not-one (= many a) path"],["prāptāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","(have) reached"],["nityatvam","n. acc. sg.","eternity"],["āgatāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","(have) come (to)"]],"lit":"\"The man who delights in constant practice attains Īśvara-hood. Many, by various paths, have reached and arrived at eternity.\"","para":"The promised fruit is upgraded: not just siddhi but <em>Īśvaratva</em>. The verse magnanimously notes \"many paths\" — but v. 26 immediately narrows: in the absence of <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em>, none of them works.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","\"<em>bahavaḥ</em>\" gestures inclusively to <em>karma-jñāna-bhakti-yoga-mārga</em>-s; \"<em>nityatva</em>\" is <em>brahma-bhāva</em>, not <em>deva-yāna</em> longevity."],["Ayyangar","\"Many have, by resorting to various paths, attained the state of perpetual existence.\""],["Sivananda","cites this verse for <em>mārga-samaccaya</em> (many-paths-converge) ecumenicism within his <em>Yoga of Synthesis</em>."],["Krishnananda","the universalism is real but conditioned by the next verse — without <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em> (i.e., without <em>prāṇa-vidyā</em> of <em>some</em> form), no path completes itself."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 26","title":"Without Haṃsa-vidyā, no means to eternity","sa":["हंसविद्यामृते लोके नास्ति नित्यत्वसाधनम् ।","यो ददाति महाविद्यां हंसाख्यां पारमेश्वरीम् ॥ २६ ॥"],"iast":["haṃsa-vidyām ṛte loke nāsti nityatva-sādhanam |","yo dadāti mahā-vidyāṃ haṃsākhyāṃ pārameśvarīm || 26 ||"],"words":[["haṃsa-vidyām","f. acc. sg.","the Haṃsa-knowledge"],["ṛte","indecl. (postposn.)","apart from"],["loke","m. loc. sg.","in (this) world"],["na asti","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. of as","there is not"],["nityatva-sādhanam","n. nom. sg.","means-to-eternity"],["yaḥ","rel. m. nom. sg.","who(ever)"],["dadāti","3 sg. pres. of dā","gives"],["mahā-vidyām","f. acc. sg.","the great knowledge"],["haṃsa-ākhyām","f. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","named Haṃsa"],["pārameśvarīm","f. acc. sg.","of the supreme Lord"]],"lit":"\"Apart from the Haṃsa-vidyā, in this world there is no means to eternity. He who gives this great knowledge, named Haṃsa, (which is) of the supreme Lord —\"","para":"Doctrinal exclusivity (after v. 25's inclusivism): every path <em>requires</em> the Haṃsa-vidyā as its inner engine. The man who imparts it is hereby framed as the Guru in the next verses.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","\"the <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em>\" is shorthand for the <em>prāṇa-vāyu</em> yoking-to-<em>manas</em> yoking-to-<em>ātman</em> — without which <em>jñāna</em> remains theoretical."],["Ayyangar","\"In a world where the Haṃsa-vidyā has ceased to exist, there is no means to be adopted for the state of perpetual existence.\""],["Sivananda","not sectarian; \"Haṃsa-vidyā\" stands for <em>any</em> <em>ajapā</em>-watching of breath — present in every contemplative tradition."],["Krishnananda","the verse can be read as Vedānta-internal: the breath-conscious <em>upāsaka</em> alone is <em>adhikāri</em> for <em>jñāna</em> — without the <em>prāṇa-niścala</em>, <em>manas-niścala</em> is impossible."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 27","title":"Service to the Haṃsa-vidyā Guru","sa":["तस्य दास्यं सदा कुर्यात्प्रज्ञया परया सह ।","शुभं वाऽशुभमन्यद्वा यदुक्तं गुरुणा भुवि ॥ २७ ॥"],"iast":["tasya dāsyaṃ sadā kuryāt prajñayā parayā saha |","śubhaṃ vā'śubham anyad vā yad uktaṃ guruṇā bhuvi || 27 ||"],"words":[["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","to him (the Guru)"],["dāsyam","n. acc. sg.","service"],["sadā","indecl.","always"],["kuryāt","3 sg. opt.","one should do"],["prajñayā parayā saha","f. instr. sg. + indecl.","along with supreme wisdom"],["śubham vā aśubham anyad vā","n. acc. sg.","whether auspicious, inauspicious, or other"],["yat","rel. n. nom. sg.","whatever"],["uktam","n. nom. sg. pp.","(is) said"],["guruṇā","m. instr. sg.","by the Guru"],["bhuvi","f. loc. sg.","in the world"]],"lit":"\"To him (the Guru) one should ever render service, along with supreme wisdom — whatever, in the world, has been said by the Guru — whether auspicious or inauspicious or other(wise) — (continued v. 28).\"","para":"<em>Guru-dāsya</em> (service to the Guru) is to be conjoined with <em>parā prajñā</em> — not blind obedience, but obedience inside wisdom. The Guru's word governs irrespective of its outward valence.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>parā prajñā</em> qualifier saves the verse from authoritarianism — service in <em>light</em>, not service in <em>darkness</em>. <em>Aśubha</em> here means \"outwardly inauspicious\", e.g., counter-intuitive instructions, not unethical commands."],["Ayyangar","\"one should always betake to service under him, with superior wisdom.\""],["Sivananda","the verse opens his <em>Guru-Tattva</em> chapters; the <em>parā prajñā</em> condition is non-negotiable."],["Krishnananda","the <em>dāsya</em> is psychological dependence-recognition, not behavioural servitude."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 28","title":"Disciple's contented obedience, fruit of Guru-service","sa":["तत्कुर्यादविचारेण शिष्यः सन्तोषसंयुतः ।","हंसविद्यामिमां लब्ध्वा गुरुशुश्रूषया नरः ॥ २८ ॥"],"iast":["tat kuryād avicāreṇa śiṣyaḥ santoṣa-saṃyutaḥ |","haṃsa-vidyām imāṃ labdhvā guru-śuśrūṣayā naraḥ || 28 ||"],"words":[["tat","pron. n. acc. sg.","that"],["kuryāt","3 sg. opt.","should do"],["avicāreṇa","n. instr. sg.","without questioning"],["śiṣyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the disciple"],["santoṣa-saṃyutaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","endowed with contentment"],["haṃsa-vidyām","f. acc. sg.","the Haṃsa-knowledge"],["imām","pron. f. acc. sg.","this"],["labdhvā","ger. of labh","having obtained"],["guru-śuśrūṣayā","f. instr. sg. (tatpur.)","by service-to-the-Guru"],["naraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the man"]],"lit":"\"That the disciple should do without questioning, endowed with contentment — having obtained this Haṃsa-vidyā by service to the Guru, the man — (cont. v. 29).\"","para":"<em>Avicāreṇa</em> (without questioning) is paired with <em>santoṣa-saṃyutaḥ</em> (in contentment) — Guru-service is not anxious obedience but settled, cheerful trust. The <em>labh</em>-clause shifts focus to the disciple's post-attainment standing.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>avicāreṇa</em> is paired with the previous verse's <em>parayā prajñayā</em> — wisdom <em>precedes</em> the obedience, so no <em>vicāra</em> is needed <em>during</em> it."],["Ayyangar","\"the disciple should carry out with pleasure, without any scruple whatsoever.\""],["Sivananda","his <em>Sādhanā Tattva</em> prescribes \"implicit obedience to the Guru\" — explicitly footnoting this verse."],["Krishnananda","the <em>santoṣa</em> qualifier is critical — discontented obedience generates a future eruption; only <em>santoṣa</em>-saturated service is <em>yoga-anukūla</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 29","title":"Renunciation of body, kin, varṇa-āśrama after Self-realisation","sa":["आत्मानमात्मना साक्षाद्ब्रह्म बुद्ध्वा सुनिश्चलम् ।","देहजात्यादिसम्बन्धान्वर्णाश्रमसमन्वितान् ॥ २९ ॥"],"iast":["ātmānam ātmanā sākṣād brahma buddhvā suniścalam |","deha-jāty-ādi-sambandhān varṇāśrama-samanvitān || 29 ||"],"words":[["ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","the Self"],["ātmanā","m. instr. sg.","by the Self"],["sākṣāt","indecl.","directly"],["brahma","n. acc. sg.","(as) Brahman"],["buddhvā","ger. of budh","having awakened-to / cognised"],["suniścalam","n. acc. sg.","utterly unmoving"],["deha-jāti-ādi-sambandhān","m. acc. pl. (tatpur.)","the body-caste-etc. ties"],["varṇa-āśrama-samanvitān","m. acc. pl. (bahuvr.)","conjoined with varṇa-and-āśrama"]],"lit":"\"Having directly cognised the Self, by the Self, as the utterly-unmoving Brahman — the ties of body, caste, etc., conjoined with the varṇa-s and āśrama-s — (cont. v. 30).\"","para":"Once <em>ātma-sākṣātkāra</em> has occurred (<em>ātmānam ātmanā buddhvā</em>), the <em>deha-jāti-varṇa-āśrama</em> identifications are about to be ruled obsolete (v. 30 supplies the verb).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>ātmānam ātmanā</em> is the technical Vedānta phrase (Gītā 6.20, <em>yatrātmānātmānaṃ paśyann ātmani tuṣyati</em>) — knowing-by-being, not knowing-by-grasping."],["Ayyangar","\"having acquired … the direct knowledge, of his own accord, that the Ātman is no other than the non-fickle Brahman, that person …\""],["Sivananda","the verse warrants <em>jīvanmukti</em>'s social posture — the realised one stands outside <em>varṇa-āśrama</em> without revolt; he simply no longer identifies."],["Krishnananda","<em>deha-jāti</em> and <em>varṇa-āśrama</em> are <em>vyavahāra</em>-categories; their abandonment is not external but recognitional — they cease to apply, are not refused."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 30","title":"Vedas, Śāstras, Guru-bhakti for great excellence","sa":["वेदशास्त्राणि चान्यानि पदपांसुमिव त्यजेत् ।","गुरुभक्तिं सदा कुर्याच्छ्रेयसे भूयसे नरः ॥ ३० ॥"],"iast":["veda-śāstrāṇi cānyāni pada-pāṃsum iva tyajet |","guru-bhaktiṃ sadā kuryāc chreyase bhūyase naraḥ || 30 ||"],"words":[["veda-śāstrāṇi","n. acc. pl.","Vedas-and-śāstras"],["ca anyāni","conj. + n. acc. pl.","and other (texts)"],["pada-pāṃsum iva","m. acc. sg. + indecl.","like the dust of (one's) feet"],["tyajet","3 sg. opt. of tyaj","(he) should renounce"],["guru-bhaktim","f. acc. sg.","devotion-to-the-Guru"],["sadā","indecl.","always"],["kuryāt","3 sg. opt.","should do"],["śreyase bhūyase","n. dat. sg. + n. dat. sg.","for greater good"],["naraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the man"]],"lit":"\"He should renounce the Vedas, the Śāstras, and other (texts) — as the dust of one's feet. For greater good, the man should always offer devotion to the Guru.\"","para":"Closes the chain begun in v. 29. The post-<em>sākṣātkāra</em> one <em>renounces</em> even the scriptures (their work is done — like <em>pada-pāṃsu</em>, foot-dust); <em>guru-bhakti</em> alone is to be continued, for <em>bhūyas</em> (yet greater) good.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>tyāga</em> of the Vedas is not antinomian — it is the <em>sādhana-mukta</em>'s state, parallel to <em>Muṇḍaka</em> 1.2.12 (<em>parīkṣya lokān karma-citān …</em>). The <em>guru-bhakti</em> continuing is <em>not</em> a <em>sādhana</em> either but a <em>svarūpa-lakṣaṇa</em> of <em>jīvanmukti</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"(he should) renounce, as he would the dust of his feet, all relationship with the body, his kith and kin and the like, along with the varṇa-s, āśrama-s, Vedas, śāstras, and all other things.\""],["Sivananda","he reads this verse as a corrective to <em>book-religion</em>; the <em>jñānin</em>'s <em>guru-bhakti</em> is the residue, not the abolition, of reverence."],["Krishnananda","the <em>pāṃsu</em>-image carefully says <em>of-the-foot</em>, not <em>under-foot</em>; the Vedas are not trampled but outgrown."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 31","title":"Guru is Hari incarnate","sa":["गुरुरेव हरिः साक्षान्नान्य इत्यब्रवीच्छ्रुतिः ॥ ३१ ॥"],"iast":["gurur eva hariḥ sākṣān nānya ity abravīc chrutiḥ || 31 ||"],"words":[["guruḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Guru"],["eva","indecl.","alone"],["hariḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) Hari"],["sākṣāt","indecl.","in-person / directly"],["na anyaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","not another"],["iti","indecl.","thus"],["abravīt","3 sg. impf. of brū","said / declared"],["śrutiḥ","f. nom. sg.","the Veda"]],"lit":"\"'The Guru alone is Hari directly, not another' — so declared the Śruti.\"","para":"A half-verse seal (single line, not a couplet) establishing the doctrinal identity of Guru = Hari, on Vedic authority. <em>Sākṣāt</em> — not symbolically.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>śruti</em> cited is the standard <em>Guru-Gītā</em>-style ascription; \"Hari\" stands for the <em>Paramātman</em>-as-saviour aspect, harmonising the Vaiṣṇava and Advaita registers."],["Ayyangar","\"The Guru alone is Hari incarnate: so says the Holy-writ.\""],["Sivananda","the verse appears in his <em>Guru-Tattva</em> as the <em>mukhya-pramāṇa</em> for <em>guru-pāramya</em>."],["Krishnananda","the equation is <em>upāsanā-bhūmika</em>, not literal — the <em>upāsaka</em>'s <em>parame śraddhā</em> in the Guru <em>makes</em> the Guru function as the <em>paramātman</em> for him."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 32","title":"Veda is the supreme means; nothing without sanction","sa":["श्रुत्या यदुक्तं परमार्थमेव तत्संशयो नात्र ततः समस्तम् ।","श्रुत्या विरोधे न भवेत्प्रमाणं भवेदनर्थाय विना प्रमाणम् ॥ ३२ ॥"],"iast":["śrutyā yad uktaṃ paramārtham eva tat saṃśayo nātra tataḥ samastam |","śrutyā virodhe na bhavet pramāṇaṃ bhaved anarthāya vinā pramāṇam || 32 ||"],"words":[["śrutyā","f. instr. sg.","by the Veda"],["yat uktam","rel. + n. nom. sg. pp.","what has been said"],["paramārtham eva","m./n. acc. sg. + indecl.","(is) the supreme end indeed"],["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["saṃśayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","doubt"],["na atra","indecl.","(is) not here"],["tataḥ","indecl.","from it (the Veda)"],["samastam","n. nom. sg.","(flows) all"],["virodhe","m. loc. sg.","in (case of) contradiction"],["na bhavet pramāṇam","3 sg. opt. + n. nom. sg.","it should not be authority"],["bhavet anarthāya","3 sg. opt. + m. dat. sg.","would lead to ruin"],["vinā pramāṇam","indecl. + n. acc. sg.","without (Vedic) authority"]],"lit":"\"What is declared by the Veda is the supreme end indeed; there is no doubt here; from it (flows) the entire (sanctioned). In contradiction to the Veda, (anything) cannot be authority; without (Vedic) authority, (action) would lead to ruin.\"","para":"A <em>pramāṇa-vyavasthā</em>: Veda is the supreme pramāṇa; whatever lacks Vedic sanction is <em>anartha</em>-producing. (This verse, by metre, is <em>upajāti</em> — a meter-shift signalling its formulaic status.)","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the verse is a <em>paribhāṣā</em> (general rule) inserted to forestall antinomian misreading of v. 30 (renunciation of Vedas) — the <em>jñānin</em> outgrows the Vedas <em>as injunctions</em>, but Vedic <em>pramāṇatā</em> remains intact for the seeker."],["Ayyangar","\"What is related by the Veda, that is the supreme end of existence. There is no doubt about it … Anything without sanction will contribute towards utter ruin.\""],["Sivananda","paired with v. 30 — <em>renounce as practitioner, retain as authority</em>."],["Krishnananda","the verse's meter-shift is itself a hermeneutic cue — the upajāti is the <em>prakṛti</em> metre of the <em>prakaraṇa</em> texts, marking a doctrinal stratum."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 33","title":"Sakala (with-the-body) vs niṣkala (bodiless) Brahman","sa":["देहस्थः सकलो ज्ञेयो निष्कलो देहवर्जितः ।","आप्तोपदेशगम्योऽसौ सर्वतः समवस्थितः ॥ ३३ ॥"],"iast":["deha-sthaḥ sakalo jñeyo niṣkalo deha-varjitaḥ |","āptopadeśa-gamyo'sau sarvataḥ samavasthitaḥ || 33 ||"],"words":[["deha-sthaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","(the one) standing-in-the-body"],["sakalaḥ","m. nom. sg.","with-parts"],["jñeyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive","is to be known (as)"],["niṣkalaḥ","m. nom. sg.","partless"],["deha-varjitaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","body-free"],["āpta-upadeśa-gamyaḥ","m. nom. sg. (tatpur.)","reachable by the trustworthy teacher's instruction"],["asau","pron. m. nom. sg.","that one"],["sarvataḥ","indecl.","on all sides"],["samavasthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","stationed alike"]],"lit":"\"What stands in the body is to be known as <em>sakala</em> (with-parts); what is body-free is <em>niṣkala</em> (partless). That (latter) is reachable (only) by the <em>āpta</em>-teacher's instruction; (it stands) stationed alike on all sides.\"","para":"Two-fold typology: <em>sakala</em> = the embodied / phenomenal aspect; <em>niṣkala</em> = the bodiless / noumenal aspect. The <em>niṣkala</em> cannot be reached by inference or scripture-reading alone — only by the <em>āpta</em> (the realised teacher's) <em>upadeśa</em>. It is <em>sarva-vyāpī</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","picks up Śvetāśvatara 6.18 (<em>kalāmātraṃ paraṃ niṣkalam</em>); the verse is the doctrinal kernel of §VIII (vv. 33–43)."],["Ayyangar","\"That which stands in the body has parts. That which is rid of the body is the indivisible (one Brahman).\""],["Sivananda","the <em>sakala</em>-meditation (with-form, <em>saguṇa</em>) is preparatory; the <em>niṣkala</em>-realisation (<em>nirguṇa</em>) is the final stage."],["Krishnananda","the strict logical point — <em>niṣkala</em> is not \"absent of parts\" but <em>prior to</em> the very category of parts; <em>āpta-upadeśa</em> alone bridges the categorial gap."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 34","title":"Haṃsa Haṃsa: identity of Haṃsa with Brahmā-Hari-Śiva","sa":["हंसहंसेति यो ब्रूयाद्धंसो ब्रह्मा हरिः शिवः ।","गुरुवक्त्रात्तु लभ्येत प्रत्यक्षं सर्वतोमुखम् ॥ ३४ ॥"],"iast":["haṃsa haṃseti yo brūyād dhaṃso brahmā hariḥ śivaḥ |","guru-vaktrāt tu labhyeta pratyakṣaṃ sarvato-mukham || 34 ||"],"words":[["haṃsa haṃsa iti","mantra","(chanting) \"Haṃsa Haṃsa\""],["yaḥ","rel. m. nom. sg.","who(ever)"],["brūyāt","3 sg. opt. of brū","should speak"],["haṃsaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa"],["brahmā hariḥ śivaḥ","m. nom. sg. (×3)","(is) Brahmā, Hari, Śiva"],["guru-vaktrāt","n. abl. sg.","from the Guru's mouth"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["labhyeta","3 sg. opt. pass. of labh","should be obtained"],["pratyakṣam","n. acc. sg.","directly-perceived"],["sarvato-mukham","n. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","(the one) facing-all-sides"]],"lit":"\"Whoever utters 'Haṃsa Haṃsa', that Haṃsa is Brahmā, Hari, Śiva. From the Guru's mouth alone is this (Haṃsa) — direct-perceived, facing all sides — to be obtained.\"","para":"Identifies the Haṃsa with the <em>tri-mūrti</em> itself, then re-anchors: the <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em>'s actual transmission is <em>guru-vaktra-gata</em> (from the Guru's mouth) — its <em>pratyakṣatā</em> is mediated.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>sarvato-mukham</em> echoes Śvetāśvatara 3.16 (<em>sarvataḥ-pāṇi-pādaṃ tat sarvato'kṣi-śiro-mukham</em>) — the Haṃsa is the <em>viśva-rūpa</em> itself."],["Ayyangar","\"Whoever says (in whispers), 'Haṃsa', 'Haṃsa', that Haṃsa is Brahman, Viṣṇu and Śiva. One should obtain from the mouth of the preceptor (the path leading to the Haṃsa) that is directly cognised and faces in all directions.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>tri-mūrti</em>-identification is read as the <em>Saguṇa-trayī</em>'s collapse into the <em>Nirguṇa</em> Haṃsa."],["Krishnananda","\"<em>guru-vaktra</em>\" — knowledge is not in the syllables but in the <em>living transmission</em>; the same verse repeated by an unenlightened teacher is <em>not</em> the Haṃsa-vidyā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 35","title":"Like oil in sesame, fragrance in flower","sa":["तिलेषु च यथा तैलं पुष्पे गन्ध इवाश्रितः ।","पुरुषस्य शरीरेऽस्मिन्स बाह्याभ्यन्तरे तथा ॥ ३५ ॥"],"iast":["tileṣu ca yathā tailaṃ puṣpe gandha ivāśritaḥ |","puruṣasya śarīre'smin sa bāhyābhyantare tathā || 35 ||"],"words":[["tileṣu","m. loc. pl.","in sesame-seeds"],["ca yathā","conj. + indecl.","and as"],["tailam","n. nom. sg.","(the) oil"],["puṣpe","n. loc. sg.","in a flower"],["gandhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","fragrance"],["iva","indecl.","as"],["āśritaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of ā-śri","(is) lodged"],["puruṣasya","m. gen. sg.","of the puruṣa"],["śarīre","n. loc. sg.","in the body"],["asmin","pron. n. loc. sg.","in this"],["saḥ","pron. m. nom. sg.","he (the Haṃsa)"],["bāhya-abhyantare","n. loc. sg. (dvandva)","outside-and-within"],["tathā","indecl.","likewise"]],"lit":"\"As oil in sesame, as fragrance in a flower, lodged — so He, in this body of the <em>puruṣa</em>, (stands) outside and within.\"","para":"Classic <em>vibhūti</em>-image: the Haṃsa is not <em>somewhere</em> in the body but pervasively <em>with</em> it — like oil in sesame (cannot be located; the seed <em>is</em> oily) or fragrance in a flower. Inside-outside is a false alternative.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the simile is from Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.6.3, repurposed; Brahma-Yogin underscores that <em>bāhya-abhyantara</em> implies <em>bāhya-abhyantara-rahita</em> (free of the very inside/outside distinction)."],["Ayyangar","literal."],["Sivananda","uses this for <em>antaryāmin</em>-meditation — the Haṃsa is the <em>inner controller</em> whose pervasion is felt before it is conceptualised."],["Krishnananda","the <em>taila</em> image is geometrically perfect — non-locatable yet non-absent; he uses it to attack any \"search for the Self <em>inside</em> the body\" framing."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ VIII. Finite vs infinite Ātman; the five ātma-sthāna-s (vv. 36–43)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 36","title":"The torch and the treasure","sa":["उल्काहस्तो यथालोके द्रव्यमालोक्य तां त्यजेत् ।","ज्ञानेन ज्ञेयमालोक्य पश्चाज्ज्ञानं परित्यजेत् ॥ ३६ ॥"],"iast":["ulkā-hasto yathāloke dravyam ālokya tāṃ tyajet |","jñānena jñeyam ālokya paścāj jñānaṃ parityajet || 36 ||"],"words":[["ulkā-hastaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","(one) torch-in-hand"],["yathā","indecl.","as"],["āloke","m. loc. sg.","in the world (Ayyangar: \"in this world\")"],["dravyam","n. acc. sg.","the object (sought)"],["ālokya","ger. of ā-lok","having seen / noted"],["tām","pron. f. acc. sg.","her (the torch)"],["tyajet","3 sg. opt.","should let go"],["jñānena","n. instr. sg.","by knowledge"],["jñeyam","n. acc. sg.","the knowable"],["ālokya","ger.","having seen"],["paścāt","indecl.","afterwards"],["jñānam","n. acc. sg.","(the) knowledge"],["parityajet","3 sg. opt. of pari-tyaj","should wholly give up"]],"lit":"\"As a torch-bearer in this world, having located the object (he sought), lets the torch go — so, having seen the <em>jñeya</em> by <em>jñāna</em>, one should afterwards wholly give up the <em>jñāna</em>.\"","para":"Beautiful epistemic <em>vibhūti</em>: knowledge is instrumental. Once the <em>brahma-sākṣātkāra</em> has occurred, even the <em>jñāna</em> that produced it must be set down — for <em>jñāna</em> is still a <em>vṛtti</em>, and lingering with it is to stand in light by holding the lamp.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","paraphrases as \"<em>vṛtti-jñāna</em> serves only until <em>svarūpa-jñāna</em> arises; thereafter the <em>vṛtti</em> itself is <em>upādhi</em>.\" The same image appears in Yoga-Vāsiṣṭha VI-i.124.5."],["Ayyangar","\"Even as a torch-bearer (in quest of treasure) should, on finding the treasure, give up the torch, one should, on coming to realize, through knowledge, the quest of all knowledge (the Brahman), give up knowledge thereafter.\""],["Sivananda","a favourite verse — quoted in <em>Practice of Vedanta</em> to distinguish <em>prajñā</em> from <em>paramārtha</em>."],["Krishnananda","the verse is the Upaniṣad's <em>strongest anti-doctrinal</em> moment — <em>vidyā</em> must be released; only the <em>vidyā-mukta</em> is <em>brahma-bhūta</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 37","title":"Flower and its fragrance; tree and its shadow","sa":["पुष्पवत्सकलं विद्याद्गन्धस्तस्य तु निष्कलः ।","वृक्षस्तु सकलं विद्याच्छाया तस्य तु निष्कला ॥ ३७ ॥"],"iast":["puṣpavat sakalaṃ vidyād gandhas tasya tu niṣkalaḥ |","vṛkṣas tu sakalaṃ vidyāc chāyā tasya tu niṣkalā || 37 ||"],"words":[["puṣpavat","indecl.","like a flower"],["sakalam","n. acc. sg.","the with-part (finite)"],["vidyāt","3 sg. opt. of vid","one should know"],["gandhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(its) fragrance"],["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","of it"],["tu","indecl.","but"],["niṣkalaḥ","m. nom. sg.","partless"],["vṛkṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","tree"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["sakalam","n. acc. sg.","finite"],["vidyāt","3 sg. opt.","one should know"],["chāyā","f. nom. sg.","(its) shadow"],["tasya","pron. m. gen. sg.","of it"],["tu","indecl.","but"],["niṣkalā","f. nom. sg.","partless"]],"lit":"\"One should know the <em>sakala</em> (finite) as like a flower; its fragrance is <em>niṣkala</em> (partless). The tree is the <em>sakala</em>; its shadow is <em>niṣkala</em>.\"","para":"Two analogies for the sakala/niṣkala pair. Flower-fragrance: the finite has a partless aroma. Tree-shadow: the tree has finite branches but its shadow has none. The fragrance/shadow are produced <em>by</em> the finite yet exceed it.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the analogies caution against monism-of-collapse; the <em>niṣkala</em> is not <em>minus</em> the <em>sakala</em> but its <em>parinīṣpattikam ananta-rūpam</em>."],["Ayyangar","literal."],["Sivananda","a favourite for <em>karma-yoga</em> — action (sakala) and its fruit-savour (niṣkala) coexist; renounce neither but realise the niṣkala is what counts."],["Krishnananda","the <em>chāyā</em> image lets him propose that <em>jagat</em> is the <em>chāyā</em> of <em>Brahman</em> — present yet substanceless."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 38","title":"Sakala and niṣkala everywhere; means and end","sa":["निष्कलः सकलो भावः सर्वत्रैव व्यवस्थितः ।","उपायः सकलस्तद्वदुपेयश्चैव निष्कलः ॥ ३८ ॥"],"iast":["niṣkalaḥ sakalo bhāvaḥ sarvatraiva vyavasthitaḥ |","upāyaḥ sakalas tadvad upeyaś caiva niṣkalaḥ || 38 ||"],"words":[["niṣkalaḥ sakalaḥ","m. nom. sg. (×2)","partless (and) with-parts"],["bhāvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","being"],["sarvatra eva","indecl.","everywhere indeed"],["vyavasthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","(is) established"],["upāyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the means"],["sakalaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) with-parts"],["tadvat","indecl.","likewise"],["upeyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the end-attained"],["ca eva","indecl.","and indeed"],["niṣkalaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) partless"]],"lit":"\"Partless and with-parts — (this) Being is established everywhere alike. The <em>upāya</em> (means) is the <em>sakala</em>; likewise the <em>upeya</em> (goal) is the <em>niṣkala</em>.\"","para":"Doctrinal restatement: <em>sat</em> manifests under both rubrics everywhere. The <em>means</em> (sādhanā) is necessarily <em>sakala</em> (has steps, instruments); the <em>goal</em> is necessarily <em>niṣkala</em>. Confusing the two — taking means <em>as</em> goal — is the Yogin's chief error.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>upāya/upeya</em> asymmetry is the Upaniṣad's working axiom — every <em>vidhi</em> (v. 22-onwards) is <em>sakala</em>; the <em>phala</em> is <em>niṣkala</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"The expedient is the finite (Jīva), while the end sought is the infinite (Brahman).\""],["Sivananda","aligns with his <em>Sādhana-Catuṣṭaya</em> schema — the four <em>sādhana</em>-s are sakala; <em>brahma-prāpti</em> is niṣkala."],["Krishnananda","a Vedānta truism, but the verse's force is the <em>sarvatraiva</em> — even where the <em>sakala</em> seems isolated, the <em>niṣkala</em> coexists; there is no domain that is <em>all-sakala</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 39","title":"Sakala-in-sakala / niṣkala-in-niṣkala; one-two-three mātrās","sa":["सकले सकलो भावो निष्कले निष्कलस्तथा ।","एकमात्रो द्विमात्रश्च त्रिमात्रश्चैव भेदतः ॥ ३९ ॥"],"iast":["sakale sakalo bhāvo niṣkale niṣkalas tathā |","eka-mātro dvi-mātraś ca tri-mātraś caiva bhedataḥ || 39 ||"],"words":[["sakale","m. loc. sg.","in the with-parts"],["sakalaḥ bhāvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the with-parts being"],["niṣkale","m. loc. sg.","in the partless"],["niṣkalaḥ tathā","m. nom. sg. + indecl.","so (also) the partless"],["eka-mātraḥ dvi-mātraḥ tri-mātraḥ","m. nom. sg. (×3)","one-mātra, two-mātra, three-mātra"],["bhedataḥ","indecl. (abl. of bheda)","by distinction"]],"lit":"\"In the <em>sakala</em>, the being is sakala; in the <em>niṣkala</em>, likewise. By distinction (Praṇava is) one-<em>mātrā</em>, two-<em>mātrā</em>, three-<em>mātrā</em>.\"","para":"Ontological non-mixing (<em>sakala</em> stays sakala; niṣkala stays niṣkala in itself — they coexist, do not blur) followed by the <em>Praṇava</em> graded again: as a single-, double-, or triple-<em>mātrā</em> sound — different practices for different aspirants.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>bhedataḥ</em> clause sets up v. 40 — different <em>mātrā</em>-counts of Praṇava-japa correspond to different <em>adhikāra</em>-s; the <em>ardha-mātrā</em> (next verse) is <em>abhedataḥ</em> (without distinction) — i.e., the <em>para</em>-Brahman."],["Ayyangar","\"The one-measured (A), the two-measured (AU), and the three-measured (AUM), on account of their difference (are finite).\""],["Sivananda","in <em>Japa Yoga</em> he prescribes one-mātrā Oṃ for <em>aṣṭāṅga</em>-beginners, three-mātrā for advanced <em>upāsakas</em>, and <em>ardha</em> (= unbroken hum) for <em>jñāna-yogis</em>."],["Krishnananda","\"the one-mātra is <em>adhibhautika</em>; two-mātra is <em>adhidaivika</em>; three-mātra is <em>adhyātmika</em>; ardha is <em>para</em>\" — a four-fold <em>adhikāra-paribhāṣā</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 40","title":"Ardha-mātrā as parā; five-fold sakala","sa":["अर्धमात्रा परा ज्ञेया तत ऊर्ध्वं परात्परम् ।","पञ्चधा पञ्चदैवत्यं सकलं परिपठ्यते ॥ ४० ॥"],"iast":["ardha-mātrā parā jñeyā tata ūrdhvaṃ parātparam |","pañcadhā pañca-daivatyaṃ sakalaṃ paripaṭhyate || 40 ||"],"words":[["ardha-mātrā","f. nom. sg.","the half-mātrā"],["parā","f. nom. sg.","(is) transcendent"],["jñeyā","f. nom. sg. gerundive","to be known"],["tataḥ","indecl. (abl.)","beyond that"],["ūrdhvam","indecl.","upward"],["parātparam","n. nom. sg.","(the) higher-than-the-high"],["pañcadhā","indecl.","five-fold"],["pañca-daivatyam","n. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","with five deities"],["sakalam","n. nom. sg.","(the) sakala"],["paripaṭhyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is read-around / declared"]],"lit":"\"The half-mātrā is to be known as the transcendent; beyond that, upward, is the higher-than-the-high. The <em>sakala</em> is declared five-fold, having five deities.\"","para":"Two moves: (a) ardha-mātrā is <em>parā</em>, and yet <em>above even that</em> there is <em>parātpara</em> (the absolutely-supreme — Brahma-Yogin treats this as a marker against settling for any sound-state); (b) the <em>sakala</em> (the phenomenal aspect) is now to be unpacked as a five-fold structure with five presiding deities — taken up in vv. 41–43.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>parātpara</em> phrase is decisive — even the <em>ardha-mātrā</em> is <em>a</em> state; the Real transcends all states, including the apparently transcendent."],["Ayyangar","\"The half-measure should be known as the infinite, the transcendent (Brahman, far above the five Ātman-s with their five seats in the heart, etc.); what is above that, transcends what is transcendent.\""],["Sivananda","the meditative implication — pursue the <em>ardha-mātrā</em> into its own <em>laya</em>; do not settle in the <em>anāhata-nāda</em>."],["Krishnananda","the verse exposes Vedānta's <em>anavasthā-vāra</em> against the <em>sāmādhi</em>-as-goal trap; the <em>para</em> is not at the end of any series."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 41","title":"The five ātma-sthānas in the body (1/2)","sa":["ब्रह्मणो हृदयस्थानं कण्ठे विष्णुः समाश्रितः ।","तालुमध्ये स्थितो रुद्रो ललाटस्थो महेश्वरः ॥ ४१ ॥"],"iast":["brahmaṇo hṛdaya-sthānaṃ kaṇṭhe viṣṇuḥ samāśritaḥ |","tālu-madhye sthito rudro lalāṭa-stho maheśvaraḥ || 41 ||"],"words":[["brahmaṇaḥ","m. gen. sg.","(the god) Brahmā's"],["hṛdaya-sthānam","n. nom. sg. (tatpur.)","heart-seat"],["kaṇṭhe","m. loc. sg.","in the throat"],["viṣṇuḥ","m. nom. sg.","Viṣṇu"],["samāśritaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of sam-ā-śri","has taken his stand"],["tālu-madhye","n. loc. sg.","in the middle of the palate"],["sthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","(is) stationed"],["rudraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Rudra"],["lalāṭa-sthaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","forehead-stationed"],["maheśvaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Maheśvara"]],"lit":"\"Brahmā's seat is the heart; in the throat Viṣṇu has taken his stand; in the middle of the palate is Rudra stationed; in the forehead is Maheśvara.\"","para":"Four of the five <em>ātma-sthāna</em>-s mapped: heart-Brahmā, throat-Viṣṇu, palate-Rudra, forehead-Maheśvara. The fifth (Sadāśiva at the nose-tip) comes in v. 42.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this is the <em>pañcāyatana-prāṇa-vidyā</em> (a variant of <em>Pāśupata-Brahma-Upaniṣad</em>'s scheme). The order ascends from gross (heart) to subtle (nose-tip); each <em>sthāna</em> is the <em>adhiṣṭhāna</em> of one of the five <em>ātman</em>-s (Viśva-Taijasa-Prājña-Turīya-Turīyātīta)."],["Ayyangar","literal."],["Sivananda","the <em>pañca-ātma-sthāna</em> meditation: chant Oṃ five times, mentally locating each deity in sequence; binds the body to the <em>jyotir-maya</em> hierarchy."],["Krishnananda","note the inverse correlation — <em>higher</em> god in the <em>higher</em> seat (heart → forehead → nose-tip) reverses the usual <em>cakra</em>-ascent."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 42","title":"Acyuta at the nose-tip; nothing beyond","sa":["नासाग्रे अच्युतं विद्यात्तस्यान्ते तु परं पदम् ।","परत्वात्तु परं नास्तीत्येवं शास्त्रस्य निर्णयः ॥ ४२ ॥"],"iast":["nāsāgre acyutaṃ vidyāt tasyānte tu paraṃ padam |","paratvāt tu paraṃ nāstīty evaṃ śāstrasya nirṇayaḥ || 42 ||"],"words":[["nāsa-agre","n. loc. sg. (tatpur.)","at the nose-tip"],["acyutam","m. acc. sg.","(the) Unfallen (Sadāśiva / Viṣṇu)"],["vidyāt","3 sg. opt. of vid","one should know"],["tasya ante","pron. + m. loc. sg.","at its end"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["param padam","n. nom. sg.","the supreme abode"],["paratvāt","n. abl. sg.","by virtue of being supreme"],["tu","indecl.","however"],["param na asti","indecl.","there is no further"],["iti evam","indecl.","thus"],["śāstrasya nirṇayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the conclusion of the śāstra"]],"lit":"\"At the nose-tip one should know <em>Acyuta</em>; at its end is the supreme abode. By its very supremacy, beyond it there is nothing — such is the conclusion of the śāstra.\"","para":"Adds the fifth locus (nose-tip = Acyuta / Sadāśiva), then explicitly closes the series: at <em>that</em> end is <em>paraṃ padam</em>, and there is no further. The <em>paratvāt … paraṃ nāsti</em> line is the Upaniṣad's <em>iti-mahā-vākyam</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>paratvāt paraṃ nāsti</em> is a <em>pratiṣedha-pratiṣedha</em> — denial of further denial. The <em>para</em> is the very <em>limit-of-limits</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"one should find the Sadā-Śiva (that never swerves) at the tip of the nose; at the end of which is the supreme seat. There is nothing beyond (the transcendent), as that itself transcends all. This is the conclusion arrived at by the śāstras.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>nāsāgra-dṛṣṭi</em> (gaze at the nose-tip) of <em>Bhagavad-Gītā</em> 6.13 is keyed to this verse — there one <em>sees</em> Acyuta."],["Krishnananda","the <em>nirṇayaḥ</em> declaration is itself authoritative — Vedānta's final word against any further <em>vihāra</em> of speculation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 43","title":"Dehātīta: the twelve-finger station beyond the nose-tip","sa":["देहातीतं तु तं विद्यान्नासाग्रे द्वादशाङ्गुलम् ।","तदन्तं तं विजानीयात्तत्रस्थो व्यापयेत्प्रभुः ॥ ४३ ॥"],"iast":["dehātītaṃ tu taṃ vidyān nāsāgre dvādaśāṅgulam |","tad-antaṃ taṃ vijānīyāt tatra-stho vyāpayet prabhuḥ || 43 ||"],"words":[["deha-atītam","n. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","beyond-the-body"],["tu tam","indecl. + pron. m. acc. sg.","indeed him"],["vidyāt","3 sg. opt.","one should know"],["nāsa-agre","n. loc. sg.","at the nose-tip"],["dvādaśa-aṅgulam","n. acc. sg.","(at a distance of) twelve fingers"],["tat-antam","n. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","(one whose) end is that"],["tam","pron. m. acc. sg.","him"],["vijānīyāt","3 sg. opt. of vi-jñā","one should fully know"],["tatra-sthaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","stationed there"],["vyāpayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of vi-āp","(he) should make-pervaded"],["prabhuḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Lord"]],"lit":"\"One should know him as beyond-the-body, at twelve fingers from the nose-tip. Him whose end is <em>that</em> (twelve-finger point), one should fully know; standing there, the Lord pervades.\"","para":"The <em>dehātīta</em> — the point twelve <em>aṅgula</em>-s in front of the nose-tip (the classical <em>bāhya-bindu</em> of <em>Khecarī</em>/<em>Trāṭaka</em>/breath-yoga). Realisation of <em>Brahman</em> is anchored <em>at</em> and <em>beyond</em> this point. The Lord, standing there, pervades all.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>dvādaśa-aṅgula</em> point is the <em>bhrū-madhya-sammukha</em> — twelve-finger projection forward; this is where <em>prāṇa</em> extends in normal exhalation and where the Yogin <em>reabsorbs</em> it."],["Ayyangar","\"That seat one should know as the Dehātīta (what transcends the body), wherein is seated the innermost Ātman, which is twelve digit-lengths from the tip of the nose.\""],["Sivananda","the practical <em>prāṇa-abhyāsa</em> — measure <em>recaka</em> and <em>kumbhaka</em> by this twelve-finger projection; when <em>kumbhaka</em> keeps <em>prāṇa</em> from reaching the twelve-finger point, <em>kevala-kumbhaka</em> is near."],["Krishnananda","the spatial language is <em>upāsanā-sthala</em> (place-of-meditation), not metaphysics; he warns against literalising the twelve fingers as a body-mass-independent constant."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ IX. Yoga's secrecy and the qualified disciple (vv. 44–48)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 44","title":"Uninterrupted yoga despite divided attention","sa":["मनोऽप्यन्यत्र निक्षिप्तं चक्षुरन्यत्र पातितम् ।","तथापि योगिनां योगो ह्यविच्छिन्नः प्रवर्तते ॥ ४४ ॥"],"iast":["mano'py anyatra nikṣiptaṃ cakṣur anyatra pātitam |","tathāpi yogināṃ yogo hy avicchinnaḥ pravartate || 44 ||"],"words":[["manas api","n. nom. sg. + indecl.","even the mind"],["anyatra","indecl.","elsewhere"],["nikṣiptam","n. nom. sg. pp.","thrown / directed"],["cakṣus","n. nom. sg.","the eye"],["anyatra","indecl.","elsewhere"],["pātitam","n. nom. sg. pp. caus.","(let) fall / cast"],["tathā api","indecl.","even so"],["yogi-nām yogaḥ","m. gen. pl. + m. nom. sg.","the yoga of the yogins"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["avicchinnaḥ","m. nom. sg.","uninterrupted"],["pravartate","3 sg. pres. ātm.","proceeds"]],"lit":"\"Even though the mind is directed elsewhere and the eye cast elsewhere, even so the yoga of yogins proceeds uninterrupted.\"","para":"<em>Sahaja</em> marker. Once the inner <em>Haṃsa-yoga</em> has stabilised, no outward distraction can break it — mind and eye may run elsewhere; the yoga runs on.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this defines <em>sahaja-samādhi</em> / <em>unmanī</em>-state — the <em>avasthā</em> in which yoga is no longer an <em>abhyāsa</em> and so cannot be interrupted by what would interrupt an <em>abhyāsa</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"With the mind directed elsewhere, and the eyes let fall on something else, even should it be so, the Yoga of Yogin-s has an uninterrupted course.\""],["Sivananda","this is the test of <em>jīvanmukti</em> — the <em>yoga</em> persists across <em>vyavahāra</em>."],["Krishnananda","the <em>avicchinnatva</em> is not \"doing yoga while doing something else\" but <em>being</em> in the state that does not break."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 45","title":"Supreme secret, supreme good","sa":["एतत्तु परमं गुह्यमेतत्तु परमं शुभम् ।","नातः परतरं किञ्चिन्नातः परतरं शुभम् ॥ ४५ ॥"],"iast":["etat tu paramaṃ guhyam etat tu paramaṃ śubham |","nātaḥ parataraṃ kiñcin nātaḥ parataraṃ śubham || 45 ||"],"words":[["etat tu","pron. n. nom. sg. + indecl.","this indeed"],["paramaṃ guhyam","n. nom. sg.","(is the) supreme secret"],["paramaṃ śubham","n. nom. sg.","the supreme good"],["na ataḥ","indecl.","not beyond this"],["parataram kiñcit","n. nom. sg. + indecl.","anything more supreme"],["na ataḥ parataraṃ śubham","indecl.","nothing more supreme as good"]],"lit":"\"This indeed is the supreme secret; this is the supreme good. Beyond this there is nothing more supreme; beyond this nothing more supreme as good.\"","para":"Praśaṃsā-stanza preparing the secrecy injunctions of v. 47–48. <em>Guhya</em> and <em>śubha</em> are paired — what is most secret is what is most beneficial.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>guhya</em> = <em>gopanīya</em> (to-be-protected, not \"occult\"); the secrecy is functional — it protects the Vidyā from unqualified handling, not from publication."],["Ayyangar","literal."],["Sivananda","he treats the <em>guhya</em>-rhetoric as a <em>upāyantara</em> — what is <em>given freely</em> is treated lightly; what is <em>withheld</em> is sought; the secrecy is the protection of <em>adhikāra</em>."],["Krishnananda","the <em>na ataḥ paratara</em> declaration is the Upaniṣad's <em>prema-paryanta-vacana</em> — the only honest praise is the one that closes the bidding."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 46","title":"Cling to it with effort","sa":["शुद्धज्ञानामृतं प्राप्य परमाक्षरनिर्णयम् ।","गुह्याद्गुह्यतमं गोप्यं ग्रहणीयं प्रयत्नतः ॥ ४६ ॥"],"iast":["śuddha-jñānāmṛtaṃ prāpya paramākṣara-nirṇayam |","guhyād guhyatamaṃ gopyaṃ grahaṇīyaṃ prayatnataḥ || 46 ||"],"words":[["śuddha-jñāna-amṛtam","n. acc. sg.","the pure-knowledge-nectar"],["prāpya","ger. of pra-āp","having obtained"],["parama-akṣara-nirṇayam","n. acc. sg.","the determination of the supreme imperishable"],["guhyāt guhyatamam","n. nom. sg.","secret-of-secrets"],["gopyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","(is) to-be-protected"],["grahaṇīyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","(is) to-be-grasped"],["prayatnataḥ","indecl. (abl.)","with effort"]],"lit":"\"Having obtained the pure-knowledge-nectar — the determination of the supreme Imperishable — secret-of-secrets, to be protected, to be grasped with effort —\"","para":"Three gerundives stack the obligation: this <em>amṛta</em> is to be protected (<em>gopya</em>) and to be grasped (<em>grahaṇīya</em>) — but <em>prayatnataḥ</em>, with effort. The verse functions as the introduction to the negative injunctions of v. 47.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the threefold <em>gopya-grahaṇīya-prayatnataḥ</em> is the <em>adhikārī</em>'s triple obligation — <em>secrecy</em> (toward the unfit), <em>appropriation</em> (toward oneself), and <em>effort</em> (against laziness in either)."],["Ayyangar","literal — \"the profoundest of profound secrets, worthy of being scrupulously hidden from view, worthy of being grasped with great effort.\""],["Sivananda","<em>prayatnataḥ</em> is the antidote to <em>sat-sanga</em>-tourism — <em>guhya</em> is paid for in <em>prayatna</em>, never in coin."],["Krishnananda","both <em>gopya</em> and <em>grāhya</em> mark the <em>vidyā</em>'s living character — it is alive only if both held inward and <em>sought</em> further; otherwise it lapses."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 47","title":"Not to be given to unworthy son or pupil","sa":["नापुत्राय प्रदातव्यं नाशिष्याय कदाचन ।","गुरुदेवाय भक्ताय नित्यं भक्तिपराय च ॥ ४७ ॥"],"iast":["nāputrāya pradātavyaṃ nāśiṣyāya kadācana |","guru-devāya bhaktāya nityaṃ bhakti-parāya ca || 47 ||"],"words":[["na","indecl.","not"],["aputrāya","m. dat. sg.","to a non-son (one not as one's son)"],["pradātavyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","is to be given"],["na aśiṣyāya","indecl. + m. dat. sg.","not to a non-disciple"],["kadācana","indecl.","ever"],["guru-devāya","m. dat. sg.","to (one for whom) the Guru is the deity"],["bhaktāya","m. dat. sg.","to the devotee"],["nityam","adv.","always"],["bhakti-parāya","m. dat. sg.","(and to) one intent on bhakti"],["ca","conj.","and"]],"lit":"\"Not to a non-son (i.e., one undeserving of son-like trust), nor ever to a non-disciple, is it to be given — (but) to him for whom the Guru is the deity, to the devotee, always to one intent on devotion —\"","para":"<em>Adhikāra</em>-clause. The Vidyā is for the <em>devout, son-like disciple</em>. <em>Aputra/aśiṣya</em> should be read functionally — \"not-son-like in trust\" / \"not-disciple-like in receptivity\".","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>aputra/aśiṣya</em> are not literal — anyone can be a <em>putra-tulya</em> by <em>guru-bhakti</em>. The verse defines the <em>adhikārī</em>, not the lineage."],["Ayyangar","\"This science should be bestowed on a devoted pupil, who looks upon his Guru as God-incarnate, and is ever intent on devotion (to the Guru).\""],["Sivananda","a standing principle of his ashram's <em>Sannyāsa</em>-dīkṣā — the <em>bhakti-test</em> precedes the <em>vidyā-dāna</em>."],["Krishnananda","the <em>bhakti-paratva</em> is psychological, not behavioural — the disciple's mind must spontaneously seek the Guru."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 48","title":"Bestower of the Vidyā to the unworthy goes to hell","sa":["प्रदातव्यमिदं शास्त्रं नेतरेभ्यः प्रदापयेत् ।","दातास्य नरकं याति सिद्ध्यते न कदाचन ॥ ४८ ॥"],"iast":["pradātavyam idaṃ śāstraṃ netarebhyaḥ pradāpayet |","dātāsya narakaṃ yāti siddhyate na kadācana || 48 ||"],"words":[["pradātavyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive","to be given"],["idaṃ śāstram","n. nom. sg.","this teaching"],["na itarebhyaḥ","indecl. + m. dat. pl.","not to others"],["pradāpayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of pra-dā","one should (not) cause to be given"],["dātā","m. nom. sg.","(such a) giver"],["asya","pron. m. gen. sg.","of this (Vidyā)"],["narakaṃ yāti","m. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","goes to hell"],["siddhyate na kadācana","3 sg. pres. pass. + indecl.","(he is) never (otherwise) accomplished"]],"lit":"\"This śāstra is to be given (only to the deserving); to others one should not cause it to be given. The bestower of it (to the unworthy) goes to hell and is never (otherwise) accomplished.\"","para":"The negative seal: indiscriminate transmission carries <em>narakaṃ yāti</em> (loss of <em>gati</em>) and arrests the giver's own <em>siddhi</em>. <em>Itarebhyaḥ</em> = \"others\" = those failing the adhikāra-test of v. 47.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>naraka</em> here is symbolic (loss of <em>gati</em>); the real harm is <em>siddhyate na</em> — the <em>vidyā</em> refuses to mature in a teacher who has cheapened it."],["Ayyangar","\"Should he do so, the bestower of it reaches hell and shall, at no time thereafter, succeed in its accomplishment.\""],["Sivananda","read alongside v. 47 as a single <em>paddhati</em> (rule) for transmission ethics."],["Krishnananda","the verse cautions modern <em>guru-s</em> against <em>dīkṣā</em>-as-publicity; the very Vidyā withdraws."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ X. Stage-independence; the three Gurus (vv. 49–53)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 49","title":"Knower of the supreme imperishable is qualified in any āśrama","sa":["गृहस्थो ब्रह्मचारी च वानप्रस्थश्च भिक्षुकः ।","यत्र तत्र स्थितो ज्ञानी परमाक्षरवित्सदा ॥ ४९ ॥"],"iast":["gṛhastho brahmacārī ca vānaprasthaś ca bhikṣukaḥ |","yatra tatra sthito jñānī paramākṣara-vit sadā || 49 ||"],"words":[["gṛhasthaḥ","m. nom. sg.","householder"],["brahmacārī","m. nom. sg.","celibate-student"],["ca","conj.","and"],["vānaprasthaḥ","m. nom. sg.","forest-dweller"],["ca","conj.","and"],["bhikṣukaḥ","m. nom. sg.","mendicant"],["yatra tatra","indecl. (×2)","wherever"],["sthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","abiding"],["jñānī","m. nom. sg.","(he is) the gnostic"],["parama-akṣara-vit","m. nom. sg.","knower of the supreme imperishable"],["sadā","indecl.","always"]],"lit":"\"Householder, celibate, forest-dweller, mendicant — wherever he abides, the gnostic is always the knower of the supreme Imperishable.\"","para":"Aśrama-indifference. The <em>jñānī</em> is identified by <em>parama-akṣara-vittva</em>, not by his <em>āśrama</em>. All four <em>āśrama</em>-s admit <em>brahma-vid</em>-s.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","parallels Bhagavad-Gītā 5.18 (<em>vidyā-vinaya-sampanne …</em>); <em>jñānin</em> is <em>guṇātīta</em>-by-vidyā, <em>āśrama</em>-neutral."],["Ayyangar","\"He who has acquired the knowledge (of this science), whether he is a householder or a celebate, or a dweller in a forest, or a mendicant, wherever he may be, having knowledge of the transcendent imperishable existence always, … attains happiness.\""],["Sivananda","often quoted in DLS literature to legitimate <em>grhastha-jñāna</em> — sannyāsa not required."],["Krishnananda","the <em>parama-akṣara-vittva</em> is <em>adhyātma-eka-niṣṭhā</em>; <em>āśrama</em> is <em>vyavahāra</em>, and <em>vyavahāra</em> doesn't determine <em>paramārtha</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 50","title":"Even the worldly knower transitions well","sa":["विषयी विषयासक्तो याति देहान्तरे शुभम् ।","ज्ञानादेवास्य शास्त्रस्य सर्वावस्थोऽपि मानवः ॥ ५० ॥"],"iast":["viṣayī viṣayāsakto yāti dehāntare śubham |","jñānād evāsya śāstrasya sarvāvastho'pi mānavaḥ || 50 ||"],"words":[["viṣayī","m. nom. sg.","the (worldly) enjoyer"],["viṣaya-āsaktaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","attached to objects"],["yāti dehāntare śubham","3 sg. pres. + m. loc. sg. + n. acc. sg.","goes to good (in a future) body"],["jñānāt eva","n. abl. sg.","by knowledge alone"],["asya śāstrasya","n. gen. sg.","of this teaching"],["sarvāvasthaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","in-every-state"],["api","indecl.","even"],["mānavaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the human"]],"lit":"\"The viṣayī, attached to objects, goes (still) to good in another body — by the very knowledge of this śāstra — the human, even in any state.\"","para":"Even partial knowledge of this Vidyā saves: an attached worldly enjoyer who has <em>had</em> this <em>jñāna</em>, in any <em>avasthā</em>, attains <em>śubha</em> in his next body. The Vidyā's <em>artha</em> (efficacy) is independent of complete <em>anuṣṭhāna</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>artha-vāda</em> extension is hyperbolic but doctrinally orthodox — the seed-knowledge fructifies eventually (Gītā 6.40–45)."],["Ayyangar","\"That man, in whatever of the several states he may be, by the mere knowledge of this science, is not smeared, either with the merit … or the sin attached to the slaughter of a Brahmaṇa and other sinful acts.\""],["Sivananda","a <em>praśaṃsā</em> verse to motivate <em>śravaṇa</em> even from imperfect students."],["Krishnananda","the verse warns Vedānta-traditionalists against gatekeeping — the Upaniṣad explicitly allows the <em>viṣayī</em> a <em>śubha-gati</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 51","title":"Unsoiled by Brahmaṇa-slaying or aśvamedha","sa":["ब्रह्महत्याश्वमेधाद्यैः पुण्यपापैर्न लिप्यते ।","चोदको बोधकश्चैव मोक्षदश्च परः स्मृतः ॥ ५१ ॥"],"iast":["brahma-hatyāśvamedhādyaiḥ puṇya-pāpair na lipyate |","codako bodhakaś caiva mokṣa-daś ca paraḥ smṛtaḥ || 51 ||"],"words":[["brahma-hatyā-aśvamedha-ādyaiḥ","n. instr. pl. (tatpur.)","by brahmin-slaying, horse-sacrifice etc."],["puṇya-pāpaiḥ","n. instr. pl. (dvandva)","by merits and demerits"],["na lipyate","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","(he is) not smeared"],["codakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the prompter"],["bodhakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the awakener"],["ca eva","indecl.","and indeed"],["mokṣa-daḥ","m. nom. sg.","the liberation-giver"],["ca paraḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","and (this is) the supreme"],["smṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of smṛ","is taught"]],"lit":"\"He is not smeared by puṇya and pāpa, by brahmin-slaying, by aśvamedha, etc. (Three Gurus:) the prompter, the awakener, and the bestower of liberation — (this last is) celebrated as the supreme.\"","para":"Two unrelated lines yoked: (a) the <em>jñānī</em> of v. 50 is not smeared by any extreme act (<em>brahma-hatyā</em> = lowest, <em>aśvamedha</em> = highest); (b) the topic of the next two verses — three kinds of Guru.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>na lipyate</em> is the standard <em>jñānin-na-lipyate-līlā</em> claim (Īśā 2; Chā 4.14.3); not an ethical license but an ontological description."],["Ayyangar","groups vv. 51b–53 as \"The Three Kinds of Preceptors\"."],["Sivananda","the three-Guru schema is the <em>Sad-Guru-Tattva</em> — many guides, few liberators; the <em>mokṣa-da</em> is rare."],["Krishnananda","the three are Patañjali's <em>prāmāṇika</em>, <em>anumānika</em>, <em>āpta</em>-grades transposed into <em>guru-tāratamya</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 52","title":"The three Gurus distinguished","sa":["इत्येषं त्रिविधो ज्ञेय आचार्यस्तु महीतले ।","चोदको दर्शयेन्मार्गं बोधकः स्थानमाचरेत् ॥ ५२ ॥"],"iast":["ity eṣaṃ tri-vidho jñeya ācāryas tu mahītale |","codako darśayen mārgaṃ bodhakaḥ sthānam ācaret || 52 ||"],"words":[["iti eṣam","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","thus this (preceptor)"],["tri-vidhaḥ","m. nom. sg.","three-fold"],["jñeyaḥ","m. nom. sg. gerundive","is to be known"],["ācāryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the teacher"],["tu mahītale","indecl. + m. loc. sg.","on earth"],["codakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the prompter"],["darśayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of dṛś","should show"],["mārgam","m. acc. sg.","the path"],["bodhakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the awakener"],["sthānam","n. acc. sg.","the (final) place"],["ācaret","3 sg. opt. of ā-car","should bring near / cause to be approached"]],"lit":"\"Thus, on earth, the teacher (ācārya) is to be known as three-fold: the <em>codaka</em> should show the path, the <em>bodhaka</em> should cause (the disciple) to approach the (final) place …\"","para":"Definitions. <em>Codaka</em> = pathfinder; <em>bodhaka</em> = approacher (who brings to the threshold); <em>mokṣa-da</em> (v. 53) = the door itself.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>codaka</em> teaches \"<em>I am Brahman</em>\" as a <em>bhāvanā</em>; the <em>bodhaka</em> deepens it to \"<em>tat tvam asi</em>\"; the <em>mokṣa-da</em> is the <em>paramātman</em> himself functioning through the Guru."],["Ayyangar","\"The prompter would show the pupil the path by pointing out that he should resort to the practice of meditation of the form — 'I am the Brahman'. The awakener will point to the Supreme seat by awakening in the pupil the strong belief indicated by 'That thou art. Thou art that' …\""],["Sivananda","the three Gurus may be the <em>same</em> person at different stages of the disciple's journey, not three different teachers."],["Krishnananda","the <em>mokṣa-da</em> is <em>Īśvara</em>-Guru — the impersonal function, not a person."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 53","title":"The mokṣa-da Guru and the inner sacrifice to come","sa":["मोक्षदस्तु परं तत्त्वं यज्ज्ञात्वा परमश्नुते ।","प्रत्यक्षयजनं देहे सङ्क्षेपाच्छृणु गौतम ॥ ५३ ॥"],"iast":["mokṣa-das tu paraṃ tattvaṃ yaj jñātvā paramaśnute |","pratyakṣa-yajanaṃ dehe saṃkṣepāc chṛṇu gautama || 53 ||"],"words":[["mokṣa-daḥ","m. nom. sg.","the liberation-giver"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["paraṃ tattvam","n. nom. sg.","(is) the supreme Real"],["yat jñātvā","rel. + ger. of jñā","knowing which"],["param aśnute","indecl. + 3 sg. pres. ātm.","one attains the supreme"],["pratyakṣa-yajanam","n. acc. sg.","the directly-perceived sacrifice"],["dehe","m. loc. sg.","in the body"],["saṃkṣepāt","indecl. (abl.)","briefly"],["śṛṇu","2 sg. impv. of śru","hear"],["gautama","m. voc. sg.","O Gautama"]],"lit":"\"The <em>mokṣa-da</em>, however, is the supreme Real (itself); knowing which, one attains the supreme. (Now) hear briefly, O Gautama, the directly-perceived sacrifice (to be performed) in the body.\"","para":"Closes the three-Guru section by identifying the third Guru with <em>Brahman</em> itself, then opens §XI: an inner <em>yajña</em> (the body as altar). The <em>vocative Gautama</em> identifies the immediate interlocutor (= the disciple-frame of the Upaniṣad).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>mokṣa-da-tattva-aikya</em> (Guru = Tattva) is the warrant for the disciple's <em>bhakti</em>-to-Brahman via the Guru; the <em>yajana</em> turns inward from here."],["Ayyangar","\"the bestower of liberation is the transcendent Brahman, the Īśvara — which imparts to him the truth, 'All is of my form. Thou wilt attain me. There is not even a speck beyond me' — on knowing which, one attains immortality.\""],["Sivananda","the verse-pair v. 53–57 is the <em>Antar-Yajña</em> lecture-text in his ashram."],["Krishnananda","<em>aśnute</em> (one <em>eats</em> / attains) — the Vedic taste-metaphor; the supreme is <em>consumed</em>, not merely <em>cognised</em>."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ XI. The inner yajña (vv. 54–57)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 54","title":"Bindu and niṣkala seen of one's own accord","sa":["तेनेष्ट्वा स नरो याति शाश्वतं पदमव्ययम् ।","स्वयमेव तु सम्पश्येद्देहे बिन्दुं च निष्कलम् ॥ ५४ ॥"],"iast":["teneṣṭvā sa naro yāti śāśvataṃ padam avyayam |","svayam eva tu sampaśyed dehe binduṃ ca niṣkalam || 54 ||"],"words":[["tena iṣṭvā","indecl. + ger. of yaj","having sacrificed by that"],["saḥ naraḥ","m. nom. sg.","that man"],["yāti","3 sg. pres.","goes (to)"],["śāśvatam padam avyayam","n. acc. sg. (×2)","the eternal undecaying abode"],["svayam eva","indecl.","of his own accord"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["sampaśyet","3 sg. opt. of sam-paś","should fully see"],["dehe","m. loc. sg.","in the body"],["bindum","m. acc. sg.","the bindu"],["ca niṣkalam","conj. + m. acc. sg.","and the partless"]],"lit":"\"By that sacrifice, that man goes to the eternal, undecaying abode. Of his own accord, he should see, in the body, the Bindu and the partless (Brahman).\"","para":"The promised fruit of the <em>antar-yajña</em> (which is described in 55–57): <em>śāśvataṃ padam avyayam</em>. The seeing is <em>svayam eva</em> — not relayed by teacher or text, but direct.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>bindu</em> is the <em>amṛta-kalā</em> of v. 22 — the visible-of-itself manifestation; the <em>niṣkala</em> is its essence."],["Ayyangar","\"In this body should he see the Bindu (the speck) and (the Brahman) which has no parts, of his own accord.\""],["Sivananda","the practical mark — when the <em>trikuṭi</em> (eyebrow-junction) shows a steady blue-white speck on <em>kumbhaka</em>, the <em>bindu</em> is being seen."],["Krishnananda","<em>svayam eva</em> — the <em>darśana</em> cannot be borrowed; the Guru can only ensure the conditions."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 55","title":"The two ayanas and equinoxes; recaka-pūraka-kumbhaka","sa":["अयने द्वे च विषुवे सदा पश्यति मार्गवित् ।","कृत्वायामं पुरा वत्स रेचपूरककुम्भकान् ॥ ५५ ॥"],"iast":["ayane dve ca viṣuve sadā paśyati mārga-vit |","kṛtvāyāmaṃ purā vatsa reca-pūraka-kumbhakān || 55 ||"],"words":[["ayane dve","n. acc. du.","the two ayana-s (solstices)"],["ca viṣuve","conj. + n. acc. du.","and the (two) equinoxes"],["sadā paśyati","indecl. + 3 sg. pres.","always sees"],["mārga-vit","m. nom. sg.","the knower of the path"],["kṛtvā āyāmam","ger. + m. acc. sg.","having (first) made the prolongation"],["purā","indecl.","beforehand"],["vatsa","m. voc. sg.","O child"],["reca-pūraka-kumbhakān","m. acc. pl. (dvandva)","(the trio) recaka-pūraka-kumbhaka"]],"lit":"\"The knower of the path always sees the two <em>ayana</em>-s and the (two) equinoxes. Having made the prolongation (āyāma) of recaka, pūraka, kumbhaka beforehand, O child —\"","para":"Inner-yajña instruction. <em>Ayana-dvaya</em> and <em>viṣuva</em> are the inner equivalents of solar transitions — junctions of <em>prāṇa-apāna</em> in <em>Iḍā/Piṅgalā</em> and the equilibrium phase in Suṣumnā. These are <em>always seen</em> by the <em>mārga-vit</em>. Practically, the Yogin first makes <em>āyāma</em> of the three breath-phases.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","explicit gloss: <em>ayana</em> = the <em>Iḍā-Piṅgalā</em> exchange (uttara/dakṣiṇa-going prāṇa); <em>viṣuva</em> = the moment of equality (when Suṣumnā opens). Cosmic time is interiorised."],["Ayyangar","\"The knower of the pathway always sees the two courses (northern and southern, i.e., the junction of the Iḍā with the Piṅgalā and vice versa) and the equinoxes (the entry of the Prāṇa in the crest and the Mūlādhāra).\""],["Sivananda","the verse is a doctrinal warrant for <em>prāṇāyāma</em> timing — long <em>recaka</em>, longer <em>kumbhaka</em>, balanced <em>pūraka</em>."],["Krishnananda","the <em>adhyātma</em>-cosmic correspondence here is one of his favourite illustrations — the body <em>is</em> a <em>yajña-śālā</em> with all the <em>vedi</em>-s of cosmic ritual."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 56","title":"Worship in sequence with namaskāra, yoga, mudrā","sa":["पूर्वं चोभयमुच्चार्य अर्चयेत्तु यथाक्रमम् ।","नमस्कारेण योगेन मुद्रयारभ्य चार्चयेत् ॥ ५६ ॥"],"iast":["pūrvaṃ cobhayam uccārya arcayet tu yathā-kramam |","namaskāreṇa yogena mudrayārabhya cārcayet || 56 ||"],"words":[["pūrvam","adv.","first"],["ca ubhayam","conj. + n. acc. sg.","and both"],["uccārya","ger. of ud-car","having uttered"],["arcayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of arc","one should worship"],["tu yathā-kramam","indecl. + adv.","indeed in due order"],["namaskāreṇa","m. instr. sg.","by salutation"],["yogena","m. instr. sg.","by yoga"],["mudrayā","f. instr. sg.","by mudrā"],["ārabhya","ger. of ā-rabh","commencing"],["ca arcayet","conj. + 3 sg. opt.","should worship"]],"lit":"\"Having first uttered both (Oṃ and Haṃsa), one should worship in due order — by salutation, by yoga, by mudrā — commencing (thus) one should worship.\"","para":"Liturgical order of the inner yajña: <em>(a) utter Oṃ + Haṃsa</em>; <em>(b) namaskāra</em>; <em>(c) yogic engagement</em>; <em>(d) mudrā</em> — and so begin worship.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>ubhayam</em> = Oṃkāra + Haṃsa-mantra; <em>mudrā</em> = the <em>cin-mudrā</em> of v. 56 (Ayyangar's expansion explicitly mentions it)."],["Ayyangar","\"Having previously performed the Recaka, Pūraka and Kumbhaka, for one Yāma (three hours) everyday, O son! and having muttered the two (the Oṃkāra and the Haṃsa-mantra) (along with the application of his mind to their full import) he should, in the proper order, reverently worship (the Brahman), commencing with prostration and assuming the Cin-mudra.\""],["Sivananda","the prescribed <em>yāma</em> (= one quarter of day = 3 hours) is the <em>abhyāsa-pramāṇa</em>; he scales it down to 1 hour for modern householders."],["Krishnananda","the <em>yathā-kramam</em> is the key — <em>adhyātma-yajña</em> without sequence is <em>anavasthā</em>; structure is sanctity."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 57","title":"Eclipse of the sun = the direct sacrifice; sāyujya like water in water","sa":["सूर्यस्य ग्रहणं वत्स प्रत्यक्षयजनं स्मृतम् ।","ज्ञानात्सायुज्यमेवोक्तं तोये तोयं यथा तथा ॥ ५७ ॥"],"iast":["sūryasya grahaṇaṃ vatsa pratyakṣa-yajanaṃ smṛtam |","jñānāt sāyujyam evoktaṃ toye toyaṃ yathā tathā || 57 ||"],"words":[["sūryasya grahaṇam","m. gen. sg. + n. nom. sg.","the eclipse of the sun"],["vatsa","m. voc. sg.","O child"],["pratyakṣa-yajanam","n. nom. sg.","the direct sacrifice"],["smṛtam","n. nom. sg. pp.","is taught"],["jñānāt","n. abl. sg.","from knowledge"],["sāyujyam","n. nom. sg.","union (with Brahman)"],["eva uktam","indecl. + n. nom. sg. pp.","is declared indeed"],["toye toyam","n. loc. sg. + n. nom. sg.","water in water"],["yathā tathā","indecl.","just as / so"]],"lit":"\"The eclipse of the sun, O child, is taught as the direct sacrifice. From knowledge, <em>sāyujya</em> (union) is declared — as water in water, so (the Yogin in Brahman).\"","para":"The <em>prāṇa</em> (sun) is \"eclipsed\" by <em>kumbhaka</em> and the Suṣumnā-darkness — this inner eclipse <em>is</em> the direct sacrifice. From the <em>jñāna</em> it generates arises <em>sāyujya</em>: not mixture, but coincidence of like-with-like — water in water.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>sūrya-grahaṇa</em> = the <em>prāṇa-laya</em> in the <em>cid-ākāśa</em> — the sun (= prāṇa, mind-sun) is eclipsed by recognition of the <em>niṣkala</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"This eclipse of the Sun, O child! is thought of as the direct sacrifice. It has been said that from knowledge is attained the oneness (of the inner and the outer Ātman-s), even as water (becomes one) with water.\""],["Sivananda","<em>toye toyam</em> — the standard <em>sāyujya</em>-simile (Praśna 6.5, Kaiv 1.18); marks the Upaniṣad's Advaita commitment in plain language."],["Krishnananda","the simile precludes both <em>bheda</em> (water + water ≠ water + oil) and <em>bhedābheda</em> (no membrane); pure <em>abheda</em>."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ XII. The full Haṃsa-yoga (vv. 58–80)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 58","title":"Yoga removes suffering; engage it freed of all pain","sa":["एते गुणाः प्रवर्तन्ते योगाभ्यासकृतश्रमैः ।","तस्माद्योगं समादाय सर्वदुःखबहिष्कृतः ॥ ५८ ॥"],"iast":["ete guṇāḥ pravartante yogābhyāsa-kṛta-śramaiḥ |","tasmād yogaṃ samādāya sarva-duḥkha-bahiṣkṛtaḥ || 58 ||"],"words":[["ete guṇāḥ","m. nom. pl.","these qualities/results"],["pravartante","3 pl. pres. ātm.","come forth"],["yoga-abhyāsa-kṛta-śramaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by the labours of yogic practice"],["tasmāt","indecl. (abl.)","therefore"],["yogam samādāya","m. acc. sg. + ger.","having undertaken yoga"],["sarva-duḥkha-bahiṣkṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","one cast out of all suffering"]],"lit":"\"These qualities come forth by the labours of yogic practice. Therefore, having undertaken yoga, (be) one cast out of all suffering —\"","para":"<em>Praśaṃsā</em>: the previous siddhi/sāyujya promises arise from sustained <em>abhyāsa-śrama</em>. <em>Tasmāt yogaṃ samādāya</em> (therefore <em>take up</em> the yoga) — and the <em>sarva-duḥkha-bahiṣkṛti</em> will follow.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>śrama</em> word is key — these fruits do not arise from <em>vicāra</em> alone; <em>abhyāsa-śrama</em> (the work of practice) is required."],["Ayyangar","\"By the travails in the persistent practice of the course of Yoga, these good results flow (and the Yogin becomes a Jīvanmukta).\""],["Sivananda","read as the bridge from <em>jñāna-mārga</em> to <em>yoga-mārga</em> — Vedānta and Yoga as one <em>abhyāsa</em>."],["Krishnananda","<em>bahiṣkṛta</em> — <em>cast out</em>; passive; the Yogin does not throw suffering away, suffering departs <em>from</em> him."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 59","title":"Yoga-dhyāna into tanmayatā; uttering the Haṃsa-mantra","sa":["योगध्यानं सदा कृत्वा ज्ञानं तन्मयतां व्रजेत् ।","ज्ञानात्स्वरूपं परमं हंसमन्त्रं समुच्चरेत् ॥ ५९ ॥"],"iast":["yoga-dhyānaṃ sadā kṛtvā jñānaṃ tanmayatāṃ vrajet |","jñānāt svarūpaṃ paramaṃ haṃsa-mantraṃ samuccaret || 59 ||"],"words":[["yoga-dhyānam","n. acc. sg.","yogic meditation"],["sadā kṛtvā","indecl. + ger.","always having performed"],["jñānam","n. nom. sg.","knowledge"],["tanmayatām","f. acc. sg.","the state-of-being-That"],["vrajet","3 sg. opt. of vraj","should go-toward"],["jñānāt","n. abl. sg.","from knowledge"],["sva-rūpam paramam","n. acc. sg.","(one's) own supreme form"],["haṃsa-mantram","m. acc. sg.","the Haṃsa-mantra"],["samuccaret","3 sg. opt. of sam-ud-car","should fully utter"]],"lit":"\"Performing yoga-meditation always, the knowledge should pass into <em>tanmayatā</em> (identity with That); from knowledge, the supreme own-form (= Haṃsa-mantra) — one should fully utter the Haṃsa-mantra.\"","para":"Sequence: <em>yoga-dhyāna</em> → <em>jñāna</em> → <em>tanmayatā</em>. Then, in <em>tanmayatā</em>, <em>japa</em> turns into <em>udghoṣa</em> — the Haṃsa-mantra is <em>uttered as one's own form</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>tanmayatā</em> is the <em>brahma-bhāva</em>; the Haṃsa-mantra \"uttered\" thereafter is no longer <em>japa</em> in the ordinary sense — the breath <em>itself</em> utters it as the Yogin's <em>svarūpa</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"he should always practice Yogic meditation and acquire wisdom and, simultaneously with the dawning of wisdom, become merged with the Brahman.\""],["Sivananda","this is the <em>ajapā-japa</em> threshold — <em>japa</em> without a <em>japaka</em>."],["Krishnananda","<em>vrajet</em> (should <em>go</em>) — not <em>bhavet</em> (should <em>become</em>); the <em>tanmayatā</em> is approached, never possessed."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 60","title":"The Haṃsa is acyuta, supreme truth, supreme power","sa":["प्राणिनां देहमध्ये तु स्थितो हंसः सदाच्युतः ।","हंस एव परं सत्यं हंस एव तु शक्तिकम् ॥ ६० ॥"],"iast":["prāṇināṃ deha-madhye tu sthito haṃsaḥ sadācyutaḥ |","haṃsa eva paraṃ satyaṃ haṃsa eva tu śaktikam || 60 ||"],"words":[["prāṇinām","m. gen. pl.","of living beings"],["deha-madhye","m. loc. sg.","in the middle of the body"],["tu sthitaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg. pp.","abides"],["haṃsaḥ sadā acyutaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa, ever unfallen"],["haṃsaḥ eva paraṃ satyam","m. + n. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa alone (is) the supreme Truth"],["haṃsaḥ eva tu śaktikam","m. + n. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa alone is the (supreme) power"]],"lit":"\"In the middle of the body of (all) living beings the Haṃsa abides, ever unfallen. The Haṃsa alone is the supreme Truth; the Haṃsa alone is the (supreme) Power.\"","para":"First of three <em>paraṃ-stuti</em> verses (60–62) — the Haṃsa is identified with every supreme: Truth, Power, Word, Wisdom, Rudra, <em>parātpara</em>, Maheśvara.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>sadā acyutaḥ</em> — the Haṃsa never falls (i.e., is never displaced from its <em>deha-madhya</em> seat by any <em>avasthā</em>); <em>acyuta</em> is a deliberate pun on Viṣṇu's epithet."],["Ayyangar","\"The Haṃsa, the unswerving (Brahman) has his abode (in the heart), in the middle of the body of all living beings.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>sarva-prāṇi-haṃsa</em> doctrine is the warrant for universal compassion — the <em>same</em> Haṃsa is in all."],["Krishnananda","<em>paraṃ satyam</em> and <em>śaktikam</em> together = <em>cit-śakti-aikya</em>; knowledge-and-power are not two predicates but the same Haṃsa."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 61","title":"Haṃsa = supreme vākya, supreme vādika, supreme Rudra","sa":["हंस एव परं वाक्यं हंस एव तु वादिकम् ।","हंस एव परो रुद्रो हंस एव परात्परम् ॥ ६१ ॥"],"iast":["haṃsa eva paraṃ vākyaṃ haṃsa eva tu vādikam |","haṃsa eva paro rudro haṃsa eva parātparam || 61 ||"],"words":[["haṃsaḥ eva paraṃ vākyam","m. + n. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa alone (is) the supreme Word (= mahā-vākya)"],["haṃsaḥ eva tu vādikam","m. + n. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa alone is the supreme speech-act"],["haṃsaḥ eva paro rudraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa alone (is) supreme Rudra"],["haṃsaḥ eva parātparam","m. + n. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa alone (is) higher-than-the-high"]],"lit":"\"The Haṃsa alone is the supreme Word; the Haṃsa alone is (its) utterance; the Haṃsa alone is the supreme Rudra; the Haṃsa alone is the higher-than-high.\"","para":"A four-fold <em>abhinna-stuti</em> extending v. 60. <em>Vākyam</em> = the supreme <em>mahā-vākya</em> (e.g., <em>Tat tvam asi</em>); <em>vādikam</em> = the very act of uttering it; <em>rudra</em> (the <em>cosmic dissolver</em>); <em>parātpara</em> (the <em>limit of limits</em>).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the verse fuses <em>vāc</em> and <em>prāṇa</em> — the Haṃsa is both the <em>mahā-vākya</em> and its <em>utterance</em>, just as it is both <em>jñāna</em> and <em>jñātṛ</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"The Haṃsa alone is the supreme (Vedic) Text. The Haṃsa alone has the sanction of the Vedas. The Haṃsa alone is the absolute Rudra. The Haṃsa alone transcends the transcendent.\""],["Sivananda","his standing <em>Haṃsa-stuti</em>; recited as a <em>dhyāna-śloka</em> in DLS <em>Upāsanā</em> sessions."],["Krishnananda","<em>para Rudra</em> — Rudra is invoked here precisely as <em>laya-deva</em>; the Haṃsa is the <em>layakṛt</em> of the <em>kalpa-process</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 62","title":"Haṃsa = Maheśvara amid all gods; the 51 tattvas, 51 letters","sa":["सर्वदेवस्य मध्यस्थो हंस एव महेश्वरः ।","पृथिव्यादिशिवान्तं तु अकाराद्याश्च वर्णकाः ॥ ६२ ॥"],"iast":["sarva-devasya madhya-stho haṃsa eva maheśvaraḥ |","pṛthivyādi-śivāntaṃ tu akārādyāś ca varṇakāḥ || 62 ||"],"words":[["sarva-devasya","m. gen. sg.","of all gods"],["madhya-sthaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","standing in the middle"],["haṃsaḥ eva maheśvaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa alone (is) Maheśvara"],["pṛthivī-ādi-śiva-antam","n. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","(the series) beginning-from-earth-ending-at-Śiva"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["a-kāra-ādyāḥ","m. nom. pl. (bahuvr.)","(the letters) beginning with A"],["ca","conj.","and"],["varṇakāḥ","m. nom. pl.","(the) syllables"]],"lit":"\"Standing in the middle of all gods, the Haṃsa alone is Maheśvara. (The series of tattvas) from earth up to Śiva, and the (alphabet) syllables beginning with A — (cont. v. 63).\"","para":"Two intersecting fifty-one-fold series are about to be identified with Haṃsa: the <em>tattva</em>-series (Pṛthivī … Śiva, 36 in Trika or up to 51 in extended schemes) and the <em>Sanskrit alphabet</em> (A … Kṣa, the 51 <em>mātṛkā</em>-letters).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this is the <em>mātṛkā-vidyā</em> — the 51-letter alphabet is the 51-fold <em>tattva</em>-set; the Haṃsa stands as <em>the</em> whole."],["Ayyangar","\"The Haṃsa alone is the fifty-one eternal verities commencing from the Pṛthivī (Earth) and ending with Śiva, He alone the fifty-one letters commencing from 'A' and ending with the Kūṭa (Kṣa)…\""],["Sivananda","the <em>mātṛkā</em> meditation is <em>Mantra-Yoga</em> proper — the alphabet itself is divinity."],["Krishnananda","the equation invites the <em>Pratyabhijñā</em> school's <em>Mātṛkā-cakra-viveka</em>; the Upaniṣad is closer to non-dual Tantra than the Yoga-Upaniṣad surface suggests."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 63","title":"Kūṭānta; the Haṃsa as Mātṛkā; no mantra without letters","sa":["कूटान्ता हंस एव स्यान्मातृकेति व्यवस्थिताः ।","मातृकारहितं मन्त्रमादिशन्ते न कुत्रचित् ॥ ६३ ॥"],"iast":["kūṭāntā haṃsa eva syān mātṛketi vyavasthitāḥ |","mātṛkā-rahitaṃ mantram ādiśante na kutracit || 63 ||"],"words":[["kūṭa-antāḥ","m. nom. pl. (bahuvr.)","(whose) end is Kūṭa (= Kṣa)"],["haṃsaḥ eva","m. nom. sg.","(is) the Haṃsa alone"],["syāt","3 sg. opt. of as","would be"],["mātṛkā iti","f. nom. sg. + indecl.","(these are) called Mātṛkā"],["vyavasthitāḥ","f. nom. pl. pp.","established"],["mātṛkā-rahitam","n. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","devoid of mātṛkā"],["mantram","m. acc. sg.","mantra"],["ādiśante","3 pl. pres. ātm. of ā-diś","do they teach"],["na kutracit","indecl.","nowhere"]],"lit":"\"(All these), ending in Kūṭa, are the Haṃsa alone — established as 'Mātṛkā'. A mantra devoid of Mātṛkā (i.e., having no alphabet-letters) — nowhere do (the teachers) teach (such a thing).\"","para":"Closes the equation: the 51 letters = <em>Mātṛkā</em>; no mantra can lack them. The Haṃsa = Mātṛkā = mantra-source. (This anticipates the <em>aham</em>-string of vv. 81–110, which is constructed as a <em>vyāhṛti</em> of the alphabet.)","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","without Mātṛkā there can be no <em>mantroccāraṇa</em> — even the supposed \"soundless\" mantras (<em>nāda</em>) take their pattern from the alphabet's <em>prāṇa-vinyāsa</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"Nowhere do people recognize a Mantra made up of sounds which have no counterparts in the Alphabet.\""],["Sivananda","his <em>Mantra Yoga</em> premise — <em>every</em> mantra is <em>mātṛkā</em>-based; the <em>bīja</em>-letters are the basis of all <em>japa</em>."],["Krishnananda","the verse implies a deep philosophical claim — <em>sound-language is consciousness's morphology</em>; no consciousness without articulation, however subtle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 64","title":"Take refuge in Dakṣiṇāmurti; Jñāna-mudrā","sa":["हंसज्योतिरनूपम्यं मध्ये देवं व्यवस्थितम् ।","दक्षिणामुखमाश्रित्य ज्ञानमुद्रां प्रकल्पयेत् ॥ ६४ ॥"],"iast":["haṃsa-jyotir anūpamyaṃ madhye devaṃ vyavasthitam |","dakṣiṇāmukham āśritya jñāna-mudrāṃ prakalpayet || 64 ||"],"words":[["haṃsa-jyotiḥ","n. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","the Haṃsa-radiance"],["anūpamyam","n. acc. sg.","peerless"],["madhye","n. loc. sg.","in the middle"],["devam","m. acc. sg.","the deity"],["vyavasthitam","m. acc. sg. pp.","established"],["dakṣiṇā-mukham","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","facing-south"],["āśritya","ger. of ā-śri","having taken refuge"],["jñāna-mudrām","f. acc. sg.","the jñāna-mudrā"],["prakalpayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of pra-kḷp","should perform"]],"lit":"\"Taking refuge in the peerless Haṃsa-radiance — the deity established in the middle, facing south — one should perform the Jñāna-mudrā.\"","para":"The <em>Dakṣiṇāmurti</em>-form of Śiva (south-facing, jñāna-mudrā with thumb-and-index circle) is now identified with the inner Haṃsa-radiance: the <em>upāsya</em> is interiorised.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the southward-facing deity is <em>Dakṣiṇāmurti</em> — Śiva as silent jñāna-Guru; the <em>Jñāna-mudrā</em> he displays is to be physically performed by the yogin."],["Ayyangar","\"One should seek protection at the hands of that peerless Haṃsa radiance (Dakṣiṇā-mūrti) well placed in the midst of the gods and facing the South and assume the Jñāna-mudrā posture.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>jñāna-mudrā</em> (tip-of-thumb to tip-of-index, other three extended) is to be held during <em>Haṃsa-japa</em> — it locks <em>aham</em> (thumb) to <em>tvam</em> (index) in non-difference."],["Krishnananda","Dakṣiṇāmurti = the Guru in his <em>paramārthika</em> aspect — silent, transmissive, identical with the Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 65","title":"Samādhi; crystal-clear divine form","sa":["सदा समाधिं कुर्वीत हंसमन्त्रमनुस्मरन् ।","निर्मलस्फटिकाकारं दिव्यरूपमनुत्तमम् ॥ ६५ ॥"],"iast":["sadā samādhiṃ kurvīta haṃsa-mantram anusmaran |","nirmala-sphaṭikākāraṃ divya-rūpam anuttamam || 65 ||"],"words":[["sadā","indecl.","always"],["samādhim","m. acc. sg.","samādhi"],["kurvīta","3 sg. opt. ātm. of kṛ","one should perform"],["haṃsa-mantram","m. acc. sg.","the Haṃsa-mantra"],["anusmaran","m. nom. sg. pres. part.","(while) remembering"],["nirmala-sphaṭika-ākāram","n. acc. sg.","(of) stainless-crystal-form"],["divya-rūpam","n. acc. sg.","divine form"],["anuttamam","n. acc. sg.","unsurpassed"]],"lit":"\"One should always perform samādhi, remembering the Haṃsa-mantra — (visualising) the unsurpassed divine form, of the shape of stainless crystal.\"","para":"The <em>dhyāna-bimba</em> (image of meditation) is a <em>flawless-crystal divine form</em>; the <em>japa</em> (Haṃsa) is held within the <em>samādhi</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>sphaṭika</em>-image is <em>transparent</em> — that is, <em>colourless</em> — fitting the <em>nirguṇa</em>-imagery while admitting a meditative form."],["Ayyangar","literal — \"ever memorizing the Haṃsa-mantra reflecting on that clear crystal-like form, the divine form that is peerless.\""],["Sivananda","the crystal <em>bimba</em> is the entry to <em>savikalpa</em> → <em>nirvikalpa</em>; once crystal-clarity is stable, the form dissolves into its own transparency."],["Krishnananda","\"stainless crystal\" — the meditative form is <em>māyā-free</em>; the absence of <em>rāga-dveṣa</em> colour is what crystal symbolises."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 66","title":"The Haṃsa as Jñāna-mudrā-form; the five vāyus","sa":["मध्यदेशे परं हंसं ज्ञानमुद्रात्मरूपकम् ।","प्राणोऽपानः समानश्चोदानव्यानौ च वायवः ॥ ६६ ॥"],"iast":["madhya-deśe paraṃ haṃsaṃ jñāna-mudrātma-rūpakam |","prāṇo'pānaḥ samānaś codāna-vyānau ca vāyavaḥ || 66 ||"],"words":[["madhya-deśe","m. loc. sg.","in the middle region (of the body)"],["paraṃ haṃsam","m. acc. sg.","the supreme Haṃsa"],["jñāna-mudrā-ātma-rūpakam","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","of-the-form-of-Self-as-jñāna-mudrā"],["prāṇaḥ apānaḥ samānaḥ","m. nom. sg. (×3)","prāṇa, apāna, samāna"],["ca udāna-vyānau ca vāyavaḥ","conj. + m. nom. du. + m. nom. pl.","and udāna, vyāna — these are the (five) vāyus"]],"lit":"\"(Visualise) the supreme Haṃsa in the middle region, of the form of Self-as-Jñāna-mudrā. <em>Prāṇa, Apāna, Samāna, Udāna, Vyāna</em> — (these are the) vāyu-s …\"","para":"Continues the <em>dhyāna-bimba</em> (middle region, Haṃsa as the <em>jñāna-mudrā-puruṣa</em>) and then enumerates the five <em>prāṇa-vāyu-s</em>. The next verse will give the second pentad.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>madhya-deśa</em> is the heart-centre; the Haṃsa as <em>jñāna-mudrā-rūpa</em> means the deity <em>holds the jñāna-mudrā as its own form</em>, not as an outward gesture."],["Ayyangar","\"Madhya-deśe paraṃ haṃsaṃ jñāna-mudrātma-rūpakam — the transcendent Haṃsa of the form of the Jñāna-mudra.\""],["Sivananda","the standard <em>prāṇa-pañcaka</em> (also vv. 67–68) maps onto his <em>prāṇāyāma</em> schema directly."],["Krishnananda","the Haṃsa-as-<em>jñāna-mudrā</em> indicates self-reference — knowledge that gestures at itself; the mudrā is consciousness's own posture."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 67","title":"Five karmendriyas and the five upa-prāṇas","sa":["पञ्चकर्मेन्द्रियैरुक्ताः क्रियाशक्तिबलोद्यताः ।","नागः कूर्मश्च कृकरो देवदत्तो धनञ्जयः ॥ ६७ ॥"],"iast":["pañca-karmendriyair uktāḥ kriyā-śakti-balodyatāḥ |","nāgaḥ kūrmaś ca kṛkaro devadatto dhanañjayaḥ || 67 ||"],"words":[["pañca-karma-indriyaiḥ","n. instr. pl. (karmadh.)","(yoked) with the five organs-of-action"],["uktāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","are declared"],["kriyā-śakti-bala-udyatāḥ","m. nom. pl. (bahuvr.)","mobilised-by-the-power-of-action"],["nāgaḥ kūrmaḥ ca kṛkaraḥ devadattaḥ dhanañjayaḥ","m. nom. sg. (×5)","Nāga, Kūrma, Kṛkara, Devadatta, Dhanañjaya"]],"lit":"\"(The five vāyus of v. 66) are declared (to be yoked) with the five karmendriya-s, mobilised by the power-of-action. (And the five upa-prāṇa-s:) Nāga, Kūrma, Kṛkara, Devadatta, Dhanañjaya …\"","para":"The first pentad (Prāṇa, Apāna, etc.) is paired with the five <em>karmendriya-s</em> (mouth, hands, feet, anus, genitals) under the rubric of <em>kriyā-śakti</em>. The second pentad (<em>upa-prāṇa-s</em>) is now enumerated: <em>Nāga</em> (eructation), <em>Kūrma</em> (blinking), <em>Kṛkara</em> (sneezing), <em>Devadatta</em> (yawning), <em>Dhanañjaya</em> (post-mortem swelling).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this is standard <em>Yoga-Sūtra-bhāṣya</em> schema (II.50–53), here interiorised into the Haṃsa."],["Ayyangar","literal listing."],["Sivananda","the <em>upa-prāṇa-s</em> deserve practical attention — most yogins ignore them; mastery of <em>Nāga</em> etc. via specific <em>kuṃbhakas</em> yields specific siddhi-s."],["Krishnananda","the doctrinal point — these are not \"ten breaths\" but ten <em>vāyu-functions</em>, all played by the one <em>prāṇa</em>; multiplicity is functional, not substantial."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 68","title":"Five jñānendriyas; fire and sun at the navel","sa":["पञ्चज्ञानेन्द्रियैर्युक्ता ज्ञानशक्तिबलोद्यताः ।","पावकः शक्तिमध्ये तु नाभिचक्रे रविः स्थितः ॥ ६८ ॥"],"iast":["pañca-jñānendriyair yuktā jñāna-śakti-balodyatāḥ |","pāvakaḥ śakti-madhye tu nābhi-cakre raviḥ sthitaḥ || 68 ||"],"words":[["pañca-jñāna-indriyaiḥ","n. instr. pl.","(yoked) with the five organs-of-knowledge"],["yuktāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","yoked"],["jñāna-śakti-bala-udyatāḥ","m. nom. pl. (bahuvr.)","mobilised-by-the-power-of-knowledge"],["pāvakaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the fire"],["śakti-madhye","f. loc. sg.","in the middle of the (two) śakti-s"],["tu","indecl.","indeed"],["nābhi-cakre","n. loc. sg.","in the navel-cakra"],["raviḥ","m. nom. sg.","the sun"],["sthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","abides"]],"lit":"\"The (five upa-prāṇa-s) are yoked with the five jñānendriya-s, mobilised by the power-of-knowledge. The fire abides in the middle of the two śakti-s (kriyā/jñāna); in the navel-cakra, the sun abides.\"","para":"The second pentad is paired with the <em>jñānendriya-s</em> (ear, skin, eye, tongue, nose) under <em>jñāna-śakti</em>. The Haṃsa-fire stands <em>between</em> the two śakti-s (<em>kriyā</em> and <em>jñāna</em>); the <em>nābhi-cakra</em> (maṇipūra) contains the sun.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>fire-between-two-śaktis</em> image: <em>kriyā</em> and <em>jñāna</em> are not opposed but <em>flank</em> the <em>agni</em> of awareness; this is a Trika-style reading."],["Ayyangar","\"the Haṃsa alone stands in the form of the fire in the Mūlādhāra, midway between the two Śakti-s.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>nābhi-sūrya</em> is what <em>Ujjāyī</em> + <em>Bhastrikā</em> activate; the verse maps the experiential heating-up in the navel during these prāṇāyāmas."],["Krishnananda","the <em>śakti-madhye</em> fire = the <em>avadhūta-prāṇa</em> — the prāṇa stripped of identification with either <em>karma</em> or <em>jñāna</em> poles."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 69","title":"Khecarī/Bandha-mudrā; fire in A, U, M","sa":["बन्धमुद्रा कृता येन नासाग्रे तु स्वलोचने ।","अकारेवह्निरित्याहुरुकारे हृदि संस्थितः ॥ ६९ ॥"],"iast":["bandha-mudrā kṛtā yena nāsāgre tu svalocane |","akāre vahnir ity āhur ukāre hṛdi saṃsthitaḥ || 69 ||"],"words":[["bandha-mudrā","f. nom. sg.","the (Khecarī-)bandha-mudrā"],["kṛtā","f. nom. sg. pp.","(is) performed"],["yena","rel. m. instr. sg.","by whom"],["nāsa-agre","n. loc. sg.","at the nose-tip"],["tu sva-locane","indecl. + n. loc. du.","and (with) the own-eyes (fixed)"],["a-kāre","m. loc. sg.","in (the letter) A"],["vahniḥ","m. nom. sg.","(there is) fire"],["iti āhuḥ","indecl. + 3 pl. pf.","so they say"],["u-kāre","m. loc. sg.","in U"],["hṛdi","n. loc. sg.","in the heart"],["saṃsthitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","(it) abides"]],"lit":"\"By whom the <em>bandha-mudrā</em> is performed — with eyes (fixed) at the nose-tip — they say there is fire in (the letter) A; in U, (the fire) abides in the heart …\"","para":"The full Haṃsa-yoga begins with a <em>bandha-mudrā</em> (the inward-locking gesture — Khecarī-Mūlabandha-Jālandhara complex, of which v. 72 will name Jālandhara explicitly) and <em>nāsāgra-dṛṣṭi</em>. Then a tri-cakric fire-map: fire in <em>A</em> (= Mūlādhāra), fire in <em>U</em> (= heart), and (next verse) fire in <em>M</em> (= bhrū-madhya).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","this is a <em>kuṇḍalinī-jāgaraṇa</em> sequence: <em>bandha-mudrā</em> + <em>nāsāgra</em> gaze + tri-cakric <em>agni-bhāvanā</em>. The fire is one; its three <em>adhiṣṭhāna-s</em> differ."],["Ayyangar","\"They say that fire stands in 'A' (the Mūlādhāra), in 'U', the heart, and 'M', the middle of the eye-brows.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>bandha-trayam</em> (Mūla, Uḍḍiyāna, Jālandhara) is the practical engine; combined with this <em>agni-bhāvanā</em> it produces <em>kuṇḍalinī-jāgaraṇa</em> in months."],["Krishnananda","the <em>yena</em> construction is significant — only <em>by</em> the <em>bandha-yogin</em> is the fire \"seen\" in these three loci; outside that yoga, the loci are anatomical only."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 70","title":"Fire in M; the three granthis","sa":["मकारे च भ्रुवोर्मध्ये प्राणशक्त्या प्रबोधयेत् ।","ब्रह्मग्रन्थिरकारे च विष्णुग्रन्थिर्हृदि स्थितः ॥ ७० ॥"],"iast":["makāre ca bhruvor madhye prāṇa-śaktyā prabodhayet |","brahma-granthir akāre ca viṣṇu-granthir hṛdi sthitaḥ || 70 ||"],"words":[["ma-kāre","m. loc. sg.","in M"],["ca bhruvor madhye","conj. + m. gen. du. + m. loc. sg.","and in the middle of the brows"],["prāṇa-śaktyā","f. instr. sg.","by the prāṇa-power"],["prabodhayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of pra-budh","one should awaken"],["brahma-granthiḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Brahmā-knot"],["a-kāre","m. loc. sg.","in A"],["ca viṣṇu-granthiḥ","conj. + m. nom. sg.","and the Viṣṇu-knot"],["hṛdi sthitaḥ","m. loc. sg. + m. nom. sg. pp.","abides in the heart"]],"lit":"\"And in M — in the middle of the brows — (the fire) one should awaken by the <em>prāṇa-śakti</em>. The Brahmā-granthi (is) in A; the Viṣṇu-granthi abides in the heart.\"","para":"Completes the tri-cakric fire-map (fire in <em>M</em> = brow-centre). Then introduces the <em>three granthi-s</em>: <em>Brahma-granthi</em> at A (Mūlādhāra), <em>Viṣṇu-granthi</em> at U (Heart). (Rudra-granthi in v. 71.)","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>granthi-trayam</em> is the <em>kanda-bandhana</em> (knotted-binding) of the Suṣumnā; <em>Brahmā-granthi</em> binds prāṇa to the <em>kāma-kośa</em>, <em>Viṣṇu</em> to the <em>karma-kośa</em>, <em>Rudra</em> to the <em>jñāna-kośa</em> — three successive <em>moha</em>-locks."],["Ayyangar","\"the knot of Brahman is situated in 'A', the knot of Viṣṇu in 'U' and the knot of Rudra in 'M'.\""],["Sivananda","his <em>Kuṇḍalinī Yoga</em> treats the <em>granthi-bheda</em> as the central technical accomplishment of <em>Haṭha</em>-yoga."],["Krishnananda","the three granthis are <em>avidyā</em> (Brahmā-knot = body-mind), <em>kāma</em> (Viṣṇu-knot = relational world), <em>karma</em> (Rudra-knot = identification with agency)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 71","title":"Rudra-granthi; A=Brahmā, U=Viṣṇu, M=Rudra","sa":["रुद्रग्रन्थिर्भ्रुवोर्मध्ये भिद्यतेऽक्षरवायुना ।","अकारे संस्थितो ब्रह्मा उकारे विष्णुरास्थितः ॥ ७१ ॥"],"iast":["rudra-granthir bhruvor madhye bhidyate'kṣara-vāyunā |","akāre saṃsthito brahmā ukāre viṣṇur āsthitaḥ || 71 ||"],"words":[["rudra-granthiḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Rudra-knot"],["bhruvor madhye","m. gen. du. + m. loc. sg.","in the middle of the brows"],["bhidyate","3 sg. pres. pass. of bhid","is burst-asunder"],["akṣara-vāyunā","m. instr. sg.","by the (Praṇava-)imperishable-wind"],["a-kāre","m. loc. sg.","in A"],["saṃsthitaḥ brahmā","m. nom. sg.","(the god) Brahmā is established"],["u-kāre","m. loc. sg.","in U"],["viṣṇuḥ āsthitaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Viṣṇu is stationed"]],"lit":"\"The Rudra-knot, in the middle of the brows, is burst-asunder by the <em>akṣara-vāyu</em> (the wind of the Praṇava). Brahmā is established in A; Viṣṇu is stationed in U.\"","para":"The third granthi (Rudra, at bhrū-madhya) is pierced by the <em>akṣara-vāyu</em> — the <em>prāṇa</em> energised by Praṇava-japa. Then deity-pairings: A-Brahmā, U-Viṣṇu (verse continues into 72).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>akṣara-vāyu</em> deliberately ambiguates — \"wind of the imperishable letter\" and \"wind of imperishable consciousness\"; the same <em>prāṇa</em> is both."],["Ayyangar","\"Rudra-granthir bhruvor madhye bhidyate akṣara-vāyunā — the Rudra knot in the middle of the brows is burst asunder by the wind of the imperishable (Praṇava).\""],["Sivananda","this verse is the diagnostic for <em>granthi-bheda</em>: the experiential signature is a sharp release at the brow-centre, often with a flash of light."],["Krishnananda","the <em>bheda</em> is recognitional — the granthi is not a physical knot but a psychological <em>abhiniveśa</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 72","title":"M = Rudra; throat-constriction by Jālandhara","sa":["मकारे संस्थितो रुद्रस्ततोऽस्यान्तः परात्परः ।","कण्ठं सङ्कुच्य नाड्यादौ स्तम्भिते येन शक्तितः ॥ ७२ ॥"],"iast":["makāre saṃsthito rudras tato'syāntaḥ parātparaḥ |","kaṇṭhaṃ saṅkucya nāḍyādau stambhite yena śaktitaḥ || 72 ||"],"words":[["ma-kāre","m. loc. sg.","in M"],["saṃsthitaḥ rudraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Rudra (is) established"],["tataḥ asya antaḥ parātparaḥ","indecl. + pron. m. gen. sg. + m. nom. sg.","beyond him (M-Rudra) is the higher-than-the-high"],["kaṇṭham saṅkucya","m. acc. sg. + ger.","having constricted the throat"],["nāḍī-ādau","f. loc. sg.","in the beginning of the (Suṣumnā-)nāḍī"],["stambhite","n. loc. sg. pp. (locative absolute)","when (the prāṇa) is arrested"],["yena","rel. m. instr. sg.","by whom"],["śaktitaḥ","indecl. (abl.)","by power"]],"lit":"\"In M, Rudra is established; beyond him is the higher-than-the-high. (Now,) by whom — by power — when the prāṇa is arrested at the root of the (Suṣumnā-)nāḍī by constricting the throat …\"","para":"A/U/M completed: Brahmā/Viṣṇu/Rudra. Beyond all three lies the <em>parātpara</em>. Then technical: by constricting the throat (= <em>Jālandhara-bandha</em>) and arresting prāṇa at the <em>nāḍī-mūla</em> (= <em>Mūlabandha</em>) — sentence continues to v. 73.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>parātpara</em> beyond M-Rudra is the same <em>parātpara</em> of v. 40; the prāṇa-yoga and the <em>vidyā-vicāra</em> converge on the same target."],["Ayyangar","\"When, by the Yogin assuming the Siddhāsana posture, after constricting the throat with the Jālandhara-mudrā, the root of the Kuṇḍalinī-nāḍī, (as well as the vital air, introspection and the like) is arrested with the help of the Kuṇḍalinī-śakti …\""],["Sivananda","the <em>Jālandhara + Mūla + (implicit) Uḍḍiyāna</em> bandha-trayam is the <em>kuṃbhaka-yoga-lakṣaṇa</em>."],["Krishnananda","\"<em>śaktitaḥ</em>\" — power must already be present; the bandhas concentrate but do not create the <em>kuṇḍalinī-śakti</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 73","title":"Tongue pressed; the ṣoḍaśī ascending; trikuṭa, golakha, nikhara","sa":["रसना पीड्यमानेयं षोडशी वोर्ध्वगामिनी ।","त्रिकूटं त्रिविधा चैव गोलाखं निखरं तथा ॥ ७३ ॥"],"iast":["rasanā pīḍyamāneyaṃ ṣoḍaśī vordhva-gāminī |","tri-kūṭaṃ tri-vidhā caiva golākhaṃ nikharaṃ tathā || 73 ||"],"words":[["rasanā","f. nom. sg.","the tongue"],["pīḍyamānā iyam","f. nom. sg. pres. pass. part. + pron.","this (one), being pressed"],["ṣoḍaśī vā","f. nom. sg. + indecl.","or the sixteen-fold (Praṇava)"],["ūrdhva-gāminī","f. nom. sg.","upward-moving"],["tri-kūṭam","n. nom. sg.","(the place called) Tri-kūṭa"],["tri-vidhā","f. nom. sg.","three-fold"],["ca eva","indecl.","and indeed"],["golākham","n. nom. sg.","(the) Golākha"],["nikharam","n. nom. sg.","(the) Nikhara"],["tathā","indecl.","likewise"]],"lit":"\"This tongue, (being) pressed — or the sixteen-fold (Praṇava) — moves upward (toward) the three-fold <em>Tri-kūṭa</em>, the <em>Golākha</em>, and so the <em>Nikhara</em> —\"","para":"With <em>Khecarī</em>-tongue-press, the <em>ṣoḍaśī</em> (sixteen-mātrā Praṇava) ascends through three subtle \"summits\": <em>Tri-kūṭa</em> (the bhrū-madhya junction of three nāḍīs), <em>Golākha</em> (primordial ākāśa), <em>Nikhara</em> (cid-ākāśa). Verse 74 will add Tri-śaṅkha.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>Tri-kūṭa</em> = junction of Iḍā-Piṅgalā-Suṣumnā at bhrū-madhya; <em>Golākha</em> = the sphere-of-primordial-Ākāśa; <em>Nikhara</em> = sky of <em>cit</em>. Three nested <em>ākāśa-s</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"Then the Tri-kūṭa (the junction of the three Nāḍī-s in the middle of the brow) leading to the threefold directions of Golākha (the primordial Ether), Nikhara (the Cid-ākāśa) and Tri-śaṅkha (the Brahman devoid of the three kinds of pleasure and pain generated by desire, non-desire and other-desire than for itself)…\""],["Sivananda","the <em>Khecarī</em>-experience: tongue presses; <em>ojas</em> drips; <em>amṛta</em> tastes sweet; <em>jyoti</em> opens at Tri-kūṭa. He records this in <em>Kundalini Yoga</em>."],["Krishnananda","these three \"summits\" are subjectivity's deepening transparencies — not anatomical, not even subtle-anatomical, but <em>cit-darśana-bhūmi-s</em>."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 74","title":"Tri-śaṅkha-vajra-Oṃkāra; piercing the moon-disc","sa":["त्रिशङ्खवज्रमोङ्कारमूर्ध्वनालं भ्रुवोर्मुखम् ।","कुण्डलीं चालयन्प्राणान्भेदयन्शशिमण्डलम् ॥ ७४ ॥"],"iast":["tri-śaṅkha-vajram oṅkāram ūrdhva-nālaṃ bhruvor mukham |","kuṇḍalīṃ cālayan prāṇān bhedayan śaśi-maṇḍalam || 74 ||"],"words":[["tri-śaṅkha-vajram","m. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","the tri-śaṅkha-vajra (Praṇava)"],["oṅkāram","m. acc. sg.","the Oṃkāra"],["ūrdhva-nālam","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","upward-stalked"],["bhruvor mukham","m. gen. du. + m. acc. sg.","(entering) the orifice between the brows"],["kuṇḍalīm cālayan","f. acc. sg. + m. nom. sg. pres. part.","(the yogin) moving the Kuṇḍalinī"],["prāṇān bhedayan","m. acc. pl. + caus. part.","piercing the prāṇas (= channels)"],["śaśi-maṇḍalam","n. acc. sg.","the moon-disc"]],"lit":"\"The adamantine (vajra) tri-śaṅkha Oṃkāra, upward-stalked, (enters) the orifice between the brows — (the yogin) moving the Kuṇḍalinī, piercing the prāṇas (= channels), (and) the moon-disc —\"","para":"The Praṇava is now a <em>vajra</em> (adamantine bolt), three-pointed (<em>tri-śaṅkha</em>), upward-shafted, piercing the brow-orifice. Simultaneously the yogin moves Kuṇḍalinī up, pierces the prāṇa-channels, and breaks through the <em>śaśi-maṇḍala</em> (the lunar disc of the <em>soma-cakra</em> / <em>candra-kalā</em>).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>tri-śaṅkha</em> = Brahman free of <em>icchā-aniccha-anya-icchā</em> (Ayyangar's gloss preserves this); <em>vajra</em> = unbreakable. The Praṇava as <em>vajra</em> is the yogin's instrument."],["Ayyangar","literal: \"the adamantine Oṃkāra (enter) the orifice between the two eyebrows with the duct leading upwards. Through this, forcing the Kuṇḍalinī and the vital airs, bursting through the region of the moon…\""],["Sivananda","the <em>śaśi-maṇḍala</em> bursting is the <em>amṛta-srāva</em> — the nectar of the soma-cakra begins to drip, perceived as cool sweetness at palate."],["Krishnananda","the imagery is <em>Vajra-yāna</em>-cognate, evidence of the <em>yoga-sāmagrī</em>'s pan-Indian common stock."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 75","title":"Vajra-kumbhakas; bind the nine gates; mind on prāṇa","sa":["साधयन्वज्रकुम्भानि नवद्वाराणि बन्धयेत् ।","सुमनःपवनारूढः सरागो निर्गुणस्तथा ॥ ७५ ॥"],"iast":["sādhayan vajra-kumbhāni nava-dvārāṇi bandhayet |","sumanaḥ-pavanārūḍhaḥ sa-rāgo nirguṇas tathā || 75 ||"],"words":[["sādhayan","m. nom. sg. pres. part. caus.","(the yogin) accomplishing"],["vajra-kumbhāni","n. acc. pl. (karmadh.)","the adamantine kumbhakas"],["nava-dvārāṇi","n. acc. pl.","the nine gates (of the body)"],["bandhayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of bandh","should bind"],["su-manaḥ-pavana-ārūḍhaḥ","m. nom. sg. (bahuvr.)","mounted on the well-mind-and-the-wind"],["sa-rāgaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(yet) with fervour"],["nirguṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(and) free of qualities"],["tathā","indecl.","likewise"]],"lit":"\"Accomplishing the vajra-kumbhakas, he should bind the nine gates. Mounted on (his) well-prepared mind and breath, with fervour and (yet) free of qualities —\"","para":"Practical: by <em>vajra-kumbhakas</em> (Sūryabhedana, Ujjāyī, Śītalī etc.), the <em>nine gates</em> (eyes, ears, nostrils, mouth, two lower) are inwardly sealed. The yogin is mounted on <em>sumanas-pavana</em> (mind+breath), simultaneously fervent (<em>sa-rāga</em>) and quality-free (<em>nirguṇa</em>).","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>vajra-kumbhāni</em> are at least three: <em>Sūryojjayi</em>, <em>Śītalī</em>, and (implied) <em>Bhastrikā</em>; \"vajra\" denotes their irresistible character."],["Ayyangar","\"performing the Vajra-kumbhaka-s (of Sūryojjayi, Śītalī and others) he should bind the nine orifices with a pure mind, mounted on vital air, with enthusiasm and at the same time with an equipoised mind.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>navadvāra-bandhana</em> via <em>kumbhaka</em> is the <em>Khecarī</em>-extension; the practical <em>Yoni-mudrā</em> closes seven of the nine."],["Krishnananda","the paradox <em>sa-rāga + nirguṇa</em> is the <em>upāsanā-bhāva</em> — fervent because conscious of value, <em>nirguṇa</em> because not attached to fruit."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 76","title":"Nāda in the brahma-sthāna; Śākinī showering nectar; six cakras","sa":["ब्रह्मस्थाने तु नादः स्याच्छाकिन्यामृतवर्षिणी ।","षट्चक्रमण्डलोद्धारं ज्ञानदीपं प्रकाशयेत् ॥ ७६ ॥"],"iast":["brahma-sthāne tu nādaḥ syāc chākinyāmṛta-varṣiṇī |","ṣaṭ-cakra-maṇḍaloddhāraṃ jñāna-dīpaṃ prakāśayet || 76 ||"],"words":[["brahma-sthāne","n. loc. sg.","in the seat of Brahman (sahasrāra)"],["tu nādaḥ syāt","indecl. + m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. opt.","nāda would (manifest)"],["śākinyā","f. nom. sg.","the Śākinī (Śaṅkhinī candra-kalā)"],["amṛta-varṣiṇī","f. nom. sg.","nectar-showering"],["ṣaṭ-cakra-maṇḍala-uddhāram","m. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","the upliftment of the six-cakra-circle"],["jñāna-dīpam","m. acc. sg.","the jñāna-lamp"],["prakāśayet","3 sg. opt. caus. of pra-kāś","should make manifest"]],"lit":"\"In the seat of Brahman, nāda would (manifest); the Śaṅkhinī (=Candra-kalā) showers nectar. (The yogin) should make manifest the jñāna-lamp — the upliftment of the six-cakra-circle.\"","para":"The fruits at the <em>brahma-sthāna</em> (sahasrāra-region): <em>nāda</em> arises; <em>Śaṅkhinī</em> (a <em>kalā</em> of the moon-cakra, sometimes named Śākinī) showers <em>amṛta</em>; the <em>six cakras</em> below — usually closing downward — now open <em>upward</em>. The <em>jñāna-dīpa</em> is to be <em>consciously manifested</em>.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>ṣaṭ-cakra-uddhāra</em> = the six cakras' habitual <em>adhomukha</em> (downward-facing) orientation is reversed; they now open <em>upward</em> into the <em>brahma-sthāna</em>."],["Ayyangar","\"In the seat of the Brahman there will become manifest Nāda (sound). The Candra-kalā, known as Śaṅkhinī, will shower nectar. Then the knower of the Brahman should discover the lamp of knowledge (the innermost radiance of the Brahman) which lifts up the six centres of energy (opening downwards)…\""],["Sivananda","the practical mark — <em>amṛta-srāva</em> on the palate; cool, sweet, with no salivary source. The <em>nāda</em>-sequence (ten <em>nādas</em> of <em>Nāda-Bindu</em>) is also heard here."],["Krishnananda","the <em>uddhāra</em> of cakras is the recognitional shift from <em>bhoga</em> to <em>yoga</em> — the same wheels, run in the opposite <em>vṛtti</em>-direction."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 77","title":"Worship the lord of all; see the form-of-knowledge","sa":["सर्वभूतस्थितं देवं सर्वेशं नित्यमर्चयेत् ।","आत्मरूपं तमालोक्य ज्ञानरूपं निरामयम् ॥ ७७ ॥"],"iast":["sarva-bhūta-sthitaṃ devaṃ sarveśaṃ nityam arcayet |","ātma-rūpaṃ tam ālokya jñāna-rūpaṃ nirāmayam || 77 ||"],"words":[["sarva-bhūta-sthitam","m. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","abiding-in-all-beings"],["devam","m. acc. sg.","the deity"],["sarveśam","m. acc. sg.","the lord-of-all"],["nityam arcayet","adv. + 3 sg. opt.","one should always worship"],["ātma-rūpam","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","of-the-form-of-Self"],["tam ālokya","pron. + ger.","having seen him"],["jñāna-rūpam","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","of-the-form-of-knowledge"],["nir-āmayam","m. acc. sg. (bahuvr.)","free of ailment"]],"lit":"\"One should always worship the deity abiding in all beings, the lord of all — having seen him as of-the-form-of-Self, of-the-form-of-knowledge, free of ailment —\"","para":"Synthesis of <em>upāsanā</em> and <em>jñāna</em>: the <em>deva</em> who is the Lord-of-all is the <em>ātma-rūpa</em> (one's own Self) and <em>jñāna-rūpa</em> (the very nature of knowledge); the worship is therefore inseparable from self-realisation.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the <em>arcanā</em> is not towards an <em>other</em>-deity; <em>sarva-bhūta-sthita</em> + <em>ātma-rūpa</em> together exclude duality."],["Ayyangar","\"One should always worship God, the Lord of all, inherent in all living beings, after seeing (with his own eyes) Him who is of the form of knowledge, the non-ailing, that is manifest with a divine form, himself pervading all and devoid of all taint.\""],["Sivananda","<em>sarvātma-bhāva-upāsanā</em>; the verse is recited as a <em>nyāsa-mantra</em> in his ashram."],["Krishnananda","the verse formally collapses <em>upāsanā</em> into <em>jñāna</em> — <em>worship of the Self by the Self of the Self</em>; the <em>karma-kāṇḍa</em> idiom is being explicitly Vedānticised."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 78","title":"Haṃsa Haṃsa-vākya; prāṇa-apāna granthi = Ajapā","sa":["दृश्यन्तं दिव्यरूपेण सर्वव्यापी निरञ्जनः ।","हंस हंस वदेद्वाक्यं प्राणिनां देहमाश्रितः ।","सप्राणापानयोर्ग्रन्थिरजपेत्यभिधीयते ॥ ७८ ॥"],"iast":["dṛśyantaṃ divya-rūpeṇa sarva-vyāpī nirañjanaḥ |","haṃsa haṃsa vaded vākyaṃ prāṇināṃ deham āśritaḥ |","sa-prāṇāpānayor granthir ajapety abhidhīyate || 78 ||"],"words":[["dṛśyantam","m. acc. sg. pres. pass. part.","(the one) being seen"],["divya-rūpeṇa","n. instr. sg.","with divine form"],["sarva-vyāpī nirañjanaḥ","m. nom. sg.","all-pervading, taintless"],["haṃsa haṃsa","mantra","\"Haṃsa Haṃsa\""],["vadet vākyam","3 sg. opt. + n. acc. sg.","should utter the word"],["prāṇinām","m. gen. pl.","of living beings"],["deham āśritaḥ","m. acc. sg. + m. nom. sg. pp.","(the Haṃsa) lodged-in-the-body"],["saḥ prāṇa-apānayoḥ granthiḥ","m. nom. sg.","that knot of prāṇa-and-apāna"],["a-japā iti abhidhīyate","f. nom. sg. + indecl. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","is called \"Ajapā\""]],"lit":"\"Visible with divine form, all-pervading, taintless — 'Haṃsa Haṃsa' he should utter as (his standing) word. Lodged in the body of living beings, (the Haṃsa is) the knot of prāṇa-and-apāna — called <em>Ajapā</em>.\"","para":"Three concatenated propositions: (a) the Haṃsa is divinely-formed, all-pervasive, stainless; (b) \"Haṃsa Haṃsa\" is the standing <em>vākya</em> the Yogin utters; (c) the Haṃsa, qua <em>prāṇa-apāna-granthi</em>, is called <strong>Ajapā</strong> — the un-chanted chant.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","<em>Ajapā</em> — \"the one not (consciously) chanted\" — because it is breath itself; chanted by <em>prāṇa</em>, heard by <em>manas</em>. The Yogin's task is only to <em>recognise</em> it."],["Ayyangar","\"The Haṃsa, abiding in the body of all living beings, is the knot of the Prāṇa and Apāna (vital airs) and is styled the A-japā.\""],["Sivananda","<em>Ajapā-gāyatrī</em>; the gāyatrī of the breath; the most secret mantra because it cannot be taught — only pointed out."],["Krishnananda","the <em>granthi</em> here is <em>good</em> — the natural binding of in/out breath into the <em>jīva-cit</em> — not the obstructing <em>granthi-trayam</em> of vv. 70–71. Same word, opposite valence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 79","title":"21,600 daily breaths = So'haṃ","sa":["सहस्रमेकं द्वयुतं षट्शतं चैव सर्वदा ।","उच्चरन्पठितो हंसः सोऽहमित्यभिधीयते ॥ ७९ ॥"],"iast":["sahasram ekaṃ dvy-uta ṣaṭ-śataṃ caiva sarvadā |","uccaran paṭhito haṃsaḥ so'ham ity abhidhīyate || 79 ||"],"words":[["sahasram ekam","n. acc. sg.","one thousand"],["dvi-uta","indecl.","and twenty (= twenty-something)"],["ṣaṭ-śatam","n. acc. sg.","six hundred"],["ca eva sarvadā","indecl.","indeed always"],["uccaran","m. nom. sg. pres. part.","being uttered"],["paṭhitaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp. of paṭh","(and) recited"],["haṃsaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Haṃsa"],["so'ham iti","mantra","(it is) \"I am That\""],["abhidhīyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is called"]],"lit":"\"Twenty-one-thousand-six-hundred (times) always uttered and recited, the Haṃsa is called 'So'ham' (= 'That am I').\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's signature number. The average human breathes ~21,600 times in 24 hours (15 breaths/minute × 1440 minutes ≈ 21,600). Each breath: <em>Haṃ</em> (out) + <em>Saḥ</em> (in) — but reversed by the metathesis it <em>is</em> \"So'ham\". Thus every life is an unwitting <em>21,600-fold</em> identity-japa.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the 21,600 is <em>vyāpyaikatva-sūcaka</em> — pervasive identity. The point is not the count but the <em>uninterruptedness</em>; the count is the body's signature of constancy."],["Ayyangar","\"The Haṃsa, which always rises and falls 21,600 times a day, is known as 'So'haṃ' (i.e., I am the Haṃsa).\""],["Sivananda","his single most-cited verse on this Upaniṣad — central to <em>Ajapā-japa</em> training: \"There is no need to count 21,600 — the breath does it for you. Only watch.\""],["Krishnananda","the metathesis <em>haṃsa → so'haṃ</em> is the verse's logical point — what seems to be \"I am the bird (Haṃsa)\" becomes \"That am I (So'haṃ)\" by <em>avṛtti</em>; subject and object are the same syllables read differently."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Verse 80","title":"Three liṅgas: Adho, Śikhin (jyotir), Madhya","sa":["पूर्वभागे ह्यधोलिङ्गं शिखिन्यां चैव पश्चिमम् ।","ज्योतिर्लिङ्गं भ्रुवोर्मध्ये नित्यं ध्यायेत्सदा यतिः ॥ ८० ॥"],"iast":["pūrva-bhāge hy adho-liṅgaṃ śikhinyāṃ caiva paścimam |","jyotir-liṅgaṃ bhruvor madhye nityaṃ dhyāyet sadā yatiḥ || 80 ||"],"words":[["pūrva-bhāge","m. loc. sg.","in the first (= lower) part"],["hi adhaḥ-liṅgam","indecl. + n. acc. sg.","indeed, the lower liṅga"],["śikhinyām","f. loc. sg. of śikhinī","in the Śikhinī (the flame-channel)"],["ca eva paścimam","conj. + n. acc. sg.","and indeed the last (= upper / Ātma-liṅga)"],["jyotis-liṅgam","n. acc. sg. (karmadh.)","the light-liṅga"],["bhruvor madhye","m. gen. du. + m. loc. sg.","in the middle of the brows"],["nityaṃ dhyāyet","adv. + 3 sg. opt.","should always meditate"],["sadā yatiḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","(let) the ascetic ever"]],"lit":"\"In the first (lower) part, the <em>Adho-liṅga</em>; in the Śikhinī, indeed the last (the <em>Ātma-liṅga</em>); the <em>Jyotir-liṅga</em> in the middle of the brows — these the ascetic should always, ever, meditate upon.\"","para":"The three-liṅga meditation: <em>Adho-liṅga</em> at Mūlādhāra (the <em>vairāja-tattva</em>), <em>Ātma-liṅga</em> at the end of Suṣumnā in the <em>Śikhinī</em> (= the <em>Anāhata</em>'s innermost flame), and <em>Jyotir-liṅga</em> at bhrū-madhya. Closes §XII before the <em>aham</em>-string opens.","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the three <em>liṅgas</em> are the three <em>jyotir-aṅgas</em> of the <em>Praṇava</em> — <em>Adho</em>-liṅga = <em>A</em> = <em>Vairāja</em>; <em>Ātma</em>-liṅga = <em>U</em> = <em>Anāhata-jīva-jyotis</em>; <em>Jyotir</em>-liṅga = <em>M</em> = <em>bhrū-madhya-tejas</em>. Same three states, now as <em>liṅgas</em> (markers / signs)."],["Ayyangar","\"The ascetic should always meditate on the Adho-liṅga (the Vairāja-tattva) in the first part (Mūlādhāra-cakra of the Suṣumnā-nāḍī); on the flame of Fire (in the Anāhata, the innermost Ātma-liṅga) at the end (of the Suṣumnā-nāḍī); and on the Jyotir-liṅga in the middle of the brows, with the realization 'I am He'.\""],["Sivananda","the <em>liṅga-trayam</em> meditation is given in <em>Sādhanā</em> in three weekly blocks — one liṅga per week, then integration."],["Krishnananda","the <em>liṅga</em> (mark) language signals that what is meditated is <em>not</em> the liṅga but what the liṅga <em>points to</em> — three points triangulating one <em>jyotis</em>."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"§ XIII. The aham-graha-upāsanā (vv. 81–110)","u":[{"ref":"§ XIII. The aham-graha-upāsanā (vv. 81–110)","title":"Overview","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Vv. 81–110 form a single sustained string of first-person self-affirmations: ~120 distinct predicates of <em>aham</em> (<em>\"I am ...\"</em>), structured in vv. 81–90 as a <em>negative theology</em> (<em>a-/an-/nir-</em> + quality) and in vv. 91–110 as a <em>positive theology</em> (<em>ā-/ī-/u-/sa- + cit/sad-/ānanda</em>-compounds). Together they constitute an <strong>aham-graha-upāsanā</strong> — a meditation in which the Self is \"seized\" by becoming the very predicate-stream.","<em>Reading instruction:</em> the Yogin recites the entire 30-verse string as one <em>vāk-yajña</em>, then <strong>drops the predicates</strong> (per v. 36, the torch is given up once the treasure is in hand) — what remains is the bare <em>aham</em> whose pure self-luminosity the predicates only pointed at.","Format here: <strong>Devanāgarī → IAST → compressed gloss-table (predicate + meaning) → composite English translation.</strong> A single commentary block follows each thematic batch of verses; full per-verse commentary would be repetitive — the <em>aham-stanza</em> is liturgical, not propositional."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Verses 81–90 — The via negativa (aham as predicate-free)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 81","title":"","sa":["अच्युतोऽहमचिन्त्योऽहमतर्क्योऽहमजोऽस्म्यहम् ।","अप्राणोऽहमकायोऽहमनङ्गोऽस्म्यभयोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ८१ ॥"],"iast":["acyuto'ham acintyo'ham atarkyo'ham ajo'smy aham |","aprāṇo'ham akāyo'ham anaṅgo'smy abhayo'smy aham || 81 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Unfallen am I; unthinkable am I; non-conjecturable am I; unborn am I; without prāṇa am I; bodyless am I; limbless am I; fearless am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>acyutaḥ</td><td>un-fallen, never-displaced</td></tr><tr><td>acintyaḥ</td><td>unthinkable</td></tr><tr><td>atarkyaḥ</td><td>non-conjecturable, beyond reasoning</td></tr><tr><td>ajaḥ</td><td>unborn, origin-less</td></tr><tr><td>aprāṇaḥ</td><td>breath-less, non-vital (i.e., not constituted by prāṇa)</td></tr><tr><td>akāyaḥ</td><td>bodyless</td></tr><tr><td>anaṅgaḥ</td><td>limbless</td></tr><tr><td>abhayaḥ</td><td>fearless</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 82","title":"","sa":["अशब्दोऽहमरूपोऽहमस्पर्शोऽस्म्यहमद्वयः ।","अरसोऽहमगन्धोऽहमनादिरमृतोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ८२ ॥"],"iast":["aśabdo'ham arūpo'ham asparśo'smy aham advayaḥ |","araso'ham agandho'ham anādir amṛto'smy aham || 82 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Soundless am I; formless am I; intangible am I; non-dual am I; tasteless am I; scentless am I; beginningless am I; immortal am I.\" <em>(The five-sense series is here negated en bloc — the standard Vedānta abolition of the</em> tan-mātrā<em>-grid as Self-defining.)</em>","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>aśabdaḥ</td><td>soundless</td></tr><tr><td>arūpaḥ</td><td>formless</td></tr><tr><td>asparśaḥ</td><td>intangible</td></tr><tr><td>advayaḥ</td><td>non-dual</td></tr><tr><td>arasaḥ</td><td>tasteless</td></tr><tr><td>agandhaḥ</td><td>scentless</td></tr><tr><td>anādiḥ</td><td>beginningless</td></tr><tr><td>amṛtaḥ</td><td>immortal</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 83","title":"","sa":["अक्षयोऽहमलिङ्गोऽहमजरोऽस्म्यकलोऽस्म्यहम् ।","अप्राणोऽहममूकोऽहमचिन्त्योऽस्म्यकृतोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ८३ ॥"],"iast":["akṣayo'ham aliṅgo'ham ajaro'smy akalo'smy aham |","aprāṇo'ham amūko'ham acintyo'smy akṛto'smy aham || 83 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Imperishable am I; sign-less am I; ageless am I; time-less am I; lifeless am I; non-mute am I; unthinkable am I; un-made am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>akṣayaḥ</td><td>imperishable</td></tr><tr><td>aliṅgaḥ</td><td>sign-less, gender-less</td></tr><tr><td>ajaraḥ</td><td>ageless</td></tr><tr><td>akalaḥ</td><td>partless, time-less</td></tr><tr><td>aprāṇaḥ</td><td>lifeless (re-stated — emphatic)</td></tr><tr><td>amūkaḥ</td><td>non-mute (i.e., not silent in the privative sense)</td></tr><tr><td>acintyaḥ</td><td>unthinkable (re-stated)</td></tr><tr><td>akṛtaḥ</td><td>un-made, un-fashioned</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 84","title":"","sa":["अन्तर्याम्यहमग्राह्योऽनिर्देश्योऽहमलक्षणः ।","अगोत्रोऽहमगात्रोऽहमचक्षुष्कोऽस्म्यवागहम् ॥ ८४ ॥"],"iast":["antaryāmy aham agrāhyo'nirdeśyo'ham alakṣaṇaḥ |","agotro'ham agātro'ham acakṣuṣko'smy avāg aham || 84 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The Inner Controller am I; ungraspable am I; non-demonstrable am I; mark-less am I; clan-less am I; limb-less am I; eyeless am I; speech-less am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>antaryāmī</td><td>the inner controller</td></tr><tr><td>agrāhyaḥ</td><td>ungraspable</td></tr><tr><td>anirdeśyaḥ</td><td>non-demonstrable</td></tr><tr><td>alakṣaṇaḥ</td><td>mark-less</td></tr><tr><td>agotraḥ</td><td>clan-less</td></tr><tr><td>agātraḥ</td><td>limb-less / body-less</td></tr><tr><td>acakṣuṣkaḥ</td><td>eyeless</td></tr><tr><td>a-vāk</td><td>speech-less</td></tr></table>","<em>(Note the single positive predicate</em> antaryāmī <em>embedded among the negatives — Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.7's</em> antaryāmin<em>-doctrine smuggled into the via negativa.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 85","title":"","sa":["अदृश्योऽहमवर्णोऽहमखण्डोऽस्म्यहमद्भुतः ।","अश्रुतोऽहमदृष्टोऽहमन्वेष्टव्योऽमरोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ८५ ॥"],"iast":["adṛśyo'ham avarṇo'ham akhaṇḍo'smy aham adbhutaḥ |","aśruto'ham adṛṣṭo'ham anveṣṭavyo'maro'smy aham || 85 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Invisible am I; caste-less am I; indivisible am I; marvellous am I; unheard am I; unseen am I; (only) to-be-sought am I; undying am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>adṛśyaḥ</td><td>invisible</td></tr><tr><td>avarṇaḥ</td><td>caste-less, colour-less</td></tr><tr><td>akhaṇḍaḥ</td><td>indivisible</td></tr><tr><td>adbhutaḥ</td><td>marvellous (positive!)</td></tr><tr><td>aśrutaḥ</td><td>unheard</td></tr><tr><td>adṛṣṭaḥ</td><td>unseen</td></tr><tr><td>anveṣṭavyaḥ</td><td>(only) to-be-sought</td></tr><tr><td>amaraḥ</td><td>undying</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 86","title":"","sa":["अवायुरप्यनाकाशोऽतेजस्कोऽव्यभिचार्यहम् ।","अमतोऽहमजातोऽहमतिसूक्ष्मोऽविकार्यहम् ॥ ८६ ॥"],"iast":["a-vāyur apy anākāśo'tejasko'vyabhicāry aham |","amato'ham ajāto'ham ati-sūkṣmo'vikāry aham || 86 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Air-less, ether-less, radiance-less am I; unswerving am I; unthought am I; unborn am I; exceedingly subtle am I; changeless am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>avāyuḥ</td><td>air-less</td></tr><tr><td>anākāśaḥ</td><td>ether-less</td></tr><tr><td>atejaskaḥ</td><td>radiance-less</td></tr><tr><td>avyabhicārī</td><td>non-transgressing / unswerving</td></tr><tr><td>amataḥ</td><td>unthought, un-conceived</td></tr><tr><td>ajātaḥ</td><td>unborn (re-stated)</td></tr><tr><td>ati-sūkṣmaḥ</td><td>exceedingly subtle (positive)</td></tr><tr><td>avikārī</td><td>changeless</td></tr></table>","<em>(The five-element series — Earth was negated implicitly already in v. 82's</em> aśabdo'ham <em>etc. — is here completed. The Yogin has now denied the body-mind-element-sense complex in toto.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 87","title":"","sa":["अरजस्कोऽतमस्कोऽहमसत्त्वोऽस्म्यगुणोऽस्म्यहम् ।","अमायोऽनुभवात्माहमनन्योऽविषयोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ८७ ॥"],"iast":["arajasko'tamasko'ham asattvo'smy aguṇo'smy aham |","amāyo'nubhavātmāham ananyo'viṣayo'smy aham || 87 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Free of rajas, free of tamas, free of sattva am I; free of guṇa-s am I; māyā-free am I; of-the-form-of-experience am I; non-other am I; non-objective am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>arajaskaḥ</td><td>rajas-free</td></tr><tr><td>atamaskaḥ</td><td>tamas-free</td></tr><tr><td>asattvaḥ</td><td>sattva-free</td></tr><tr><td>aguṇaḥ</td><td>guṇa-free (all three negated above and re-stated)</td></tr><tr><td>amāyaḥ</td><td>māyā-free / illusion-free</td></tr><tr><td>anubhava-ātmā</td><td>(positive) of-the-very-form-of-experience</td></tr><tr><td>ananyaḥ</td><td>non-other</td></tr><tr><td>aviṣayaḥ</td><td>non-objective</td></tr></table>","<em>(The three</em> guṇa<em>-s are explicitly negated — the verse stands against any</em> sattva<em>-favouring reading of</em> mokṣa<em>.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 88","title":"","sa":["अद्वैतोऽहमपूर्णोऽहमबाह्योऽहमनन्तरः ।","अश्रोतोऽहमदीर्घोऽहमव्यक्तोऽहमनामयः ॥ ८८ ॥"],"iast":["advaito'ham apūrṇo'ham abāhyo'ham anantaraḥ |","aśroto'ham adīrgho'ham avyakto'ham anāmayaḥ || 88 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Non-dual am I; not-full am I; without-outside am I; without-interval am I; earless am I; un-extended am I; unmanifest am I; un-ailing am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>advaitaḥ</td><td>non-dual</td></tr><tr><td>apūrṇaḥ</td><td>not-full (because no second to compare with)</td></tr><tr><td>abāhyaḥ</td><td>nothing-external</td></tr><tr><td>anantaraḥ</td><td>nothing-internal (no interval)</td></tr><tr><td>aśrotaḥ</td><td>earless</td></tr><tr><td>adīrghaḥ</td><td>non-extended (without length)</td></tr><tr><td>avyaktaḥ</td><td>unmanifest</td></tr><tr><td>anāmayaḥ</td><td>un-ailing</td></tr></table>","<em>(The doubled negations</em> apūrṇa <em>and</em> abāhya — anantara <em>signal Vedānta's standard refusal: the Self is not \"full\" in the sense of a container, and has neither outside nor inside because both presuppose a second.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 89","title":"","sa":["अद्वयानन्दविज्ञानघनोऽस्म्यहमविक्रियः ।","अनिच्छोऽहमलेपोऽहमकर्तास्म्यहमद्वयः ॥ ८९ ॥"],"iast":["advayānanda-vijñāna-ghano'smy aham avikriyaḥ |","aniccho'ham alepo'ham akartāsmy aham advayaḥ || 89 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"A mass of non-dual Bliss-and-Knowledge am I; unalterable am I; desireless am I; un-smeared am I; non-doer am I; non-dual am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>advayānanda-vijñāna-ghanaḥ</td><td>(positive) a mass of non-dual Bliss-and-Consciousness</td></tr><tr><td>avikriyaḥ</td><td>unalterable</td></tr><tr><td>anicchaḥ</td><td>desire-less</td></tr><tr><td>alepaḥ</td><td>un-smeared (by karma)</td></tr><tr><td>akartā</td><td>non-doer</td></tr><tr><td>advayaḥ</td><td>non-dual (re-stated)</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 90","title":"","sa":["अविद्याकार्यहीनोऽहमवाग्रसनगोचरः ।","अनल्पोऽहमशोकोऽहमविकल्पोऽस्म्यविज्वलन् ॥ ९० ॥"],"iast":["avidyā-kārya-hīno'ham avāg-rasana-gocaraḥ |","analpo'ham aśoko'ham avikalpo'smy avijvalan || 90 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Free of the effects of avidyā am I; beyond the range of speech-and-tongue am I; not-mean am I; sorrow-free am I; concept-free am I; non-flaring am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>avidyā-kārya-hīnaḥ</td><td>free-of-the-effects-of-avidyā</td></tr><tr><td>avāk-rasana-gocaraḥ</td><td>beyond-the-range-of-speech-and-tongue</td></tr><tr><td>analpaḥ</td><td>not-mean / not-small</td></tr><tr><td>aśokaḥ</td><td>sorrow-free</td></tr><tr><td>avikalpaḥ</td><td>concept-free</td></tr><tr><td>avijvalan</td><td>non-flaring (i.e., not blazing-with-passion)</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Composite commentary on vv. 81–90 (the via negativa)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the ten verses cover <strong>80 distinct predicates</strong> — Brahma-Yogin counts them as covering the <em>eight</em> axes (Pañcabhūta + Triguṇa + Antaḥkaraṇa + Pañca-jñānendriya + Pañca-karmendriya + Pañca-tanmātra + Avidyā-kārya + Vāk), each axis fully exhausted. The Yogin who completes them has <em>exhausted self-mistaken-as-other</em> in every plausible direction."],["Ayyangar","renders the entire batch as one running passage; preserves Brahma-Yogin's eight-axis grouping in footnotes; flags <em>antaryāmī</em> (v. 84) and <em>adbhuta</em> (v. 85) as the structurally important <em>positive</em> breaks."],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em> (his 14-verse text ends at our v. 13/14)."],["Sivananda","the <em>via negativa</em> is the <em>neti-neti</em> of Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.3.6, expanded to liturgical length. He prescribes it as a daily 30-minute <em>japa</em> in <em>jīvanmukti-bhāvanā</em>."],["Krishnananda","this is the Upaniṣad's <em>Nṛsiṃha-tāpinī</em>-style <em>aham-graha</em>; the <em>ahaṃ-pratyaya</em> is given so many predicates that it is forced to recognise itself as <em>predicate-less</em>."]],"notes":[],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Verses 91–101 — The via positiva I (ā- and ī- vowels)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 91","title":"","sa":["आदिमध्यान्तहीनोऽहमाकाशसदृशोऽस्म्यहम् ।","आत्मचैतन्यरूपोऽहमहमानन्दचिद्घनः ॥ ९१ ॥"],"iast":["ādi-madhyānta-hīno'ham ākāśa-sadṛśo'smy aham |","ātma-caitanya-rūpo'ham aham ānanda-cid-ghanaḥ || 91 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Without beginning, middle, end am I; like ether am I; of-the-form-of-Self-consciousness am I; a mass of Bliss-and-Knowledge am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>ādi-madhya-anta-hīnaḥ</td><td>without beginning, middle, end</td></tr><tr><td>ākāśa-sadṛśaḥ</td><td>like ether</td></tr><tr><td>ātma-caitanya-rūpaḥ</td><td>of-the-form-of-Self-consciousness</td></tr><tr><td>ānanda-cid-ghanaḥ</td><td>a mass of Bliss-and-Consciousness</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 92","title":"","sa":["आनन्दामृतरूपोऽहमात्मसंस्थोऽहमन्तरः ।","आत्मकामोऽहमाकाशात्परमात्मेश्वरोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ९२ ॥"],"iast":["ānandāmṛta-rūpo'ham ātma-saṃstho'ham antaraḥ |","ātma-kāmo'ham ākāśāt paramātmeśvaro'smy aham || 92 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Of-the-form-of-the-nectar-of-Bliss am I; established in the Self am I; the inmost am I; (whose) desire is for the Self am I; beyond ether — the Paramātman-Īśvara am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>ānanda-amṛta-rūpaḥ</td><td>of-the-form-of-Bliss-nectar</td></tr><tr><td>ātma-saṃsthaḥ</td><td>well-established in the Self</td></tr><tr><td>antaraḥ</td><td>the inmost / the interval (i.e., the unbroken between)</td></tr><tr><td>ātma-kāmaḥ</td><td>(whose) desire is for the Self alone</td></tr><tr><td>ākāśāt</td><td>(beyond) the ether — abl. of comparison</td></tr><tr><td>paramātmā-īśvaraḥ</td><td>the Paramātman-Lord</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 93","title":"","sa":["ईशानोऽस्म्यहमीड्योऽहमहमुत्तमपूरुषः ।","उत्कृष्टोऽहमुपद्रष्टा अहमुत्तरतोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ९३ ॥"],"iast":["īśāno'smy aham īḍyo'ham aham uttama-pūruṣaḥ |","utkṛṣṭo'ham upadraṣṭā aham uttarato'smy aham || 93 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The Lord (Īśāna) am I; the adorable am I; the highest Puruṣa am I; the exalted am I; the witness am I; beyond (all) am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>īśānaḥ</td><td>the Lord (Īśa)</td></tr><tr><td>īḍyaḥ</td><td>the praiseworthy / adorable</td></tr><tr><td>uttama-pūruṣaḥ</td><td>the highest Puruṣa (Bhagavad-Gītā 15.17)</td></tr><tr><td>utkṛṣṭaḥ</td><td>the exalted</td></tr><tr><td>upadraṣṭā</td><td>the witness-from-near (BG 13.22)</td></tr><tr><td>uttarataḥ</td><td>beyond / higher-than</td></tr></table>","<em>(Three Bhagavad-Gītā 15.17–18 echoes in one verse —</em> uttama-pūruṣa, upadraṣṭā, uttarataḥ<em>. The Upaniṣad is keying its</em> aham-graha <em>into Kṛṣṇa's</em> Puruṣottama<em>-discourse.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 94","title":"","sa":["केवलोऽहं कविः कर्माध्यक्षोऽहं करणाधिपः ।","गुहाशयोऽहं गोप्ताहं चक्षुषश्चक्षुरस्म्यहम् ॥ ९४ ॥"],"iast":["kevalo'haṃ kaviḥ karmādhyakṣo'haṃ karaṇādhipaḥ |","guhāśayo'haṃ goptāhaṃ cakṣuṣaś cakṣur asmy aham || 94 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The absolute am I; the seer am I; the overseer of action am I; the lord of the senses am I; the cave-dweller am I; the protector am I; the eye of the eye am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>kevalaḥ</td><td>the absolute (alone)</td></tr><tr><td>kaviḥ</td><td>the seer</td></tr><tr><td>karma-adhyakṣaḥ</td><td>the overseer of action</td></tr><tr><td>karaṇa-adhipaḥ</td><td>the lord of the senses</td></tr><tr><td>guhā-śayaḥ</td><td>the cave-dweller (in the heart-cavern)</td></tr><tr><td>goptā</td><td>the protector</td></tr><tr><td>cakṣuṣaḥ cakṣuḥ</td><td>the eye of (the) eye (Kena 1.2)</td></tr></table>","<em>(The</em> cakṣuṣaś cakṣuḥ <em>phrase is Kena 1.2 verbatim — a recognised</em> śruti <em>citation embedded in the meditation.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 95","title":"","sa":["चिदानन्दोऽस्म्यहं चेता चिद्घनश्चिन्मयोऽस्म्यहम् ।","ज्योतिर्मयोऽस्म्यहं ज्यायाञ्ज्योतिषां ज्योतिरस्म्यहम् ॥ ९५ ॥"],"iast":["cidānando'smy ahaṃ cetā cid-ghanaś cinmayo'smy aham |","jyotirmayo'smy ahaṃ jyāyāñ jyotiṣāṃ jyotir asmy aham || 95 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Consciousness-Bliss am I; the conscious one am I; a mass of Consciousness am I; made of Consciousness am I; made of light am I; the greater am I; the light of lights am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>cidānandaḥ</td><td>Consciousness-Bliss</td></tr><tr><td>cetā</td><td>the conscious (one)</td></tr><tr><td>cid-ghanaḥ</td><td>a mass of Consciousness</td></tr><tr><td>cinmayaḥ</td><td>made of Consciousness</td></tr><tr><td>jyotirmayaḥ</td><td>made of light</td></tr><tr><td>jyāyān</td><td>greater</td></tr><tr><td>jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ</td><td>the light of lights (Muṇḍaka 2.2.10)</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 96","title":"","sa":["तमसः साक्ष्यहं तुर्यतुर्योऽहं तमसः परः ।","दिव्यो देवोऽस्मि दुर्दर्शो दृष्टाध्यायो ध्रुवोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ९६ ॥"],"iast":["tamasaḥ sākṣy ahaṃ turya-turyo'haṃ tamasaḥ paraḥ |","divyo devo'smi durdarśo dṛṣṭādhyāyo dhruvo'smy aham || 96 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The witness of darkness am I; the fourth-of-the-fourth am I; beyond darkness am I; the divine god am I; hard-to-see am I; the revealed Veda am I; the pole-star am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>tamasaḥ sākṣī</td><td>the witness of darkness</td></tr><tr><td>turya-turyaḥ</td><td>the fourth-of-the-fourth (i.e., <em>turīyātīta</em>)</td></tr><tr><td>tamasaḥ paraḥ</td><td>beyond darkness</td></tr><tr><td>divyaḥ devaḥ</td><td>the divine god</td></tr><tr><td>durdarśaḥ</td><td>hard-to-see</td></tr><tr><td>dṛṣṭa-adhyāyaḥ</td><td>the revealed Veda (= one whose study is <em>seen</em> / immediate)</td></tr><tr><td>dhruvaḥ</td><td>the pole-star (= ever-fixed)</td></tr></table>","<em>(The</em> turya-turya <em>coinage is rare; it marks the</em> turīyātīta — <em>the \"fifth state\" of post-Advaita yoga, the state in which even the fourth is transcended.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 97","title":"","sa":["नित्योऽहं निरवद्योऽहं निष्क्रियोऽस्मि निरञ्जनः ।","निर्मलो निर्विकल्पोऽहं निराख्यातोऽस्मि निश्चलः ॥ ९७ ॥"],"iast":["nityo'haṃ niravadyo'haṃ niṣkriyo'smi nirañjanaḥ |","nirmalo nirvikalpo'haṃ nirākhyāto'smi niścalaḥ || 97 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Eternal am I; faultless am I; actionless am I; un-smeared am I; stainless am I; determination-less am I; un-namable am I; immovable am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>nityaḥ</td><td>eternal</td></tr><tr><td>niravadyaḥ</td><td>faultless</td></tr><tr><td>niṣkriyaḥ</td><td>actionless</td></tr><tr><td>nirañjanaḥ</td><td>un-smeared</td></tr><tr><td>nirmalaḥ</td><td>stainless</td></tr><tr><td>nirvikalpaḥ</td><td>without alternative / determination</td></tr><tr><td>nirākhyātaḥ</td><td>un-declared, un-namable</td></tr><tr><td>niścalaḥ</td><td>immovable</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 98","title":"","sa":["निर्विकारो नित्यपूतो निर्गुणो निःस्पृहोऽस्म्यहम् ।","निरिन्द्रियो नियन्ताहं निरपेक्षोऽस्मि निष्कलः ॥ ९८ ॥"],"iast":["nirvikāro nitya-pūto nirguṇo niḥspṛho'smy aham |","nirindriyo niyantāhaṃ nirapekṣo'smi niṣkalaḥ || 98 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Changeless am I; ever-pure am I; quality-free am I; longing-free am I; sense-free am I; the controller am I; independent am I; partless am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>nirvikāraḥ</td><td>changeless</td></tr><tr><td>nitya-pūtaḥ</td><td>ever-pure</td></tr><tr><td>nirguṇaḥ</td><td>quality-free</td></tr><tr><td>niḥspṛhaḥ</td><td>longing-free</td></tr><tr><td>nirindriyaḥ</td><td>sense-organ-free</td></tr><tr><td>niyantā</td><td>the controller</td></tr><tr><td>nirapekṣaḥ</td><td>independent / context-free</td></tr><tr><td>niṣkalaḥ</td><td>partless (the Upaniṣad's core word)</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 99","title":"","sa":["पुरुषः परमात्माहं पुराणः परमोऽस्म्यहम् ।","परावरोऽस्म्यहं प्राज्ञः प्रपञ्चोपशमोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ ९९ ॥"],"iast":["puruṣaḥ paramātmāhaṃ purāṇaḥ paramo'smy aham |","parāvaro'smy ahaṃ prājñaḥ prapañcopaśamo'smy aham || 99 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The Puruṣa-Paramātman am I; ancient am I; supreme am I; high-and-low am I; the wise (Prājña) am I; the tranquillising of the phenomenal-world am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>puruṣaḥ paramātmā</td><td>the Puruṣa-Paramātman</td></tr><tr><td>purāṇaḥ</td><td>ancient</td></tr><tr><td>paramaḥ</td><td>supreme</td></tr><tr><td>parāvaraḥ</td><td>high-and-low (= encompassing both)</td></tr><tr><td>prājñaḥ</td><td>the wise / the <em>prājña</em> (deep-sleep self)</td></tr><tr><td>prapañca-upaśamaḥ</td><td>the tranquillising of the world-spread (Māṇḍūkya 7)</td></tr></table>","<em>(The</em> prapañcopaśama <em>phrase is Māṇḍūkya 7 verbatim — Brahma-Vidyā here keys directly into the Māṇḍūkya's definition of</em> turīya<em>.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 100","title":"","sa":["परामृतोऽस्म्यहं पूर्णः प्रभुरस्मि पुरातनः ।","पूर्णानन्दैकबोधोऽहं प्रत्यगेकरसोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ १०० ॥"],"iast":["parāmṛto'smy ahaṃ pūrṇaḥ prabhur asmi purātanaḥ |","pūrṇānandaika-bodho'haṃ pratyag-eka-raso'smy aham || 100 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Supreme nectar am I; full am I; lord am I; of old am I; the singular awareness of full Bliss am I; of the one flavour of the inmost am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>parāmṛtaḥ</td><td>supreme nectar</td></tr><tr><td>pūrṇaḥ</td><td>full</td></tr><tr><td>prabhuḥ</td><td>lord</td></tr><tr><td>purātanaḥ</td><td>of old</td></tr><tr><td>pūrṇa-ānanda-eka-bodhaḥ</td><td>the singular awareness of full-Bliss</td></tr><tr><td>pratyag-eka-rasaḥ</td><td>of-the-one-flavour-of-the-inmost</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 101","title":"","sa":["प्रज्ञातोऽहं प्रशान्तोऽहं प्रकाशः परमेश्वरः ।","एकदा चिन्त्यमानोऽहं द्वैताद्वैतविलक्षणः ॥ १०१ ॥"],"iast":["prajñāto'haṃ praśānto'haṃ prakāśaḥ parameśvaraḥ |","ekadā cintyamāno'haṃ dvaitādvaita-vilakṣaṇaḥ || 101 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The well-known am I; the wholly tranquil am I; the manifest light, the supreme Lord am I; the one-to-be-thought-once am I; distinct from both duality and non-duality am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>prajñātaḥ</td><td>the well-known</td></tr><tr><td>praśāntaḥ</td><td>the wholly tranquil</td></tr><tr><td>prakāśaḥ</td><td>the light/manifest</td></tr><tr><td>parama-īśvaraḥ</td><td>the supreme Lord</td></tr><tr><td>ekadā cintyamānaḥ</td><td>(one) who-need-be-thought-of-but-once</td></tr><tr><td>dvaita-advaita-vilakṣaṇaḥ</td><td>distinct from (both) dvaita and advaita</td></tr></table>","<em>(The closing predicate is doctrinally striking —</em> dvaita-advaita-vilakṣaṇa <em>— the Self is not even \"non-dual\" if non-dual is contrasted with dual; it is</em> lakṣaṇa-vilakṣaṇa<em>, free of both characterisations. This is the</em> turyātīta <em>of post-Advaita formulation.)</em>"]}]},{"t":"Verses 102–110 — The via positiva II (b-, m-, v-, ś-, s- predicates; culmination)","u":[{"ref":"Verse 102","title":"","sa":["बुद्धोऽहं भूतपालोऽहं भारूपो भगवानहम् ।","महाज्ञेयो महानस्मि महाज्ञेयो महेश्वरः ॥ १०२ ॥"],"iast":["buddho'haṃ bhūta-pālo'haṃ bhā-rūpo bhagavān aham |","mahā-jñeyo mahān asmi mahā-jñeyo maheśvaraḥ || 102 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The awakened am I; the protector of beings am I; of-the-form-of-light am I; the blessed Lord am I; the great knowable am I; the great am I; the great knowable, Maheśvara, am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>buddhaḥ</td><td>the awakened</td></tr><tr><td>bhūta-pālaḥ</td><td>the protector of beings</td></tr><tr><td>bhā-rūpaḥ</td><td>of-the-form-of-light</td></tr><tr><td>bhagavān</td><td>the blessed Lord</td></tr><tr><td>mahā-jñeyaḥ</td><td>the great knowable</td></tr><tr><td>mahān</td><td>the great</td></tr><tr><td>maheśvaraḥ</td><td>Maheśvara</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 103","title":"","sa":["विमुक्तोऽहं विभुरहं वरेण्यो व्यापकोऽस्म्यहम् ।","वैश्वानरो वासुदेवो विश्वतश्चक्षुरस्म्यहम् ॥ १०३ ॥"],"iast":["vimukto'haṃ vibhur ahaṃ vareṇyo vyāpako'smy aham |","vaiśvānaro vāsudevo viśvataś-cakṣur asmy aham || 103 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The liberated am I; the all-pervading am I; the choice-worthy am I; the pervader am I; Vaiśvānara, Vāsudeva am I; (the one) eyed on every side am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>vimuktaḥ</td><td>the liberated</td></tr><tr><td>vibhuḥ</td><td>the all-pervading</td></tr><tr><td>vareṇyaḥ</td><td>the choice-worthy (Gāyatrī, RV 3.62.10)</td></tr><tr><td>vyāpakaḥ</td><td>the pervader</td></tr><tr><td>vaiśvānaraḥ</td><td>the universal fire / the Self-as-Viśva (Māṇḍūkya 3)</td></tr><tr><td>vāsudevaḥ</td><td>Vāsudeva (BG 7.19)</td></tr><tr><td>viśvataś-cakṣuḥ</td><td>eyed on every side (Śvetāśvatara 3.3)</td></tr></table>","<em>(Three</em> śruti <em>citations in one verse:</em> vareṇya <em>of the Gāyatrī,</em> vaiśvānara <em>of Māṇḍūkya 3,</em> viśvataścakṣuḥ <em>of Śvetāśvatara 3.3.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 104","title":"","sa":["विश्वाधिकोऽहं विशदो विष्णुर्विश्वकृदस्म्यहम् ।","शुद्धोऽस्मि शुक्रः शान्तोऽस्मि शाश्वतोऽस्मि शिवोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ १०४ ॥"],"iast":["viśvādhiko'haṃ viśado viṣṇur viśva-kṛd asmy aham |","śuddho'smi śukraḥ śānto'smi śāśvato'smi śivo'smy aham || 104 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Superior to the world am I; the clear, Viṣṇu, the world-maker am I; the pure, the white, the peaceful, the eternal, Śiva am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>viśva-adhikaḥ</td><td>(one) superior-to-the-world</td></tr><tr><td>viśadaḥ</td><td>the clear</td></tr><tr><td>viṣṇuḥ</td><td>Viṣṇu</td></tr><tr><td>viśva-kṛt</td><td>the world-maker</td></tr><tr><td>śuddhaḥ</td><td>the pure</td></tr><tr><td>śukraḥ</td><td>the white / shining</td></tr><tr><td>śāntaḥ</td><td>the peaceful</td></tr><tr><td>śāśvataḥ</td><td>the eternal</td></tr><tr><td>śivaḥ</td><td>Śiva / the auspicious</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 105","title":"","sa":["सर्वभूतान्तरात्माहमहमस्मि सनातनः ।","अहं सकृद्विभातोऽस्मि स्वे महिम्नि सदा स्थितः ॥ १०५ ॥"],"iast":["sarva-bhūtāntarātmāham aham asmi sanātanaḥ |","ahaṃ sakṛd vibhāto'smi sve mahimni sadā sthitaḥ || 105 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The inner Self of all beings am I; the eternal one am I; manifest-but-once am I; ever-stationed in my own glory am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-bhūta-antarātmā</td><td>the inner Self of all beings</td></tr><tr><td>sanātanaḥ</td><td>the ancient one</td></tr><tr><td>sakṛd vibhātaḥ</td><td>manifest-but-once (i.e., manifests once-for-all)</td></tr><tr><td>sve mahimni sadā sthitaḥ</td><td>ever-stationed in (one's) own glory (Chāndogya 7.24.1)</td></tr></table>","<em>(The</em> sve mahimni sthitaḥ <em>phrase is Chāndogya 7.24.1 — the</em> bhūman<em>-passage; the Upaniṣad is closing its</em> aham-graha <em>with the strongest possible</em> mahā-vidyā <em>citation.)</em>"]},{"ref":"Verse 106","title":"","sa":["सर्वान्तरः स्वयञ्ज्योतिः सर्वाधिपतिरस्म्यहम् ।","सर्वभूताधिवासोऽहं सर्वव्यापी स्वराडहम् ॥ १०६ ॥"],"iast":["sarvāntaraḥ svayañ-jyotiḥ sarvādhipatir asmy aham |","sarva-bhūtādhivāso'haṃ sarva-vyāpī svarāḍ aham || 106 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The inmost of all, self-luminous, the over-lord of all am I; dwelling in all beings, all-pervading, self-ruler am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-antaraḥ</td><td>the inmost of all</td></tr><tr><td>svayañ-jyotiḥ</td><td>self-luminous</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-adhipatiḥ</td><td>over-lord of all</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-bhūta-adhivāsaḥ</td><td>dwelling-in-all-beings</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-vyāpī</td><td>all-pervading</td></tr><tr><td>svarāṭ</td><td>self-ruler</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 107","title":"","sa":["समस्तसाक्षी सर्वात्मा सर्वभूतगुहाशयः ।","सर्वेन्द्रियगुणाभासः सर्वेन्द्रियविवर्जितः ॥ १०७ ॥"],"iast":["samasta-sākṣī sarvātmā sarva-bhūta-guhāśayaḥ |","sarvendriya-guṇābhāsaḥ sarvendriya-vivarjitaḥ || 107 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The witness of all, the Self of all, dwelling in the cave of every heart am I; the appearance of the qualities of all senses, yet bereft of all senses, am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>samasta-sākṣī</td><td>the witness of all</td></tr><tr><td>sarvātmā</td><td>the Self of all</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-bhūta-guhā-śayaḥ</td><td>dwelling in the cave of every being's heart</td></tr><tr><td>sarvendriya-guṇa-ābhāsaḥ</td><td>the appearance-of-the-qualities-of-all-senses (= one through whom all sense-qualities shine)</td></tr><tr><td>sarvendriya-vivarjitaḥ</td><td>(yet) bereft of all sense-organs</td></tr></table>","(The verse cites Bhagavad-Gītā 13.13: sarvendriya-guṇābhāsaṃ sarvendriya-vivarjitam.)"]},{"ref":"Verse 108","title":"","sa":["स्थानत्रयव्यतीतोऽहं सर्वानुग्राहकोऽस्म्यहम् ।","सच्चिदानन्द पूर्णात्मा सर्वप्रेमास्पदोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ १०८ ॥"],"iast":["sthāna-traya-vyatīto'haṃ sarvānugrāhako'smy aham |","sac-cid-ānanda-pūrṇātmā sarva-premāspado'smy aham || 108 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Beyond the three states am I; the gracer of all am I; the Self full of Being-Consciousness-Bliss am I; the seat of all love am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>sthāna-traya-vyatītaḥ</td><td>beyond the three states (waking, dreaming, deep-sleep)</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-anugrāhakaḥ</td><td>the gracer of all</td></tr><tr><td>sat-cit-ānanda-pūrṇa-ātmā</td><td>the Self full of Being-Consciousness-Bliss</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-prema-āspadaḥ</td><td>the seat of all love</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 109","title":"","sa":["सच्चिदानन्दमात्रोऽहं स्वप्रकाशोऽस्मि चिद्घनः ।","सत्त्वस्वरूपसन्मात्रसिद्धसर्वात्मकोऽस्म्यहम् ॥ १०९ ॥"],"iast":["sac-cidānanda-mātro'haṃ sva-prakāśo'smi cid-ghanaḥ |","sattva-svarūpa-san-mātra-siddha-sarvātmako'smy aham || 109 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Nothing but Being-Consciousness-Bliss am I; self-luminous, a mass of Consciousness am I; accomplished-as-everything by being mere Existence-of-the-form-of-pure-Being am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>sat-cit-ānanda-mātraḥ</td><td>nothing-but Being-Consciousness-Bliss</td></tr><tr><td>sva-prakāśaḥ</td><td>self-luminous</td></tr><tr><td>cid-ghanaḥ</td><td>a mass of Consciousness</td></tr><tr><td>sattva-svarūpa-sat-mātra-siddha-sarvātmakaḥ</td><td>accomplished-as-everything by-being-mere-Existence-of-the-form-of-pure-being</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Verse 110","title":"","sa":["सर्वाधिष्ठानसन्मात्रः स्वात्मबन्धहरोऽस्म्यहम् ।","सर्वग्रासोऽस्म्यहं सर्वद्रष्टा सर्वानुभूरहम् ॥ ११० ॥"],"iast":["sarvādhiṣṭhāna-san-mātraḥ svātma-bandha-haro'smy aham |","sarva-grāso'smy ahaṃ sarva-draṣṭā sarvānubhūr aham || 110 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The mere-Being that is the substratum of all am I; the destroyer of self-bondage am I; the all-devourer am I; the all-seeing, the all-experiencing am I.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>predicate</td><td>gloss</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-adhiṣṭhāna-sat-mātraḥ</td><td>the mere-Being that is the substratum of all</td></tr><tr><td>sva-ātma-bandha-haraḥ</td><td>the destroyer of (one's own) self-bondage</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-grāsaḥ</td><td>the all-devourer</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-draṣṭā</td><td>the all-seeing</td></tr><tr><td>sarva-anubhūḥ</td><td>the all-experiencing</td></tr></table>"]},{"ref":"Composite commentary on vv. 91–110 (the via positiva and culmination)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Brahma-Yogin","the positive predicates <em>do not</em> re-introduce duality; they are <em>upalakṣaṇa-vākyāni</em> (indicative phrases) — each predicate, given to the Yogin to chant, is to be <em>consumed</em> by the <em>aham</em> into which it folds. The closing verses 105–110 systematically cite <em>Chāndogya</em> (<em>sve mahimni</em>), <em>Māṇḍūkya</em> (<em>prapañcopaśama</em>, <em>vaiśvānara</em>), <em>Śvetāśvatara</em> (<em>viśvataś-cakṣuḥ</em>), <em>Kena</em> (<em>cakṣuṣaś cakṣuḥ</em>), <em>Kaṭha</em>/<em>Muṇḍaka</em> (<em>jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ</em>), and <em>Bhagavad-Gītā</em> (<em>upadraṣṭā, uttama-pūruṣa, sarvendriya-guṇābhāsa</em>) — the Upaniṣad is <strong>assembling</strong> itself as a tissue of <em>mahā-vākya</em>-equivalents."],["Ayyangar","renders the whole as a continuous flowing passage; preserves the <em>aham</em> cadence at the head of each clause; closes with \"He who, with philosophic insight, knows thus, he is indeed said to be the Puruṣa — Thus the Upaniṣad\" (= the <em>iti-vākya</em> at the end of v. 110)."],["Deussen","<em>not in his recension</em>."],["Sivananda","recommends the <em>via positiva</em> (vv. 91–110) as the <em>evening sādhanā</em> and the <em>via negativa</em> (vv. 81–90) as the <em>morning</em> — the rhythm trains <em>neti</em> in the freshness of dawn and <em>iti</em> in the depth of dusk."],["Krishnananda","the structural logic — <em>negative</em> exhausts the <em>not-Self</em>; <em>positive</em> names what the Self <em>is</em> without inserting it back into the predicate-net; <em>dvaita-advaita-vilakṣaṇa</em> (v. 101) is the meta-predicate that says \"I do not even have to be 'non-dual' to be myself.\" The text ends as it must — with <em>no</em> predicate, only <em>iti</em>."]],"notes":[],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Concluding declaration (after v. 110)","title":"","sa":["एवं यो वेद तत्त्वेन स वै पुरुष उच्यत इत्युपनिषत् ॥"],"iast":["evaṃ yo veda tattvena sa vai puruṣa ucyata ity upaniṣat ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Whoever knows thus, in truth — he indeed is called the <em>Puruṣa</em>. Thus the Upaniṣad.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This is the <strong>iti-vākya</strong> — the standard closing formula. <em>Puruṣa</em> here carries the full force of vv. 99 and 102: <em>Puruṣa-Paramātman, Uttama-Pūruṣa, Bhūta-Pāla</em>."]},{"ref":"Closing śānti-pāṭha (repeated)","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्विनावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥","इति ब्रह्मविद्योपनिषत्समाप्ता ॥"],"iast":["Oṃ saha nāvavatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai | tejasvi nāv adhītam astu | mā vidviṣāvahai || Oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ || iti brahma-vidyopaniṣat-samāptā ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"… Thus is the <em>Brahma-Vidyā Upaniṣad</em> concluded.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Visual Representations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Upaniṣad's doctrine is geometric — Praṇava as four-mātrā architecture, body as nāḍī-cakra-granthi grid, breath as cyclic count. The diagrams below render those structures."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 1","title":"Praṇava: four mātrās, four correspondences (vv. 3–9)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                  ╱ Ardha-mātrā (v. 9) ╲\n                 ╱   the flame-tip       ╲      → above all matrās\n                ╱   parā / parātparam     ╲     → laya = paraṃ brahma (vv. 12–13)\n               ▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔▔\n                          M\n            ╔═══════════════════════════════════╗\n            ║ Sāma-veda │ Dyaus │ Āhavanīya     ║   Mātrā 3 (v. 6)\n            ║ Īśvara    │ Fire (smokeless,      ║   = laya phase\n            ║           │  like lightning, v.8) ║\n            ╠═══════════════════════════════════╣\n                          U\n            ╔═══════════════════════════════════╗\n            ║ Yajur-veda │ Antarikṣa │ Dakṣiṇa  ║   Mātrā 2 (v. 5)\n            ║ Viṣṇu      │ Moon-disc (v. 7)     ║   = sthiti phase\n            ╠═══════════════════════════════════╣\n                          A\n            ╔═══════════════════════════════════╗\n            ║ Ṛg-veda │ Pṛthivī │ Gārhapatya    ║   Mātrā 1 (v. 4)\n            ║ Brahmā  │ Sun-orb in śaṅkha       ║   = udaya phase\n            ║         │ (v. 7)                  ║\n            ╚═══════════════════════════════════╝\n\n                ↓ chant proceeds upward through A→U→M→ardha → laya ↓\n                  (per v. 12: like a bronze bell decaying into silence)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 2","title":"The five ātma-sthānas (vv. 41–43)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                   ┌──── DEHĀTĪTA — Brahman (v. 43)\n                   │     12 aṅgula beyond nose-tip\n                   │     &quot;paratvāt paraṃ nāsti&quot;\n                   ▼\n                   ●                                  Nose-tip\n                  / \\      ← Acyuta / Sadāśiva       (v. 42)\n                 /   \\        nāsāgra-dṛṣṭi\n            ◆──/─────\\──◆                            Forehead\n           /    \\   /    \\  ← Maheśvara              (lalāṭa)\n          /      \\ /      \\\n         /        ●        \\                         Bhrū-madhya\n        /        / \\        \\  ← jyotir-liṅga         (v. 80)\n       /        /   \\        \\    Rudra-granthi\n      /        /     \\        \\   pierced by\n     /        ◆ tālu  ◆        \\  akṣara-vāyu\n    /          ↑                \\  (v. 71)\n   /     Rudra in middle of      \\\n  /         palate (v. 41)        \\\n ◆──────────────●─────────────────◆                  Throat (kaṇṭha)\n                ↑                                    Jālandhara-bandha\n        Viṣṇu samāśritaḥ                             constricts here\n        (v. 41); Viṣṇu-granthi                       (v. 72)\n        in heart (v. 70)\n                ●                                    Heart (hṛdaya)\n                ↑\n        Brahmā&#x27;s seat (v. 41)\n        Daharākāśa\n        Anāhata-nāda heard (v. 20)\n                ●\n                ↑\n        Nābhi-kanda (vv. 15, 22):\n        Sun-bright &quot;All&quot;, lamp-Mahādeva,\n        meeting-point of 72,000 nāḍīs (v. 11)\n                ●\n                ↑\n        Mūlādhāra: Brahma-granthi (v. 70),\n        Adho-liṅga (v. 80), seat of fire\n        in &#x27;A&#x27; (v. 69)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 3","title":"The three granthis and three liṅgas","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                       ╔══════════════════════════════════╗\n                       ║   M     bhrū-madhya              ║\n       ┌── Jyotir-liṅga│   │     RUDRA-granthi (v. 71)    ║\n       │   (v. 80)     │   │     ↑ pierced by akṣara-vāyu ║\n       │               ║   │     ↑                        ║\n       │   Daharākāśa  ║   │     ↑                        ║\n       │               ║   U     hṛdaya                   ║\n       ├── Ātma-liṅga  ║   │     VIṢṆU-granthi (v. 70)    ║\n       │   in Śikhinī  ║   │     ↑                        ║\n       │   (v. 80)     ║   │     ↑                        ║\n       │               ║   A     mūlādhāra                ║\n       └── Adho-liṅga  ║   │     BRAHMA-granthi (v. 70)   ║\n           (v. 80)     ║         ↑ Suṣumnā ascends        ║\n                       ╚══════════════════════════════════╝\n\n   Each granthi is the boundary at which a phoneme of OM &quot;locks&quot;\n   the prāṇa into an identification (avidyā → kāma → karma).\n   The ascending akṣara-vāyu (Praṇava-driven prāṇa) pierces all three.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 4","title":"The Ajapā-gāyatrī cycle of 21,600 (vv. 16, 78–79)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                    ┌────────── EXHALATION (recaka) ──────────┐\n                    │                                          │\n                    │              &quot;HAṂ&quot;                       ▼\n                    │                                          │\n                  (15× per minute)                       (1× = ~4 sec)\n                    │                                          │\n                    ▲                                          │\n                    │              &quot;SAḤ&quot;                       │\n                    │                                          │\n                    └────────── INHALATION (pūraka) ───────────┘\n\n                    ↳ heard &quot;from outside&quot;: ha—sa—ha—sa…  =  HAṂSAḤ (&quot;the bird&quot;)\n                    ↳ heard &quot;from inside&quot; : sa—ha—sa—ha…  =  SO&#x27;HAM   (&quot;That am I&quot;)\n\n                    The metathesis is the verse&#x27;s whole teaching:\n                    same breath, two grammars, identical content.\n\n                    Daily count: 15 × 60 × 24 = 21,600 (v. 79)\n\n                    The &quot;japa&quot; is therefore already happening; the practice\n                    is only to REMEMBER that it is happening.\n                    Hence: Ajapā = &quot;the un-chanted (i.e., already-chanting)&quot; (v. 78).</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 5","title":"The five vāyus + five upa-vāyus (vv. 66–68)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   Power-axis        Pentad-1 (Prāṇa proper)         Pentad-2 (Upa-prāṇa)\n                     ─────────────────────             ──────────────────\n   KRIYĀ-ŚAKTI   →   Prāṇa    (in-breath)             Nāga      (eructation)\n                     Apāna    (eliminative)           Kūrma     (blinking)\n                     Samāna   (digestion-fire)        Kṛkara    (sneezing)\n                     Udāna    (upward, speech)        Devadatta (yawning)\n                     Vyāna    (pervasive)             Dhanañjaya (post-mortem\n                                                                  swelling)\n                     paired with                       paired with\n                     5 KARMENDRIYAS                    5 JÑĀNENDRIYAS\n                     (mouth-hands-feet-anus-          (ear-skin-eye-tongue-\n                       genitals)                       nose)\n\n                  ── Both pentads are HAṂSA in disguise ──\n                  Haṃsa-fire stands between the two śaktis,\n                  in the nābhi-cakra where the Sun abides (v. 68).</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 6","title":"The structural arc of the 110 verses","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>   vv. 1–3      INVOCATION  ◆ &quot;OM is Brahman; here is its anatomy&quot;\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 4–9      PRAṆAVA     ◆ A / U / M / ardha-mātrā unpacked\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 10–13    SUṢUMNĀ &amp;   ◆ 72,000 nāḍīs pierced; bell-laya\n                LAYA          → silence = Brahman\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 14–21    HAṂSA       ◆ jīva → Sa/Ha → niṣkala → anāhata\n                INTRODUCED    → &quot;He is sung as the Haṃsa&quot;\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 22–26    HAṂSA-YOGA  ◆ kumbhaka + amṛta-svāda + japa\n                PRACTISED    → &quot;no jarā, no maraṇa, no roga&quot;\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 27–35    GURU &amp;      ◆ Guru = Hari; renounce body/varna;\n                ŚRUTI         &quot;tile in oil, fragrance in flower&quot;\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 36–43    SAKALA /    ◆ &quot;torch and treasure&quot;; five ātma-sthāna;\n                NIṢKALA       dehātīta at 12 aṅgula\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 44–48    SECRECY     ◆ adhikāra-rule for transmission\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 49–53    THREE       ◆ jñānī independent of āśrama;\n                GURUS         codaka → bodhaka → mokṣa-da\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 54–57    INNER       ◆ &quot;eclipse of the sun&quot;; sāyujya\n                YAJÑA         = water-in-water\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 58–80    FULL        ◆ 5+5 vāyus, 3 granthis, Kuṇḍalinī,\n                HAṂSA-YOGA    Khecarī, vajra-kumbhakas, Ajapā,\n                              3 liṅgas (Adho/Ātma/Jyotir)\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   vv. 81–110   AHAM-       ◆ via negativa (81–90) → via positiva\n                GRAHA         (91–110) → &quot;sa vai puruṣa ucyate&quot;\n   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical Steps and Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Distilled from the verses. Each step cites the verse(s) it rests on. Where the Upaniṣad gives <em>quantitative</em> prescriptions, they are preserved exactly."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"A. Prerequisites (adhikāra)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>#</td><td>Step</td><td>Verse</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Find a Guru who can transmit the Haṃsa-vidyā from his own mouth (<em>guru-vaktrāt</em>) — not via book alone.</td><td>26, 34</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Develop <em>guru-bhakti</em> and <em>santoṣa-saṃyukta dāsya</em> (serene service) with <em>parā prajñā</em> (alongside wisdom).</td><td>27–28, 30</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Be a <em>putra-tulya</em> (son-like) and <em>bhakta</em> — the verse states the Vidyā is not given to the un-devoted.</td><td>47</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Recognise that <em>āśrama</em> (gṛhastha, brahmacārī, vānaprastha, bhikṣuka) does <em>not</em> qualify or disqualify you.</td><td>49</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Hold the Vedas as authoritative (<em>pramāṇa-vyavasthā</em>); abandon nothing without sanction.</td><td>32</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"B. Daily sādhanā outline (the Yogin's day)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>#</td><td>Practice</td><td>Duration / Mark</td><td>Verse</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Āyāma</strong> (prolongation of recaka–pūraka–kumbhaka)</td><td>one <em>Yāma</em> (≈ 3 hours/day) — Brahma-Yogin's gloss</td><td>55</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Praṇava + Haṃsa</strong> utterance (both, in sequence)</td><td>21 long Oṃ-s + matching Haṃsa-japa, AM &amp; PM</td><td>56, 79</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Namaskāra → Yoga → Mudrā → Arcanā</strong></td><td>sequence at each session-opening</td><td>56</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Jñāna-mudrā</strong> assumed throughout</td><td>Dakṣiṇāmurti-form held physically</td><td>64</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Samādhi</strong> on the crystal-clear divine form, <em>anusmaran haṃsa-mantram</em></td><td>continuous through session</td><td>65</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Ajapā-japa</strong>: simply watch the natural <em>Haṃ-Saḥ</em> of breath</td><td>all 21,600 daily breaths (continuous attention, not count)</td><td>16, 78–79</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"C. The Praṇava-laya practice (vv. 8–13)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre> 1.  Sit. Chant one long Oṃ with attention placed on:\n        A — sun-disc in middle of forehead (śaṅkha-madhya)\n        U — moon-bindu inside that sun-disc\n        M — smokeless fire / lightning above the brow\n 2.  Hold the ardha-mātrā nasal resonance after M as long as the breath\n     naturally sustains it — without forcing.\n 3.  When the resonance fades, do not &quot;cut&quot; it; let attention follow\n     the fading sound into its silence (the &quot;bell-decay&quot;, v. 12).\n 4.  Rest in the silence — that silence IS the paraṃ brahma (v. 13).\n 5.  Repeat. The intellect (dhī) that follows the sound into silence\n     is itself &quot;dissolved&quot; — and is fit for amṛta.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"D. The Haṃsa-yoga practice (vv. 21–25, 65–80)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre> 1.  Posture: Siddhāsana (Brahma-Yogin&#x27;s gloss on v. 72).\n 2.  Bandha-trayam:\n       (a) Jālandhara-bandha     — constrict throat            (v. 72)\n       (b) Mūla-bandha           — arrest prāṇa at nāḍī-mūla   (v. 72)\n       (c) Khecarī (tongue-press) — rasanā pīḍyamānā            (v. 73)\n 3.  Drṣṭi: nāsāgra (eyes at nose-tip)                          (v. 69)\n 4.  Drop recaka and pūraka; rest in kumbhaka                   (v. 21)\n 5.  Equalise prāṇa-apāna at the nābhi-kanda                    (v. 22)\n 6.  Drink (by bhāvanā) the amṛta dripping from the head\n     and &quot;anoint&quot; the lamp-Mahādeva blazing in the navel\n     while chanting Haṃsa Haṃsa                                 (vv. 22–23)\n 7.  Fire-bhāvanā successively at:\n       A — Mūlādhāra\n       U — heart                                                (v. 69–70)\n       M — bhrū-madhya\n     Pierce the three granthis with the akṣara-vāyu:\n       Brahma-granthi  (A — mūlādhāra)\n       Viṣṇu-granthi   (U — heart)                              (v. 70–71)\n       Rudra-granthi   (M — bhrū-madhya)\n 8.  Tongue-press; the ṣoḍaśī (16-mātra Praṇava) ascends through:\n       Tri-kūṭa  → Golākha  → Nikhara  → Tri-śaṅkha-vajra-Oṃkāra\n                                                                (vv. 73–74)\n 9.  Move Kuṇḍalinī upward; pierce the śaśi-maṇḍala (soma-cakra);\n     perform vajra-kumbhakas (Sūryojjayi, Śītalī, ...);\n     bind the nine gates                                        (vv. 74–75)\n10.  Nāda manifests at brahma-sthāna; Śaṅkhinī showers amṛta;\n     the six cakras open UPWARD; manifest the jñāna-dīpa        (v. 76)\n11.  Worship the deity who is the inner Self of all,\n     of-the-form-of-knowledge, free of taint                    (v. 77)\n12.  Three-liṅga meditation:\n       Adho-liṅga (Mūlādhāra)\n       Ātma-liṅga (in Śikhinī, end of Suṣumnā)\n       Jyotir-liṅga (bhrū-madhya)                               (v. 80)\n13.  Final attitude: &quot;I am He&quot; (so&#x27;ham) — repeat through every breath.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"E. The Aham-graha-upāsanā (vv. 81–110)","title":"the closing meditation","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre> 1.  After Haṃsa-yoga, while still in āsana:\n 2.  Recite (audibly or mentally) the predicate-string of vv. 81–110.\n     ┌─ MORNING:  vv. 81–90  (via negativa: &quot;a-/an-/nir-...&quot;)\n     │            The Yogin negates body-mind-element-sense-guṇa\n     │            in eight axes (see Brahma-Yogin&#x27;s count).\n     └─ EVENING:  vv. 91–110 (via positiva: &quot;ā-/ī-/u-/sa-...&quot;)\n                  The Yogin names the Self in positive śruti-citations\n                  (Chā 7.24, Māṇḍūkya 7, Kena 1.2, Śv 3.3, BG 13.13, etc.)\n 3.  When the entire string has been recited, DROP all predicates\n     (per v. 36: torch is released once treasure is found).\n 4.  Rest in the bare ahaṃ-pratyaya — predicate-less awareness.\n 5.  Seal with: &quot;evaṃ yo veda tattvena sa vai puruṣa ucyate.&quot;</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"F. Diagnostic markers (the experiential signatures)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Stage</td><td>Verse</td><td>Mark</td></tr><tr><td>Praṇava-laya beginning</td><td>12</td><td>The chanted Oṃ's decay starts to \"carry\" attention forward instead of having to be \"held\".</td></tr><tr><td>Bell-silence stabilised</td><td>13</td><td>The <em>dhī</em> spontaneously dissolves; brief loss of subject/object.</td></tr><tr><td>Nābhi-kanda lit</td><td>15, 23</td><td>Steady warmth at navel; \"lamp-Mahādeva\" not visualised but felt.</td></tr><tr><td>Anāhata-nāda heard</td><td>20</td><td>A subtle bell or conch-tone audible in the right ear or in the brow-centre.</td></tr><tr><td>Kṣīra-daṇḍa strike</td><td>18</td><td>A sharp white \"click\" at mūlādhāra; Suṣumnā opens.</td></tr><tr><td>Khecarī-amṛta tasting</td><td>22, 76</td><td>Cool, sweet liquid at palate with no salivary source.</td></tr><tr><td>Granthi-bheda</td><td>70–71</td><td>Successive releases at mūlādhāra, heart, brow — each accompanied by a flash of light or sudden ease.</td></tr><tr><td>Six cakras opening upward</td><td>76</td><td>The body's downward-pulling sense of gravity reverses; an upward \"wakefulness\" replaces it.</td></tr><tr><td>21,600-fold ajapā recognised</td><td>79</td><td>Breath, formerly heard as breath, is now heard as \"haṃ-saḥ\"; the chant is independent of will.</td></tr><tr><td>\"I am He\" stabilised</td><td>80</td><td>The body-identification becomes a <em>passing thought</em>, not a <em>standing condition</em>.</td></tr><tr><td>Predicate-drop (aham-graha)</td><td>110 + iti-vākya</td><td>No further self-description seems necessary or sufficient; <em>iti</em> itself becomes the closure.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"G. The negative injunction (what NOT to do)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>#</td><td>Don't</td><td>Verse</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Don't impart the Vidyā to the unworthy (<em>a-putra</em>, <em>a-śiṣya</em>). The bestower <em>goes to hell and is never accomplished.</em></td><td>47–48</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Don't act in contradiction to the Veda; without Vedic sanction, action <em>contributes to ruin</em>.</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Don't cling to the <em>jñāna</em> after the <em>jñeya</em> is reached — <em>let the torch go.</em></td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Don't settle for the <em>ardha-mātrā</em> — there is <em>parātparam</em> above even that.</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Don't confuse the <em>upāya</em> (sakala — has steps) with the <em>upeya</em> (niṣkala — has none).</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Don't aim primarily at the <em>siddhi-s</em>. They appear <em>en route</em>; Īśvaratva is the proper aim.</td><td>24–25</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Don't substitute book-religion for <em>guru-vaktra</em> transmission.</td><td>26, 34</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"H. Fruits (phala-pratijñā)","title":"what the Upaniṣad explicitly promises","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Fruit</td><td>Verse</td></tr><tr><td>Release from <em>bhava-bandhana</em> (the bondage of becoming)</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Freedom from <em>jarā, maraṇa, roga</em> in this world</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Attainment of the eight siddhis beginning with <em>aṇimā</em></td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Attainment of <em>Īśvaratva</em></td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Going to the eternal, undecaying abode (<em>śāśvataṃ padam avyayam</em>)</td><td>54</td></tr><tr><td><em>Sāyujya</em> — water-in-water union with Brahman</td><td>57</td></tr><tr><td><em>Sarva-duḥkha-bahiṣkṛti</em> — being cast out of all suffering</td><td>58</td></tr><tr><td>Not smeared by either <em>puṇya</em> (e.g., <em>aśvamedha</em>) or <em>pāpa</em> (e.g., <em>brahma-hatyā</em>)</td><td>50–51</td></tr><tr><td>Recognition as <em>Puruṣa</em> (= <em>Uttama-Pūruṣa</em> of BG 15) — final</td><td>110 + iti</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Primary text","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Sanskritdocuments.org</strong> (Sunder Hattangadi, ed.) — <em>Brahmavidyopaniṣat</em> (Devanāgarī, ITRANS), file `brahmavidya.itx`, 110 verses, Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda recension, last update 16 Feb 2000. URL: `https://sanskritdocuments.org/doc_upanishhat/brahmavidya.html`. The Sanskrit text used as the basis of every verse here."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Translations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>T.R. Śrīnivāsa Ayyaṅgār &amp; Paṇḍit S. Subrahmaṇya Śāstrī</strong> (trans./ed.), <em>The Yoga Upaniṣads — Translated into English on the Basis of the Commentary of Śrī Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</em>, Adyar Library, Madras, 1938 (1st ed.); revised by G. Śrīnivāsa Mūrti, 1952. <em>Brahma-Vidyopaniṣad</em> on pp. 197–216 (Ayyangar verse-groups 1–111). Internet Archive: `archive.org/details/TheYogaUpanishads`. (Working English; cited throughout.)","- <strong>Paul Deussen</strong>, <em>Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda</em>, F.A. Brockhaus, Leipzig, 1897. <em>Brahma-Vidyā</em> (14-verse Atharvaveda recension), §§ 1–14. English trans. Bedekar &amp; Palsule, <em>Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda</em>, Motilal Banarsidass, 1980. (Covers only vv. 2–13 of our text.)","- <strong>Hattangadi, S.</strong> (ed.), Sanskrit text <em>with</em> English working translation, derivative from Ayyangar. (Available at sanskritdocuments.org alongside the ITRANS file.)","- <strong>Classic Yoga</strong> (online, K. Suresh, 2018), English working translation of all 110 verses. URL: `classicyoga.co.in/2018/04/brahma-vidya-upanishad/`. (Crosschecked.)","- <strong>Celextel Library</strong>, abbreviated English (14-verse text only). URL: `celextel.org/upanishads-krishna-yajur-veda/brahma-vidya-upanishad/`."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Commentaries","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin","(Rāmacandrendra Sarasvatī, Kāñcī, early 18th c.), <em>Brahma-Vidyopaniṣad-bhāṣya</em>, in <em>108 Upaniṣads with Sanskrit Commentary of Upaniṣad-Brahma-Yogin</em>, Adyar Library, 8 vols. The full text accessed via Ayyangar's bracketed embeddings in the 1938 translation."]],"notes":["- <strong>Swami Sivananda</strong>, <em>Mind: Its Mysteries and Control</em> (1937), <em>Practical Lessons in Yoga</em> (1938), <em>Japa Yoga</em> (1947); Divine Life Society, Rishikesh. <em>Ajapā-gāyatrī</em> and <em>Haṃsa-vidyā</em> chapters consulted for thematic gloss on vv. 16, 19–25, 65–80.","- <strong>Swami Krishnananda</strong>, <em>The Mandukya Upanishad</em> lecture-series; <em>Studies in the Upanishads</em>; <em>The Philosophy of Life</em>. DLS, Rishikesh. (`swami-krishnananda.org`). Consulted for thematic Advaita gloss on vv. 1–13, 38–43, 81–110."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Scholarly context","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- <strong>Gavin Flood</strong>, <em>An Introduction to Hinduism</em>, Cambridge UP, 1996, pp. 96–97 (dating of Yoga-Upaniṣads). — places Brahmavidyā c. 100 BCE – 300 CE.","- <strong>Mircea Eliade</strong>, <em>Yoga: Immortality and Freedom</em>, Princeton UP, 1958 (1st French ed. 1954). — places Brahmavidyā post-Maitrāyaṇī, contemporary with early didactic <em>Mahābhārata</em>.","- <strong>K. Narayanaswami Aiyar</strong>, <em>Thirty Minor Upanishads</em>, Madras, 1914. (Does <em>not</em> cover Brahmavidyā; included for reference on adjacent Yoga-Upaniṣads.)","- <strong>Wikipedia</strong>, <em>Brahmavidya Upanishad</em>, accessed 2026-05-23. URL: `en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brahmavidya_Upanishad`. (Summary article; refs Flood, Eliade, Deussen, Ayyangar.)"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references within the Upaniṣadic corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Brahma-Vidyā Upaniṣad's intertextual fabric — particularly in §XIII (vv. 81–110) — is dense. The verses-and-citations below are explicitly identified by Brahma-Yogin (and, in modern scholarship, by Ayyangar/Krishnananda):","<table><tr><td>Verse</td><td>Citation</td><td>Source</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><em>oṃ ity etad akṣaram idaṃ sarvam</em></td><td>Māṇḍūkya 1</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><em>amātra</em></td><td>Māṇḍūkya 12</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>lotus-fibre simile</td><td>Chāndogya 8.6.1</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><em>vālāgra-śata-bhāga</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 5.9</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td><em>ātmānam ātmanā</em></td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 6.20</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td><em>parīkṣya lokān karma-citān</em></td><td>Muṇḍaka 1.2.12</td></tr><tr><td>35</td><td>oil-in-sesame</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.6.3</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td><em>jñānāgni-dagdha-karma</em> (na lipyate)</td><td>Īśā 2; Bhagavad-Gītā 4.37</td></tr><tr><td>67</td><td><em>upa-prāṇa</em> schema</td><td>Yoga-Sūtra II.50–53 bhāṣya</td></tr><tr><td>79</td><td>21,600 Ajapā-gāyatrī</td><td><em>Vijñāna-Bhairava-Tantra</em> 24, also <em>Haṃsa-Upaniṣad</em></td></tr><tr><td>94</td><td><em>cakṣuṣaś cakṣuḥ</em></td><td>Kena 1.2</td></tr><tr><td>95</td><td><em>jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ</em></td><td>Muṇḍaka 2.2.10</td></tr><tr><td>99</td><td><em>prapañcopaśama</em></td><td>Māṇḍūkya 7</td></tr><tr><td>103</td><td><em>vareṇyam</em></td><td>Ṛgveda 3.62.10 (Gāyatrī)</td></tr><tr><td>103</td><td><em>vaiśvānara</em></td><td>Māṇḍūkya 3</td></tr><tr><td>103</td><td><em>viśvataś-cakṣuḥ</em></td><td>Śvetāśvatara 3.3</td></tr><tr><td>105</td><td><em>sve mahimni sthitaḥ</em></td><td>Chāndogya 7.24.1 (<em>bhūman</em>-vidyā)</td></tr><tr><td>107</td><td><em>sarvendriya-guṇābhāsam</em></td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 13.13</td></tr></table>","The Upaniṣad is, in this sense, a <strong>knot of mahā-vākyas</strong> — assembled into a continuous <em>aham-graha-japa</em> whose every predicate is already vouched-for by a prior śruti.","<em>Document compiled 2026-05-23. Sanskrit collated against `brahmavidya.itx` (Hattangadi/sanskritdocuments.org). English translations, where not original, follow Ayyangar (1938) closely. Pada-by-pada glosses, grammatical analysis, and commentary digests by the present compiler.</em>","<em>Format mirrors the</em> Amṛta-bindu Upaniṣad <em>document in the same series</em> (`Amrita_Bindu_Upanishad.md`)<em>.</em>"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
